{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md docs/design/dao.stream.middleware.md docs/design/shibi.md'","aggregated_output":"# DaoStream Remote: Any Stream, Reachable As Itself\n\nStatus: design target, not implemented. Subordinate to\n[`dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md), which is the contract and wins on any\ndisagreement. Every sentence below is a rule. It replaces the earlier serve\ndraft (git history, `docs/design/dao.stream.serve.md` at `71f3fb93`) and\nsucceeds `dao.jing.remote`, which is deprecated whole once this is\nimplemented (section 8).\n\n## 1. Invariant, impossibilities, non-goals\n\nThe governing requirement, in the owner's words:\n\n> any implementation of dao.stream can be mechanically exposed via websocket\n> or udp and communicate p2p. client-server is just one stigmergic behavior\n> that interpreters implement. It should be able to traverse a NAT. A toy\n> example would be a string with a dao.stream wrapper that can be exposed on\n> a websocket on a remote interpreter.\n\n> There should be no concept of a server or client. its P2P but client/server\n> model can be implemented by convention. there is no priviledge server or\n> client.\n\n`dao.stream` is an abstraction boundary, not a network boundary. This\ndocument makes it a network boundary too, and keeps every network concept on\nits own side: nothing here adds an operation, an outcome or a key to the\ncontract beyond the three accepted decisions listed in section 10.\n\n**Impossibilities, stated plainly.**\n\n- Two peers that are both unreachable (symmetric NAT or carrier-grade NAT on\n  both sides, or two browsers) cannot exchange a byte directly. A third peer\n  with a reachable address is required. That peer runs the code in this\n  document and nothing else; it holds no protocol role and any reachable\n  peer can take its place (section 4).\n- A browser cannot listen. It dials, or it is reached through a third peer.\n- Everything is plaintext and unauthenticated unless middleware is composed\n  (section 7). A relay peer sees every message it carries.\n- UDP messages are bounded by a composed maximum (section 3.2).\n- A reclaimed lease means the resource is gone, not disconnected (section 6).\n- A false lapse under partition is possible (`dao.lease.md`, Limits).\n\n**Non-goals.** Authentication, authorization and key distribution (section 7\nnames the seam; `shibi.md` owns the rest); WebRTC data channels; browser\ninbound; readiness notification; exactly-once delivery.\n\n## 2. The design: mirror and reflection\n\nOne sentence: a peer that holds a handle answers the handle's own operations\nas data over a channel; a peer at the other end holds a handle whose\noperations are those questions asked across the channel, with the source's\noutcome maps returned verbatim.\n\nFour concepts.\n\n+-------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Concept     | Definition                                                                                                     |\n+=============+================================================================================================================+\n| channel     | Two ordinary handles with exactly two ends: a writer whose `append!` carries one value toward the other end,    |\n|             | and a reader positioned on the values the other end sent. A WebSocket attachment plus its deposit medium is a  |\n|             | channel (`dao.stream.ws.md`); a UDP socket adapter plus its deposit medium is a channel (3.2); two ring buffers |\n|             | in one process are a channel; two reflections served by a third peer are a channel (3.3).                      |\n+-------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| table       | Composition data on a peer: `{identity {:handle h :surface S}}`, plus the optional keys of sections 6 and 7.    |\n|             | It is a plain map the composition owns, not a registry (`dao.stream.md`, No hidden global state).              |\n+-------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| mirror step | The pure step a peer runs over a channel to answer requests against its table. It holds no state between       |\n|             | calls beyond the channel reader's cursor.                                                                      |\n+-------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| reflection  | The handle `attach!` returns for a remote descriptor. A **link**, one per channel, holds what the reflections   |\n|             | on that channel share.                                                                                         |\n+-------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n\nEvery peer may run any number of mirror steps and hold any number of\nreflections, on the same channel, in both directions. No request names a\nkind of peer; the protocol has no peer identity at all. A peer is a channel\nend.\n\n### 2.1 Wire shapes\n\nTwo logical shapes cross every channel. Keys the contract owns stay under\n`:dao.stream/`; keys this document owns are under `:dao.stream.remote/`,\nthe transport-owned namespace rule of `dao.stream.md` (Envelopes).\n\n```clojure\n;; request\n{:dao.stream/identity        id\n :dao.stream.remote/op       op        ; :dao.stream/descriptor | :dao.stream/cursor\n                                       ; | :dao.stream/next | :dao.stream/append!\n :dao.stream.remote/args     [...]     ; []  | [anchor] | [cursor] | [value]\n :dao.stream.remote/id       n         ; asker-minted, unique per channel reader the asker holds\n :dao.stream.remote/budget   k}        ; optional, next only, a positive integer\n\n;; answer: the contract's outcome map for the requested operation, verbatim, plus\n{:dao.stream.remote/id       n\n :dao.stream/identity        id\n :dao.stream.remote/oldest   <cursor>  ; the source's current :oldest, when the identity is served\n :dao.stream.remote/newest   <cursor>  ; the source's current :newest, likewise\n :dao.stream.remote/more     [...]}    ; optional, next only: further outcome maps in order\n```\n\nA request is one value on the channel writer; an answer is one value on the\nchannel writer of the other end. The request map is open: keys not named\nhere pass to the mirror step's middleware chain (section 7) and are\notherwise ignored. A value that is neither a well-formed request nor a\nwell-formed answer is dropped as a diagnostic below the mirror step and the\nlink, as malformed wire input (`datom.world.md`, Host Boundaries).\n\nWire ops are exactly four. `close!` never crosses: the mirror holds nothing\nper remote party to close, and the owner's close is never remote. `attach!`\nnever crosses: it consumes a descriptor and produces a local handle.\n\n### 2.2 The remote descriptor\n\n```clojure\n{:dao.stream/type      :dao.stream/remote\n :dao.stream/identity  id                      ; the served logical stream\n :dao.stream/channel   <channel descriptor>}   ; section 3: ws, udp or pair\n```\n\nThe descriptor names a stream and a channel. It carries no authorization, no\nsurface and no anchors; the mirror answers those. Two remote descriptors\nthat name one identity through different channels reach one stream, as the\ncontract's Envelopes section requires of descriptors for one logical stream\nserved by one transport (OD-3 (a), accepted). Attaching through this\ntransport never creates: a dynamic dispatch table has an\n`:dao.stream/attach` entry for `:dao.stream/remote` and no\n`:dao.stream/create` entry.\n\n### 2.3 The mirror step\n\n`(mirror-step table chan-reader cursor chan-writer) -> cursor'`. For each\nrequest read from `chan-reader`, in order, bounded by a composition budget:\n\n1. Look up `:dao.stream/identity` in the table. Absent: append\n   `{:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/not-found}` with the request's id and\n   identity; an identity this peer does not serve has no operations, so\n   `not-found` is the honest answer for all four. Present, but\n   `:dao.stream.remote/op` outside the entry's declared surface: append\n   `not-found` with `:dao.stream.remote/reason :dao.stream.remote/no-surface`,\n   because the contract forbids answering a nonexistent operation with an\n   outcome of that operation (Surfaces).\n2. Otherwise answer `:dao.stream/descriptor` from the entry's handle\n   directly, and apply `:dao.stream/cursor`, `:dao.stream/next` and\n   `:dao.stream/append!` to it through `dao.stream.middleware/apply-request`\n   (`dao.stream.middleware.md`), with context\n   `{:dao.stream.remote/channel <channel attachment identity>}`.\n3. Append the outcome map with `:dao.stream.remote/id`,\n   `:dao.stream/identity`, and the source's current `:oldest` and `:newest`\n   cursors obtained by `cursor` on the entry's handle. Where `cursor` does\n   not answer `ok` for an anchor, that piggyback key is omitted.\n\nFor `:dao.stream/next` with a budget `k` the mirror may follow the successor\ncursor of each `ok` outcome up to `k - 1` further times and place the\nfurther outcome maps, in order, under `:dao.stream.remote/more`. The first\noutcome is the answer map itself. The vector ends at and includes the first\nnon-`ok` outcome when one occurs, so `blocked`, `end` and `gap` reach the\nreader with the source's own recovery cursor. A mirror that ignores the\nbudget is correct.\n\nA value the channel cannot carry (`invalid-value` from the channel writer)\nis answered as `{:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/transport-error\n:dao.stream.remote/reason :dao.stream.remote/oversize}` in place of that\nelement; the read is not skipped and no cursor advances past it. Large\nmedia travel as `dao.jing` addresses (`dao.stream.md`, Granularity).\n\nThe mirror never constructs, parses or rewrites a cursor, an anchor or an\noutcome. The served stream is the original: its cursors, its positions, its\n`gap`, its `end`. This is the whole basis of the claim in the title, and it\nrests on cursors being plain data that survive the codec (OD-3 decision 2,\naccepted). A handle whose cursors hold a host object cannot be served, and\na composition refuses to enter it into a table.\n\n### 2.4 The reflection\n\n`attach!` on a remote descriptor returns `:dao.stream/ok` at once with a\nreflection handle and a local, opaque `:dao.stream/attachment`. This is the\ncontract's deferred remote confirmation (`dao.stream.md`, attach outcomes).\nThe host-composed attach closure keeps one link per channel descriptor,\nshared by every reflection through that channel. A link holds: the channel\nwriter; the channel reader and the link's own cursor on it; the set of\noutstanding request ids with the request each was sent for; filed answers\nkeyed by id; the latest piggybacked anchors per identity; an optional event\nwriter; and the policy datum `:dao.stream.remote/resend-after`.\n\nOn attach the link sends one `descriptor` request for the identity. Its\nanswer is the reflection's confirmation. `not-found` marks the reflection\n**gone**; `ok` records the source's descriptor and anchors. Either is\nappended to the event writer when one is composed.\n\n**Drain.** Every operation on any reflection first reads the link's channel\nreader to `blocked`, filing each answer whose id is outstanding, installing\neach `:dao.stream.remote/more` outcome at the cursor that precedes it, and\nrecording piggybacked anchors. Answers whose id is not outstanding are\ndropped. Non-answer values are dropped as diagnostics. This is where the\nasynchrony goes (`dao.stream.md`, Where the asynchrony goes): the caller's\nown polling is the cadence, and no driver step exists.\n\nThen the operation answers as follows.\n\n+---------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Operation           | Reflection behaviour after the drain                                                                        |\n+=====================+=============================================================================================================+\n| `descriptor`        | Local. `ok` with the remote descriptor it was attached with and `:dao.stream/identity`. Same identity as   |\n|                     | the source's own descriptor, different reachability.                                                       |\n+---------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `cursor` `:oldest`  | `ok` with the latest piggybacked cursor for that anchor. Before any answer has arrived, `transport-error`   |\n| or `:newest`        | with `:dao.stream/retry? true`. A stale `:newest` is earlier than a fresh one, so a composition that mints  |\n|                     | before an operation still observes everything the operation causes; a stale `:oldest` may yield `gap`,     |\n|                     | which the contract already permits of a fresh `:oldest`.                                                   |\n+---------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `cursor` other      | Send a `cursor` request if none is outstanding for that anchor; until answered, `transport-error` with     |\n|                     | `:dao.stream/retry? true`; once filed, the source's map verbatim, `invalid-anchor` included.                |\n+---------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `next` `c`          | A filed answer for `c` is returned and forgotten. Otherwise a `next` request for `c` is sent if none is     |\n|                     | outstanding, and the answer is `blocked`: nothing is observable at this position through this handle yet.  |\n+---------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `append!` `v`       | Send the request; return the channel writer's outcome. `ok` is acceptance on the outbound path, exactly     |\n|                     | `dao.stream.md` Writing's rule for a transport that carries values toward a stream elsewhere. The source's  |\n|                     | outcome is appended to the event writer when it is filed, correlated by id; without an event writer it is  |\n|                     | dropped.                                                                                                    |\n+---------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `close!`            | Local. The reflection forgets its outstanding ids; afterwards `append!` answers `closed`, `cursor` answers  |\n|                     | `closed`, `next` answers filed outcomes then `end`. Nothing crosses the wire.                               |\n+---------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n\n**Gone.** Any filed `not-found` for a reflection's identity that carries no\n`:dao.stream.remote/reason`, from the attach probe or from a later\noperation, marks it gone. A `not-found` with reason `no-surface` marks\nnothing and is returned as `transport-error` with that reason for the\noperation that caused it. Every later `cursor`,\n`next` and `append!` answers `transport-error` with\n`:dao.stream.remote/reason :dao.stream.remote/not-found`. `descriptor`\nstill answers `ok`: a name outlives what it named. A reflection marked gone\nis how a remote holder observes reclaim (section 6).\n\n**Refused.** A filed `:dao.stream/refused` is returned verbatim for the\noperation it answers and marks nothing: refusal is per operation\n(`dao.stream.md`, Result Convention).\n\n**Declared nature.** A reflection declares the surface its remote descriptor's\nsource declares, learned from the attach probe's answer; before that answer\nit declares `#{:reader :writer :closable}`, and an operation the source\nturns out to lack is answered as the `no-surface` case above. Its `blocked`\nis handle-relative, as the contract's\nConcurrency section already makes every outcome: one perspective at one\nmoment. Its excluded outcomes: `next` never answers `cursor-mismatch` or\n`invalid-cursor` from its own judgement, only relayed; `append!` never\nanswers the source's `full`, which arrives on the event writer.\n\n### 2.5 Loss and resend\n\nThe reflection reads no clock. A link's `:dao.stream.remote/resend-after k`\nmeans: an outstanding `descriptor`, `cursor` or `next` request is re-sent\nwhen the caller has asked `k` further times and it is still unanswered.\n`k` is unbounded on an ordered reliable channel and small on UDP. Those\nthree operations are idempotent, so a duplicate request recomputes the same\nor a later, equally true answer. `append!` is never re-sent: deduplication\nand correlation are the payload's (OD-2, accepted), and an append whose\nanswer never arrives has unknown effect, reported on the event writer as\n`{:dao.stream.remote/event :dao.stream.remote/append-unknown\n:dao.stream.remote/id n}` when the reflection is closed or the channel ends.\n\nThere are no sessions, no acknowledgement vectors, no windows and no\nresult retention at the answering peer. The cursor is the sequence number for reads,\nand `gap` remains the source's declaration, never a channel artifact.\n\n## 3. Channels\n\nA channel declares a portable value domain, a frame budget, and whether it\nis ordered and reliable. The link reads the last as composition data for\n`:dao.stream.remote/resend-after`; nothing else in section 2 changes per\nchannel.\n\n### 3.1 WebSocket\n\n`dao.stream.ws` as specified in `dao.stream.ws.md`: a channel is one\nattachment; its deposit medium is the channel reader and its socket handle\nthe channel writer. Transit-JSON text or the CBOR binary profile\n(`dao.stream.transit`, `dao.stream.cbor`). Ordered and reliable; frame\nbudget unbounded.\n\nDirection is establishment, not authority. A WebSocket has a dialer and an\nacceptor because TCP does. Once established the channel is symmetric and\nnothing in this document remembers who dialed. Hosts: clj, cljs on Node and\ncljd may dial or accept (`src/clj/dao/stream/ws/jvm.clj`,\n`src/cljs/dao/stream/ws/node.cljs`, `src/cljd/dao/stream/ws/dart.cljd`);\ncljs in a browser may only dial (`src/cljs/dao/stream/ws/browser.cljs`).\n\n### 3.2 UDP\n\n`dao.stream.udp` is a new channel with the WebSocket deposit model: a socket\nretains nothing, so it has no reader surface, and its adapter deposits every\ndatagram as an event carrying the datagram's source address and its decoded\nvalue onto the medium the composition wired.\n\n- Descriptor: `{:dao.stream/type :dao.stream/udp :dao.stream/identity\n  <channel identity> :dao.stream.udp/host h :dao.stream.udp/port p}`.\n  `attach!` binds or reuses a local socket and returns a handle whose writer\n  path is datagrams to that address.\n- One CBOR value per datagram, canonical profile. Datagram budget 1200\n  bytes, the figure `dao.jing.dht.node` already uses to clear common path\n  MTUs.\n- **Fragmentation is channel-internal.** An encoded message over budget is\n  split into `k` datagrams, each\n  `{:dao.stream.remote/id n :dao.stream.udp/part i :dao.stream.udp/parts k\n  :dao.stream.udp/bytes b}`, where `n` is the carried message's own request\n  id. The receiver reassembles by `[source-address direction id]` and\n  delivers only a complete message. Loss of any part is loss of the whole\n  message, recovered by the link's resend rule. Reassembly is bounded by\n  two composition data, `:dao.stream.udp/max-message-bytes` (default 64\n  KiB) and `:dao.stream.udp/max-partial-messages`, evicting the oldest\n  partial message; no clock. Neither the mirror step nor the link sees a\n  fragment.\n- Replies go to the datagram's source address, never to an address claimed\n  in a payload.\n- A send may name an explicit destination from an already-bound socket. Hole\n  punching depends on it (section 4).\n- Unordered and best-effort. Attachment identity is the remote host and port\n  as the socket saw them; it is the reflexive address a meeting peer\n  republishes.\n\nHosts: clj (`DatagramSocket`), cljs on Node (`dgram`), cljd\n(`RawDatagramSocket`). Browsers have no UDP.\n\n### 3.3 Pair\n\nA pair descriptor names two remote descriptors:\n\n```clojure\n{:dao.stream/type      :dao.stream/pair\n :dao.stream/identity  <channel identity>\n :dao.stream.remote/in  <remote descriptor>    ; this peer reads here\n :dao.stream.remote/out <remote descriptor>}   ; this peer writes here\n```\n\n`attach!` on it returns a channel whose reader is a reflection of `in` and\nwhose writer is a reflection of `out`. When a third peer serves both\nstreams from its table, two peers that cannot reach each other have a\nchannel, and the third peer runs nothing but mirror steps over two ring\nbuffers; the requests and answers inside are values in its streams it never\ninterprets. Streams are values sent through streams. Ordered; best-effort\nunder the inboxes' retention. A `gap` observed on `in` is channel loss,\nabsorbed by the resend rule, and is never reported to an inner reader as\nthe source's `gap`. Double framing consumes budget: an inner value inside an\nouter `append!` must fit the outer channel's budget with both envelopes.\n\n## 4. Reachability and NAT\n\nA channel needs one end that can receive an unsolicited packet, or a\ncoordinated simultaneous send. The protocol cannot change that; it makes\nthe workarounds conventions.\n\n+------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Situation                                      | Outcome                                                                    |\n+================================================+============================================================================+\n| One side reachable (public address, forwarded  | Direct: the other side dials, WebSocket or UDP.                            |\n| port, or full-cone NAT)                        |                                                                            |\n+------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Restricted or port-restricted NAT on one or    | Hole punch through a meeting peer (below).                                 |\n| both sides, UDP allowed                        |                                                                            |\n+------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Symmetric NAT on both sides, or carrier-grade  | Punching is unreliable. Relay through a pair served by a reachable peer.   |\n| NAT                                            |                                                                            |\n+------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| UDP blocked                                    | WebSocket only; the browser rule applies.                                  |\n+------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Browser at one end                             | It dials. Two browsers relay through a pair. A WebRTC data channel is a    |\n|                                                | future channel type whose candidates ride the same meeting stream.        |\n+------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n\n**Hole punching, as a convention.** A meeting peer M serves a\n`meet-requests` stream with surface `#{:writer}` and a `meet-board` stream\nwith surface `#{:reader}`, both ordinary table entries. Over a UDP channel\na peer appends `{:meet/here <name>}`; M's interpreter reads the request and\nthe channel attachment identity it arrived on, which for UDP is the\nreflexive address, and appends `{:meet/seen <name> :meet/reflexive {:host h\n:port p} :dao.lease/lease L}` to the board. Both parties of a call read the\nboard, then each sends a `descriptor` request with a fresh id to the other's\nreflexive address from the same socket M observed. A path exists when an\nanswer, even `not-found`, carrying one of this peer's outstanding ids\narrives from the expected address; the first inbound datagram alone confirms\nnothing. That is the whole of STUN, as facts on a stream; no probe frame\nexists.\n\n**Relay, as a convention.** A peer asks M, through `meet-requests`, for a\npair; M creates two ring buffers, enters them in its table, and posts their\nremote descriptors on the board as a lease grant (section 6). The asking\npeers attach a pair channel over them. M holds no authority, runs the same\ncode as every peer, interprets nothing it relays, and may be replaced by any\nreachable peer. That two unreachable peers need M at all is a fact about the\nnetwork, not a role in this protocol.\n\n**Address exposure.** Reflexive addresses on a board are readable by every\nreader of the board. This convention claims no private delivery.\n\n## 5. Conventions that are not protocol\n\nNone of these has a frame. Each is peers using sections 2 and 3.\n\n**The toy.** Peer B holds a handle `s` over the single value `\"hello\"`,\nidentity `\"str-1\"`, complete history, surface `#{:reader}`. B's table is\n`{\"str-1\" {:handle s :surface #{:reader}}}`. B accepts WebSocket connections\nas `dao.stream.ws.md` specifies and drives `mirror-step` over each\nconnection's deposit medium and socket handle. B publishes\n`{:dao.stream/type :dao.stream/remote :dao.stream/identity \"str-1\"\n:dao.stream/channel <B's ws descriptor>}`. Peer A calls `attach!`; the link\ndials and sends the `descriptor` probe; the answer carries `c0` and `c1`.\nA mints `cursor :oldest`, receiving `c0`. A calls `next c0`: no answer\nfiled, the link sends `{... :dao.stream.remote/op :dao.stream/next\n:dao.stream.remote/args [c0] :dao.stream.remote/budget 64\n:dao.stream.remote/id 2}` and answers `blocked`. B's mirror applies\n`(next s c0)`, `ok \"hello\" c1`, then `(next s c1)`, `end`, and answers\n`{:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok :dao.stream/value \"hello\"\n:dao.stream/cursor c1 :dao.stream.remote/more [{:dao.stream/outcome\n:dao.stream/end}] ...}`. A calls `next c0` again: the drain files the\nanswer and `ok \"hello\" c1` is returned; `next c1` answers `end`. Nothing\nknew it was a string.\n\n**Request and response service.** A peer S enters `requests` with surface\n`#{:writer}` and `answers` with surface `#{:reader}` in its table and runs\nan interpreter over the local ends. A peer P attaches reflections to both,\nmints `:newest` on `answers` first, appends `{:svc/request r ...}` through\n`requests`, and reads `answers` until it observes `:svc/request r`.\nRequest ids are self-minted random values so callers sharing one `answers`\nstream do not collide; a caller that needs private answers asks S for a\npair. S is a \"server\" only in that its table has those entries and its\ninterpreter has that policy. Either peer may serve such a pair to the other\nover the same channel. This is Linda's request medium, and the shape\n`yin.repl` and content lookup take (section 8).\n\n**Meeting board and relay pair.** Section 4.\n\n**Peer names.** A name is a self-minted value of at least 128 random bits,\nor a self-certifying hash of a public key (`dao.stream.discovery.md`). It\nappears only inside convention payloads. Nothing authenticates it until\nauthentication exists.\n\n## 6. Lease integration\n\nA served stream is a network resource, so its lifetime beyond one call is\ngoverned by `dao.lease.md`. The judge is composed inside the peer that\npossesses the resource, as that contract requires.\n\n+--------------------------------------------------------+---------+-------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Resource                                               | Lease?  | Holder; grantor and judge; subject                          |\n+========================================================+=========+=============================================================+\n| Table entry a peer serves by its own policy            | No      | The peer retires it when it likes.                          |\n+--------------------------------------------------------+---------+-------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Table entry served for a remote party (a stream a      | Yes     | The remote party; the holding peer; the identity.           |\n| migrated continuation still reads)                     |         |                                                             |\n+--------------------------------------------------------+---------+-------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Relay pair on a meeting peer                           | Yes     | The requesting peer; the meeting peer; the two identities.  |\n+--------------------------------------------------------+---------+-------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Link state, UDP reassembly buffers, channel sockets    | No      | Governed by close or by a bounded buffer.                   |\n+--------------------------------------------------------+---------+-------------------------------------------------------------+\n| NAT mappings and punch state                           | No      | Possessed by the NAT box, so no judge can exist; keep-alive |\n|                                                        |         | probes are the peer's own cadence.                          |\n+--------------------------------------------------------+---------+-------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Board postings                                         | No      | Records, never reclaimed; each carries the lease id of the  |\n|                                                        |         | registration or pair it belongs to, and a reader treats a   |\n|                                                        |         | posting whose lease has a `:lapsed` on the board as stale.  |\n+--------------------------------------------------------+---------+-------------------------------------------------------------+\n\n**Mechanism: no new wire shape.** A grantor enters two conventional streams\nin its table, `lease-proposals` with surface `#{:writer}` and\n`lease-grants` with surface `#{:reader}`, published as remote descriptors\nbeside its meeting descriptors. A holder appends a proposal through a\nreflection on the first and reads its grant through a reflection on the\nsecond. The grant creates one more table entry, a ring buffer with surface\n`#{:writer}`, and names its identity as `:dao.lease/holder`: this is the\nholder's **renewal medium**, and whatever lands on it is attributed to the\nholder by the medium, which is the attribution resolver `dao.lease.md`\nnames as per-author media. The judge reads it with a local cursor. The\nrenewal medium lives and dies with its lease; the reclaim procedure removes\nthe subject entries and the renewal entry together, idempotently, and\nreports success. A table entry under lease carries `:dao.lease/lease L`.\n\n**Renewal.** `append!` of `{:dao.lease/event :dao.lease/renewal\n:dao.lease/lease L}` on the holder's reflection of its renewal medium. The\nreflection's `ok` is outbound acceptance only; the holder advances its\nrenewal bound only on the source's `ok` observed on the link's event\nwriter. A holder with no event writer stops at its bound early, never late.\n\n**Reclaim observed.** The mirror answers `not-found` for a reclaimed\nidentity and the reflection marks itself gone (2.4). The holder may also\nread the grantor's own `:lapsed` record through its reflection on\n`lease-grants`: that is observing the grantor's stream, not carriage. And\nthe holder stops at its own bound regardless.\n\n**Expiry versus channel loss.** Channel loss is the absence of answers; the\ntable entry persists, so a holder that reconnects within duration plus\ntolerance finds its entry, its cursors and its lease intact. Expiry is a\npresent answer, `not-found`, on a healthy channel. Only expiry frees\nanything. A meeting peer that restarts loses its table and ledger, so every\npair is gone, holders observe `not-found` and re-propose; this is\n`dao.lease.md`'s reclaim-and-regrant with an empty inventory, correct\nbecause the table is process-scoped.\n\nUnits are the composition's; these conventions fix `{:ms 1 :s 1000}`. Each\nside reads its own tick stream, and cross-host rate skew is what the judge's\ntolerance covers.\n\n## 7. Middleware and the ShiBi seam\n\nThe mechanism is `dao.stream.middleware.md`: a middleware is a map of two\npure transforms over the operation map and the outcome map; `wrap` turns a\nhandle and a chain into a handle on the same logical stream; a middleware\nmaps position `p` of the inner to position `p` of the outer and never\ntouches a cursor, an anchor, an identity, an id or an outcome kind. This\ndocument adds the network attachment points.\n\n+------------------+--------------------------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------+\n| Attachment point | What is wrapped                                  | Typical use                                          |\n+==================+==================================================+======================================================+\n| table entry      | The served handle, before it enters the table.   | Gating, value encryption for serving, redaction,     |\n|                  | The mirror sees only the wrapped handle.         | metering.                                            |\n+------------------+--------------------------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------+\n| reflection       | The reflection, inside the host-composed attach  | Presenting a credential, value decryption,           |\n|                  | closure.                                         | metering.                                            |\n+------------------+--------------------------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------+\n| channel          | The channel's writer and reader at each end.     | Authenticated encryption of whole requests and       |\n|                  |                                                  | answers, so a relay sees opaque values.              |\n+------------------+--------------------------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------+\n\nKeys and chains are composition data handed to `wrap`, never descriptor\nfields. Value-level encryption hides values and leaves identity, ops,\ncursors, sizes and timing visible; channel-level encryption hides all but\nsizes, timing and which pair talks. Key distribution is a convention and is\nnot specified here. Ciphers come from libraries.\n\n**The seam.** Authentication and authorization belong to ShiBi (`shibi.md`),\nunspecified. This document offers only:\n\n- **The slot.** A reserved open key on the request map,\n  `:dao.stream.remote/credential`, whose value is opaque here and travels\n  per request. A per-channel credential is a convention: the reflection's\n  `present` middleware attaches the same value to every request.\n- **The context.** The mirror hands `apply-request` the context\n  `{:dao.stream.remote/channel <attachment identity>}`; local callers hand\n  `{}`. No other context key exists in this version.\n- **The gate**, mirror-side: `dao.stream.middleware/gate`, whose `verify`\n  is a pure function of folded state, context and request, whose `fold` is\n  a pure function over facts drained from explicit source streams, and\n  whose refusal is the contract's `:dao.stream/refused` with an opaque\n  `:dao.stream.middleware/reason`.\n- **The presenter**, reflection-side: `dao.stream.middleware/present`, a\n  pure function of context and request that fills the slot.\n\nA ShiBi built as index and query interpreters over a tuple space plugs in\nwithout changing this document: its index interpreter is the gate's `fold`\nover the streams it observes, its query interpreter is the gate's `verify`,\nits tokens are the slot's values, and its decisions, where it meters, are\nthe gate's decision emissions onto a stream it also indexes. The seam\nforecloses none of attenuation, offline verification, revocation, caveats on\nop, identity or time as tick readings, issuance by any peer, or migration\ncredentials; a budget spent across peers waits on `dao.stream.discovery.md`'s\ncapability-versus-currency decision, which this document does not depend\non. Until ShiBi exists, gating is an allow-list keyed on\n`:dao.stream.remote/channel` or a pre-shared value in the slot.\n\nLease attribution (section 6) rests on per-author media alone and needs no\ncredential; a gate on a renewal medium is an addition.\n\n## 8. Unification\n\nEvery existing network path, verified against the tree.\n\n+----------------------------------------------------+------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Existing                                           | Fate             | Why                                                                  |\n+====================================================+==================+======================================================================+\n| `dao.stream.ws` and the jvm, node, browser, dart   | subsumed         | Already a channel: writer plus deposit medium. Its accept and        |\n| adapters                                           |                  | disclaim frames and served-path table become unnecessary once        |\n|                                                    |                  | `not-found` is an answer per identity; retiring them is a slice.     |\n+----------------------------------------------------+------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `dao.stream.serving`                               | retired          | Forwards a source into every accepted socket and flattens a source    |\n|                                                    |                  | `gap` into a detach: the copy model. Its one consumer is             |\n|                                                    |                  | `yin.repl.serve`.                                                    |\n+----------------------------------------------------+------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `dao.stream.forward`                               | unrelated        | A local copy step; replication by convention, not exposure.          |\n+----------------------------------------------------+------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `dao.stream.rpc`                                   | convention-over  | Correlation by id over two streams is section 5's service; its       |\n|                                                    |                  | state machine may be kept as one such vocabulary over reflections.   |\n+----------------------------------------------------+------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `dao.stream.rpc.ws`                                | retired          | Its only job is translating `:ws/` envelopes for rpc; a reflection    |\n|                                                    |                  | is the channel binding now.                                          |\n+----------------------------------------------------+------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `dao.stream.apply` as a wire envelope              | retired          | Its ok-or-error wrapper duplicates the outcome map. The              |\n|                                                    |                  | `:dao.stream.apply/call` AST node and the VM's FFI bridge keep the    |\n|                                                    |                  | name and are unrelated to the network.                               |\n+----------------------------------------------------+------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `yin.repl.serve`, `connect`, `driver`, `adapter`,  | convention-over  | An eval service: `requests` and `answers` streams, `:op/eval` as     |\n| the `daostream:ws://` URL                          |                  | payload; the URL becomes a remote descriptor for that pair. The      |\n|                                                    |                  | shared-shell privilege is its interpreter's policy.                  |\n+----------------------------------------------------+------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `dao.jing.remote`, `remote.step`, `remote.async`,  | retired          | Deprecated whole; replaced below.                                    |\n| the `:dao.jing/remote` coordinate in               |                  |                                                                      |\n| `dao.jing.coordinate`                              |                  |                                                                      |\n+----------------------------------------------------+------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `yin.repl.link`, `yin.vm.linker` M3 and M4         | convention-over  | The content pair is a request and response service over reflections  |\n|                                                    |                  | or local ring buffers; the linker's stepped core is unchanged.       |\n+----------------------------------------------------+------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `dao.data.btree.storage/hydrate-async`,            | convention-over  | Consume the content client of this section; the callback facade is   |\n| `store-tree-async`                                 |                  | host policy, per `dao.stream.md` OD-5.                               |\n+----------------------------------------------------+------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `dao.jing.dht.node` UDP DHT                        | unrelated        | Raw JVM datagram RPC with its own Kademlia envelope; its socket       |\n|                                                    |                  | rules and 1200-byte budget are the UDP channel's template.           |\n+----------------------------------------------------+------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `dao.stream.transit`, `dao.stream.cbor`            | subsumed         | The channel codecs, unchanged.                                       |\n+----------------------------------------------------+------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n\n**What replaces `dao.jing.remote`.** Content lookup is a request and response\nservice over remote streams, in one new module `dao.jing.content`:\n\n- **Vocabulary**, a payload convention: `{:jing/request r :jing/get\n  address}` answered by `{:jing/request r :jing/found? b :jing/bytes b64}`;\n  `{:jing/request r :jing/put address :jing/bytes b64}` answered by\n  `{:jing/request r :jing/result :inserted | :present}`. `r` is a\n  self-minted random value. Bytes travel as padded standard Base64 text, as\n  today.\n- **The interpreter** (`dao.jing.content/serve-step`): a pure step over a\n  local `requests` reader and `answers` writer against a `dao.jing` handle.\n  It runs the one ingress canonicality check on every put.\n- **The ingress check**, one shared function `dao.jing/accept-bytes!`,\n  moved from `dao.jing.remote/accept-bytes!` and used by the interpreter,\n  by the stepped client on every `get` answer and `:present` verify, and\n  by `yin.vm.linker`. The three copies become one.\n- **The stepped client** (`dao.jing.content.step`): the `request-put`,\n  `request-get`, `request-materialize`, `step`, `abandon` shape of\n  `dao.jing.remote.step`, over a `requests` writer and an `answers` reader\n  that are reflections or local handles. This is the portable interface,\n  per `dao.stream.md` OD-5.\n- **The blocking driver** (`dao.jing.content.driver`, clj only): host\n  policy that steps the client and sleeps, returning a synchronous\n  `dao.jing` handle, for `dao.space.index`, `dao.data.btree.storage` and\n  `yin.vm.content` on the JVM. It replaces `connect-content!`.\n- **The async facade** (`dao.jing.content.async`): replaces\n  `dao.jing.remote.async` over the new stepped client, same consumer API.\n- **The coordinate**: `{:dao.jing/type :dao.jing/remote :dao.jing/requests\n  <remote descriptor> :dao.jing/answers <remote descriptor>}` on every\n  host, resolved by `dao.jing.coordinate/open!` to the driver on clj and to\n  the stepped client elsewhere. The `:url` form is gone.\n\n## 9. Completion criteria and implementation slices\n\nOrdered. Each names files, host lanes and its proof. Verification commands\nare in `docs/agents/build-n-test.md`.\n\n+----+------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+---------------+\n| #  | Slice and files                                            | Proves                                                 | Lanes         |\n+====+============================================================+========================================================+===============+\n| 0  | Contract amendments: `docs/design/dao.stream.md` OD-1,     | Existing consumers pass; `dao.stream.observe/step` and | clj cljs cljd |\n|    | OD-2, OD-3 accepted; `dao.stream.observe`, `yin.vm.engine` | the engine treat an unrecognized outcome as refused,   |               |\n|    | follow the unrecognized-outcome rule.                      | not a throw.                                           |               |\n+----+------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+---------------+\n| 1  | `dao.stream.middleware`: `apply-request`, `wrap`, `gate`,  | Position rule under a value cipher over a ring buffer; | clj cljs cljd |\n|    | `present`, a metering exemplar. `src/cljc/dao/stream/      | a gate with the channel allow-list refuses with        |               |\n|    | middleware.cljc`, tests.                                   | `:dao.stream/refused`; a filter cannot be expressed.   |               |\n+----+------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+---------------+\n| 2  | `dao.stream.remote` core: `mirror-step`, link, reflection, | The toy over two in-process ring buffers as the        | clj cljs cljd |\n|    | remote descriptor dispatch. `src/cljc/dao/stream/          | channel; `gap` from an evicting source crosses         |               |\n|    | remote.cljc`, tests.                                       | verbatim with the source's cursor; `blocked` then      |               |\n|    |                                                            | `ok` on re-ask; `not-found` marks gone; kept cursor    |               |\n|    |                                                            | from a reflection is accepted by the source handle.    |               |\n+----+------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+---------------+\n| 3  | WebSocket channel composition: mirror steps over accepted  | The toy across a socket on clj to Node, Node to clj,   | clj cljs cljd |\n|    | and dialed attachments; retire `:ws/accept`, disclaim and  | cljd to clj; both directions serve on one channel;     |               |\n|    | the served-path table in `dao.stream.ws`.                  | browser dials and reads.                               |               |\n+----+------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+---------------+\n| 4  | Content service: `dao.jing.content` with `serve-step`,     | `dao.jing.remote` tests ported and passing over the    | clj cljs cljd |\n|    | `step`, `driver` (clj), `async`; `dao.jing/accept-bytes!`; | new module; `dao.space.index`, btree hydration, the    |               |\n|    | `dao.jing.coordinate` new coordinate; delete               | linker M3 and M4 tests unchanged in outcome; no        |               |\n|    | `src/cljc/dao/jing/remote.cljc`, `remote/step.cljc`,       | require of `dao.jing.remote` remains.                  |               |\n|    | `remote/async.cljc` and their tests.                       |                                                        |               |\n+----+------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+---------------+\n| 5  | REPL as a service: `yin.repl.serve` and `connect` over     | `(connect \"daostream:ws://...\")` resolves to a remote   | clj cljs cljd |\n|    | `requests` and `answers` reflections; delete               | descriptor pair; the demo REPLs work; `yin.repl.link`  |               |\n|    | `dao.stream.serving`, `dao.stream.rpc.ws`, the apply wire  | serves content through slice 4.                        |               |\n|    | envelope from `dao.stream.apply`.                          |                                                        |               |\n+----+------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+---------------+\n| 6  | UDP channel: `dao.stream.udp` with fragmentation;          | The toy over UDP with a 100 KiB value; loss injection  | clj cljs cljd |\n|    | adapters `src/clj/dao/stream/udp/jvm.clj`, `src/cljs/dao/  | recovers by resend; oversize beyond the maximum is     |               |\n|    | stream/udp/node.cljs`, `src/cljd/dao/stream/udp/dart.cljd`.| `transport-error` with `:dao.stream.remote/oversize`.  |               |\n+----+------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+---------------+\n| 7  | Pair channel and the meeting and relay conventions with    | Two peers behind a simulated restricted NAT punch;     | clj           |\n|    | leases: `dao.stream.remote.pair`, `dao.stream.remote.meet`.| behind a simulated symmetric NAT they relay; a pair    |               |\n|    |                                                            | whose holder stops renewing answers `not-found` after  |               |\n|    |                                                            | duration plus tolerance; a reconnect within it finds   |               |\n|    |                                                            | the pair intact.                                       |               |\n+----+------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+---------------+\n| 8  | UCF facade: `yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md`      | The M4 acceptance row: a string-backed stream migrates | clj cljs cljd |\n|    | 7.4.3 and 7.5.3 lift and lower through remote descriptors  | with a kept cursor and resumes; forced eviction yields |               |\n|    | with the `:dao.stream.remote/v1` cursor profile.           | `gap` with the source's cursor.                        |               |\n+----+------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+---------------+\n\nDeferred, in order of expected arrival: authentication and authorization\n(ShiBi), key distribution, a WebRTC data channel, browser inbound. None\nchanges a shape in section 2.\n\n## 10. Contract amendments and companion edits\n\nIn `dao.stream.md`: OD-1 accepted with the write fallback \"effect unknown; no\nautomatic retry\" and the `:dao.stream/retry?` key; OD-2 accepted; OD-3\ndecision 2 accepted and (a)'s endpoint sentence in Envelopes; one\n`:dao.stream/refused` row on `cursor`, `next` and `append!`; one sentence\nin Surfaces on a handle composed over another handle. Nothing network.\n\nIn `dao.lease.md`: served entries and relay pairs named as subjects a lease\nmay govern; the carriage note gains this document's `not-found`.\n\nIn `dao.stream.ws.md`: the stream belongs to the peer that holds it;\nserving copies is the transport's, attaching to the original is this\ndocument's; dialer-side and acceptor-side; deferred liveness, resumption\nand RPC items point here.\n\nIn `yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md`: 7.4.3 and 7.5.3 cite this\ndocument for the facade; the put resume rule waits on the event writer's\nsource outcome; `:yin.k/cursor-profiles` names `:dao.stream.remote/v1`; the\nacceptance row cites section 9 slice 8.\n\nIn `dao.jing.md`, `yin.vm.linker.md`, `dao.data.btree.md`, `dao.jing.dht.md`\nand `daostream-udp-design.md`: the sentences naming `dao.jing.remote` as\nthe remote path, the linker's content pair \"over `dao.jing.remote.step`\",\nthe btree remote row, the DHT's NAT limitation, and the v1 UDP design's\nstatus now cite this document.\n# DaoStream Middleware\n\nStatus: design target, not implemented. Subordinate to\n[`dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md), which wins on any disagreement. Every\nsentence below is a rule. This document names no network; the network\nattachment points are in [`dao.stream.remote.md`](./dao.stream.remote.md),\nsection 7.\n\n## What a middleware is\n\nA middleware is a value: a map of two pure transforms, one over the\noperation map on the way in and one over the outcome map on the way out. It\nis the adapter shape of `datom.world.md` (a map of transform functions)\napplied to a handle rather than to a host boundary. Ring's request and\nresponse pair maps onto the operation map and the contract's outcome map.\n\n```clojure\n{:dao.stream.middleware/in  (fn [ctx req] -> req | outcome-map)\n :dao.stream.middleware/out (fn [ctx req outcome] -> outcome)}\n```\n\n`in` returning an outcome map short-circuits: the inner handle is not\nconsulted and that map is the operation's outcome, after the `out`\ntransforms of the middlewares outside it. `ctx` is a plain map the caller\nsupplies and no middleware may fabricate; a local caller supplies `{}`.\n\n## The operation map\n\nAn operation as data. Its vocabulary is `dao.stream.remote.md`'s so that the\nsame chain runs on either side of a channel without translation:\n\n```clojure\n{:dao.stream.remote/op    :dao.stream/cursor | :dao.stream/next | :dao.stream/append!\n :dao.stream.remote/args  [anchor] | [cursor] | [value]\n ...}                     ; open: further qualified keys pass through untouched\n```\n\n`descriptor` and `close!` are not operations a middleware sees: `descriptor`\nis a name and `close!` is a lifecycle transition, and neither has an outcome\na policy may decline (`dao.stream.md`, Close; Envelopes).\n\n`(apply-request h ctx req) -> outcome` applies one operation map to a\nhandle. It is the one place an operation map becomes a protocol call.\n\n## wrap\n\n`(wrap h chain) -> handle`, where `chain` is a vector of middlewares\napplied outermost first. The returned handle implements the protocols the\ninner handle implements and no others. On each protocol call it builds the\noperation map, runs every `in` from the outermost inward, applies the result\nto `h` with `apply-request`, and runs every `out` from the innermost\noutward. Its `descriptor` and `close!` delegate to `h` unchanged.\n\nThe wrapped handle is a handle on the same logical stream as `h`: it\ndeclares `h`'s identity, passes cursors through, and may present values\nunder its own interpretation (`dao.stream.md`, Surfaces).\n\n## The position rule\n\nA middleware maps position `p` of the inner handle to position `p` of the\nouter handle, for every `p`. It therefore never:\n\n- constructs, parses or rewrites a cursor, an anchor or a `gap` recovery\n  cursor;\n- changes `:dao.stream/outcome`, `:dao.stream/identity`,\n  `:dao.stream.remote/op` or `:dao.stream.remote/id`;\n- skips, merges or reorders elements.\n\nIt may replace `(first args)` on `append!` and `:dao.stream/value` on an\n`ok` from `next` with any value, of any size; add or read open keys; and\nshort-circuit with an outcome from the operation's exhaustive set. A cipher,\na compressor, a redactor and an annotator preserve the rule. A filter that\ndrops elements does not: it is an interpreter that forwards into a new\nstream with its own identity (`dao.stream.md`, Composition), never a\nmiddleware.\n\n## The four prohibitions\n\nA transform is total and returns at once. It never loops over the inner\nhandle, never retries, never waits, and never invokes any handle operation\nother than one `append!` to a side stream it was composed with. A transform\nthat needs facts from a stream drains that stream with its own cursor inside\nthe call, as `gate` below does, and reads no host clock.\n\n## Failure inside a transform\n\nA value that cannot be transformed on the way out (undecodable ciphertext,\na corrupt compressed value) is presented as\n`{:dao.stream.middleware/undecodable true :dao.stream.middleware/raw v}`\nunder `:dao.stream/value`, with the outcome and cursor untouched: the\nposition is real and the reader decides. A value that cannot be transformed\non the way in answers `:dao.stream/invalid-value`.\n\n## Two generic middlewares\n\n**gate**, the authorize step. Composition data only; the policy is not\nspecified here.\n\n```clojure\n(gate {:dao.stream.middleware/verify  (fn [state ctx req] -> nil | reason)  ; pure; nil allows\n       :dao.stream.middleware/fold    (fn [state fact] -> state)             ; pure\n       :dao.stream.middleware/sources [{:handle h :cursor c} ...]            ; explicit fact streams\n       :dao.stream.middleware/state   s0})\n```\n\nIts `in` drains every source to `blocked` with the gate's own cursors,\nfolds each fact into its state, then calls `verify`. `nil` passes the\nrequest through; a reason short-circuits with\n`{:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/refused :dao.stream.middleware/reason\nreason}`. A source that answers `gap` is folded as the fact\n`{:dao.stream.middleware/gap true}` so a policy may fail closed. A gate that\nmust account for its own decisions appends each as\n`{:dao.stream.middleware/decision :allow | :refuse ...}` to a side stream\nit was composed with and that is also one of its sources: this is\n**decision emission**, and it is how metered use keeps its state on a\nstream rather than in the gate.\n\n**present**, the credential step.\n\n```clojure\n(present {:dao.stream.middleware/present (fn [ctx req] -> credential)})\n```\n\nIts `in` associates the result under `:dao.stream.remote/credential`. Its\n`out` is identity.\n\nNeither middleware knows what a credential is. The trivial policy this\nrepository ships with is an allow-list keyed on `:dao.stream.remote/channel`\nin `ctx`, ignoring the credential. Everything else belongs to\n[`shibi.md`](./shibi.md).\n\n## Exemplars\n\nValue encryption: `in` on `:dao.stream/append!` replaces the value with its\nauthenticated ciphertext; `out` on an `ok` from `:dao.stream/next` replaces\n`:dao.stream/value` with the plaintext or the undecodable marker. Cursors\nuntouched. The key is an argument to the constructor that built the\nmiddleware, exactly as a deposit medium is an argument to a transport\nconstructor. Ciphers come from libraries.\n\nMetering: `out` appends `{:meter/identity id :meter/op op :meter/outcome\nkind}` to a composed metering stream and returns the outcome unchanged. The\nside effect is a stream emission (`datom.world.md`, Streams).\n\n## Invariant compliance\n\nNo callback: a transform is called downward by the caller in the caller's\ncontrol flow and returns a value; nothing is registered to be invoked\nlater. No implicit control flow: the four prohibitions. No hidden global\nstate: keys, policies and sources are arguments to `wrap`. No shared\nmutable state beyond what any handle holds. Interpretation and execution\nstay separate: `apply-request` judges no value. No assumed graph: a chain is\nan explicit vector.\n# ShiBi\n\nStatus: stub. ShiBi is not specified and not implemented\n(`docs/bootstrap.md`, Capability Engine). This page records only what other\ndocuments may rely on and what ShiBi must supply. It designs nothing.\n\n## What it is\n\nShiBi is the capability system of datom.world: authentication and\nauthorization are its job and no other document's. It is to be built the\nway `dao.space` is built, as a tuple space that emerges from two\ninterpreters over ordinary streams, one that indexes and one that queries,\nneither knowing the other exists (`datom.world.md`, Streams). Its tokens are\ndescribed elsewhere as Macaroon-style: attenuatable, offline-verifiable,\nrevocable caveats (`dao.space.security.md`, Capabilities Govern\nInterpreters).\n\n## How it plugs in\n\nShiBi integrates through the middleware seam of\n[`dao.stream.middleware.md`](./dao.stream.middleware.md) and the attachment\npoints of [`dao.stream.remote.md`](./dao.stream.remote.md) section 7, which\nare fixed and which ShiBi must not change. To plug in it supplies:\n\n- a `verify`, a pure function of folded state, context and request that\n  answers nil or a reason: its query interpreter;\n- a `fold` and the source streams it observes: its index interpreter;\n- a `present`, a pure function of context and request that produces the\n  value carried in `:dao.stream.remote/credential`;\n- a reason vocabulary under its own namespace for\n  `:dao.stream.middleware/reason`;\n- where use is metered, the decision facts it emits and indexes.\n\nThe contract's refusal outcome is `:dao.stream/refused` and is\ncapability-free (`dao.stream.md`, Result Convention). Lease attribution\nrests on per-author media and does not depend on ShiBi\n(`dao.stream.remote.md`, section 6; `yin.repl.link-policy.md`).\n\n## What blocks it being load-bearing\n\n`dao.stream.discovery.md`, The ShiBi precondition: ShiBi is a capability\ntoken in one place and a fungible currency in another, and a currency needs\ndouble-spend resolution, which is global consensus. Until that decision is\nmade, no design may depend on ShiBi for anything beyond the seam above, and\ngating in this repository is an allow-list or a pre-shared value.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md | sed -n '1,160p;160,340p;340,520p;520,725p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.middleware.md; nl -ba docs/design/shibi.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md | sed -n '1,160p;160,340p;340,520p;520,725p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.middleware.md; nl -ba docs/design/shibi.md\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t# DaoStream Remote: Any Stream, Reachable As Itself\n     2\t\n     3\tStatus: design target, not implemented. Subordinate to\n     4\t[`dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md), which is the contract and wins on any\n     5\tdisagreement. Every sentence below is a rule. It replaces the earlier serve\n     6\tdraft (git history, `docs/design/dao.stream.serve.md` at `71f3fb93`) and\n     7\tsucceeds `dao.jing.remote`, which is deprecated whole once this is\n     8\timplemented (section 8).\n     9\t\n    10\t## 1. Invariant, impossibilities, non-goals\n    11\t\n    12\tThe governing requirement, in the owner's words:\n    13\t\n    14\t> any implementation of dao.stream can be mechanically exposed via websocket\n    15\t> or udp and communicate p2p. client-server is just one stigmergic behavior\n    16\t> that interpreters implement. It should be able to traverse a NAT. A toy\n    17\t> example would be a string with a dao.stream wrapper that can be exposed on\n    18\t> a websocket on a remote interpreter.\n    19\t\n    20\t> There should be no concept of a server or client. its P2P but client/server\n    21\t> model can be implemented by convention. there is no priviledge server or\n    22\t> client.\n    23\t\n    24\t`dao.stream` is an abstraction boundary, not a network boundary. This\n    25\tdocument makes it a network boundary too, and keeps every network concept on\n    26\tits own side: nothing here adds an operation, an outcome or a key to the\n    27\tcontract beyond the three accepted decisions listed in section 10.\n    28\t\n    29\t**Impossibilities, stated plainly.**\n    30\t\n    31\t- Two peers that are both unreachable (symmetric NAT or carrier-grade NAT on\n    32\t  both sides, or two browsers) cannot exchange a byte directly. A third peer\n    33\t  with a reachable address is required. That peer runs the code in this\n    34\t  document and nothing else; it holds no protocol role and any reachable\n    35\t  peer can take its place (section 4).\n    36\t- A browser cannot listen. It dials, or it is reached through a third peer.\n    37\t- Everything is plaintext and unauthenticated unless middleware is composed\n    38\t  (section 7). A relay peer sees every message it carries.\n    39\t- UDP messages are bounded by a composed maximum (section 3.2).\n    40\t- A reclaimed lease means the resource is gone, not disconnected (section 6).\n    41\t- A false lapse under partition is possible (`dao.lease.md`, Limits).\n    42\t\n    43\t**Non-goals.** Authentication, authorization and key distribution (section 7\n    44\tnames the seam; `shibi.md` owns the rest); WebRTC data channels; browser\n    45\tinbound; readiness notification; exactly-once delivery.\n    46\t\n    47\t## 2. The design: mirror and reflection\n    48\t\n    49\tOne sentence: a peer that holds a handle answers the handle's own operations\n    50\tas data over a channel; a peer at the other end holds a handle whose\n    51\toperations are those questions asked across the channel, with the source's\n    52\toutcome maps returned verbatim.\n    53\t\n    54\tFour concepts.\n    55\t\n    56\t+-------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n    57\t| Concept     | Definition                                                                                                     |\n    58\t+=============+================================================================================================================+\n    59\t| channel     | Two ordinary handles with exactly two ends: a writer whose `append!` carries one value toward the other end,    |\n    60\t|             | and a reader positioned on the values the other end sent. A WebSocket attachment plus its deposit medium is a  |\n    61\t|             | channel (`dao.stream.ws.md`); a UDP socket adapter plus its deposit medium is a channel (3.2); two ring buffers |\n    62\t|             | in one process are a channel; two reflections served by a third peer are a channel (3.3).                      |\n    63\t+-------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n    64\t| table       | Composition data on a peer: `{identity {:handle h :surface S}}`, plus the optional keys of sections 6 and 7.    |\n    65\t|             | It is a plain map the composition owns, not a registry (`dao.stream.md`, No hidden global state).              |\n    66\t+-------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n    67\t| mirror step | The pure step a peer runs over a channel to answer requests against its table. It holds no state between       |\n    68\t|             | calls beyond the channel reader's cursor.                                                                      |\n    69\t+-------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n    70\t| reflection  | The handle `attach!` returns for a remote descriptor. A **link**, one per channel, holds what the reflections   |\n    71\t|             | on that channel share.                                                                                         |\n    72\t+-------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n    73\t\n    74\tEvery peer may run any number of mirror steps and hold any number of\n    75\treflections, on the same channel, in both directions. No request names a\n    76\tkind of peer; the protocol has no peer identity at all. A peer is a channel\n    77\tend.\n    78\t\n    79\t### 2.1 Wire shapes\n    80\t\n    81\tTwo logical shapes cross every channel. Keys the contract owns stay under\n    82\t`:dao.stream/`; keys this document owns are under `:dao.stream.remote/`,\n    83\tthe transport-owned namespace rule of `dao.stream.md` (Envelopes).\n    84\t\n    85\t```clojure\n    86\t;; request\n    87\t{:dao.stream/identity        id\n    88\t :dao.stream.remote/op       op        ; :dao.stream/descriptor | :dao.stream/cursor\n    89\t                                       ; | :dao.stream/next | :dao.stream/append!\n    90\t :dao.stream.remote/args     [...]     ; []  | [anchor] | [cursor] | [value]\n    91\t :dao.stream.remote/id       n         ; asker-minted, unique per channel reader the asker holds\n    92\t :dao.stream.remote/budget   k}        ; optional, next only, a positive integer\n    93\t\n    94\t;; answer: the contract's outcome map for the requested operation, verbatim, plus\n    95\t{:dao.stream.remote/id       n\n    96\t :dao.stream/identity        id\n    97\t :dao.stream.remote/oldest   <cursor>  ; the source's current :oldest, when the identity is served\n    98\t :dao.stream.remote/newest   <cursor>  ; the source's current :newest, likewise\n    99\t :dao.stream.remote/more     [...]}    ; optional, next only: further outcome maps in order\n   100\t```\n   101\t\n   102\tA request is one value on the channel writer; an answer is one value on the\n   103\tchannel writer of the other end. The request map is open: keys not named\n   104\there pass to the mirror step's middleware chain (section 7) and are\n   105\totherwise ignored. A value that is neither a well-formed request nor a\n   106\twell-formed answer is dropped as a diagnostic below the mirror step and the\n   107\tlink, as malformed wire input (`datom.world.md`, Host Boundaries).\n   108\t\n   109\tWire ops are exactly four. `close!` never crosses: the mirror holds nothing\n   110\tper remote party to close, and the owner's close is never remote. `attach!`\n   111\tnever crosses: it consumes a descriptor and produces a local handle.\n   112\t\n   113\t### 2.2 The remote descriptor\n   114\t\n   115\t```clojure\n   116\t{:dao.stream/type      :dao.stream/remote\n   117\t :dao.stream/identity  id                      ; the served logical stream\n   118\t :dao.stream/channel   <channel descriptor>}   ; section 3: ws, udp or pair\n   119\t```\n   120\t\n   121\tThe descriptor names a stream and a channel. It carries no authorization, no\n   122\tsurface and no anchors; the mirror answers those. Two remote descriptors\n   123\tthat name one identity through different channels reach one stream, as the\n   124\tcontract's Envelopes section requires of descriptors for one logical stream\n   125\tserved by one transport (OD-3 (a), accepted). Attaching through this\n   126\ttransport never creates: a dynamic dispatch table has an\n   127\t`:dao.stream/attach` entry for `:dao.stream/remote` and no\n   128\t`:dao.stream/create` entry.\n   129\t\n   130\t### 2.3 The mirror step\n   131\t\n   132\t`(mirror-step table chan-reader cursor chan-writer) -> cursor'`. For each\n   133\trequest read from `chan-reader`, in order, bounded by a composition budget:\n   134\t\n   135\t1. Look up `:dao.stream/identity` in the table. Absent: append\n   136\t   `{:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/not-found}` with the request's id and\n   137\t   identity; an identity this peer does not serve has no operations, so\n   138\t   `not-found` is the honest answer for all four. Present, but\n   139\t   `:dao.stream.remote/op` outside the entry's declared surface: append\n   140\t   `not-found` with `:dao.stream.remote/reason :dao.stream.remote/no-surface`,\n   141\t   because the contract forbids answering a nonexistent operation with an\n   142\t   outcome of that operation (Surfaces).\n   143\t2. Otherwise answer `:dao.stream/descriptor` from the entry's handle\n   144\t   directly, and apply `:dao.stream/cursor`, `:dao.stream/next` and\n   145\t   `:dao.stream/append!` to it through `dao.stream.middleware/apply-request`\n   146\t   (`dao.stream.middleware.md`), with context\n   147\t   `{:dao.stream.remote/channel <channel attachment identity>}`.\n   148\t3. Append the outcome map with `:dao.stream.remote/id`,\n   149\t   `:dao.stream/identity`, and the source's current `:oldest` and `:newest`\n   150\t   cursors obtained by `cursor` on the entry's handle. Where `cursor` does\n   151\t   not answer `ok` for an anchor, that piggyback key is omitted.\n   152\t\n   153\tFor `:dao.stream/next` with a budget `k` the mirror may follow the successor\n   154\tcursor of each `ok` outcome up to `k - 1` further times and place the\n   155\tfurther outcome maps, in order, under `:dao.stream.remote/more`. The first\n   156\toutcome is the answer map itself. The vector ends at and includes the first\n   157\tnon-`ok` outcome when one occurs, so `blocked`, `end` and `gap` reach the\n   158\treader with the source's own recovery cursor. A mirror that ignores the\n   159\tbudget is correct.\n   160\t\n   160\t\n   161\tA value the channel cannot carry (`invalid-value` from the channel writer)\n   162\tis answered as `{:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/transport-error\n   163\t:dao.stream.remote/reason :dao.stream.remote/oversize}` in place of that\n   164\telement; the read is not skipped and no cursor advances past it. Large\n   165\tmedia travel as `dao.jing` addresses (`dao.stream.md`, Granularity).\n   166\t\n   167\tThe mirror never constructs, parses or rewrites a cursor, an anchor or an\n   168\toutcome. The served stream is the original: its cursors, its positions, its\n   169\t`gap`, its `end`. This is the whole basis of the claim in the title, and it\n   170\trests on cursors being plain data that survive the codec (OD-3 decision 2,\n   171\taccepted). A handle whose cursors hold a host object cannot be served, and\n   172\ta composition refuses to enter it into a table.\n   173\t\n   174\t### 2.4 The reflection\n   175\t\n   176\t`attach!` on a remote descriptor returns `:dao.stream/ok` at once with a\n   177\treflection handle and a local, opaque `:dao.stream/attachment`. This is the\n   178\tcontract's deferred remote confirmation (`dao.stream.md`, attach outcomes).\n   179\tThe host-composed attach closure keeps one link per channel descriptor,\n   180\tshared by every reflection through that channel. A link holds: the channel\n   181\twriter; the channel reader and the link's own cursor on it; the set of\n   182\toutstanding request ids with the request each was sent for; filed answers\n   183\tkeyed by id; the latest piggybacked anchors per identity; an optional event\n   184\twriter; and the policy datum `:dao.stream.remote/resend-after`.\n   185\t\n   186\tOn attach the link sends one `descriptor` request for the identity. Its\n   187\tanswer is the reflection's confirmation. `not-found` marks the reflection\n   188\t**gone**; `ok` records the source's descriptor and anchors. Either is\n   189\tappended to the event writer when one is composed.\n   190\t\n   191\t**Drain.** Every operation on any reflection first reads the link's channel\n   192\treader to `blocked`, filing each answer whose id is outstanding, installing\n   193\teach `:dao.stream.remote/more` outcome at the cursor that precedes it, and\n   194\trecording piggybacked anchors. Answers whose id is not outstanding are\n   195\tdropped. Non-answer values are dropped as diagnostics. This is where the\n   196\tasynchrony goes (`dao.stream.md`, Where the asynchrony goes): the caller's\n   197\town polling is the cadence, and no driver step exists.\n   198\t\n   199\tThen the operation answers as follows.\n   200\t\n   201\t+---------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   202\t| Operation           | Reflection behaviour after the drain                                                                        |\n   203\t+=====================+=============================================================================================================+\n   204\t| `descriptor`        | Local. `ok` with the remote descriptor it was attached with and `:dao.stream/identity`. Same identity as   |\n   205\t|                     | the source's own descriptor, different reachability.                                                       |\n   206\t+---------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   207\t| `cursor` `:oldest`  | `ok` with the latest piggybacked cursor for that anchor. Before any answer has arrived, `transport-error`   |\n   208\t| or `:newest`        | with `:dao.stream/retry? true`. A stale `:newest` is earlier than a fresh one, so a composition that mints  |\n   209\t|                     | before an operation still observes everything the operation causes; a stale `:oldest` may yield `gap`,     |\n   210\t|                     | which the contract already permits of a fresh `:oldest`.                                                   |\n   211\t+---------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   212\t| `cursor` other      | Send a `cursor` request if none is outstanding for that anchor; until answered, `transport-error` with     |\n   213\t|                     | `:dao.stream/retry? true`; once filed, the source's map verbatim, `invalid-anchor` included.                |\n   214\t+---------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   215\t| `next` `c`          | A filed answer for `c` is returned and forgotten. Otherwise a `next` request for `c` is sent if none is     |\n   216\t|                     | outstanding, and the answer is `blocked`: nothing is observable at this position through this handle yet.  |\n   217\t+---------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   218\t| `append!` `v`       | Send the request; return the channel writer's outcome. `ok` is acceptance on the outbound path, exactly     |\n   219\t|                     | `dao.stream.md` Writing's rule for a transport that carries values toward a stream elsewhere. The source's  |\n   220\t|                     | outcome is appended to the event writer when it is filed, correlated by id; without an event writer it is  |\n   221\t|                     | dropped.                                                                                                    |\n   222\t+---------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   223\t| `close!`            | Local. The reflection forgets its outstanding ids; afterwards `append!` answers `closed`, `cursor` answers  |\n   224\t|                     | `closed`, `next` answers filed outcomes then `end`. Nothing crosses the wire.                               |\n   225\t+---------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   226\t\n   227\t**Gone.** Any filed `not-found` for a reflection's identity that carries no\n   228\t`:dao.stream.remote/reason`, from the attach probe or from a later\n   229\toperation, marks it gone. A `not-found` with reason `no-surface` marks\n   230\tnothing and is returned as `transport-error` with that reason for the\n   231\toperation that caused it. Every later `cursor`,\n   232\t`next` and `append!` answers `transport-error` with\n   233\t`:dao.stream.remote/reason :dao.stream.remote/not-found`. `descriptor`\n   234\tstill answers `ok`: a name outlives what it named. A reflection marked gone\n   235\tis how a remote holder observes reclaim (section 6).\n   236\t\n   237\t**Refused.** A filed `:dao.stream/refused` is returned verbatim for the\n   238\toperation it answers and marks nothing: refusal is per operation\n   239\t(`dao.stream.md`, Result Convention).\n   240\t\n   241\t**Declared nature.** A reflection declares the surface its remote descriptor's\n   242\tsource declares, learned from the attach probe's answer; before that answer\n   243\tit declares `#{:reader :writer :closable}`, and an operation the source\n   244\tturns out to lack is answered as the `no-surface` case above. Its `blocked`\n   245\tis handle-relative, as the contract's\n   246\tConcurrency section already makes every outcome: one perspective at one\n   247\tmoment. Its excluded outcomes: `next` never answers `cursor-mismatch` or\n   248\t`invalid-cursor` from its own judgement, only relayed; `append!` never\n   249\tanswers the source's `full`, which arrives on the event writer.\n   250\t\n   251\t### 2.5 Loss and resend\n   252\t\n   253\tThe reflection reads no clock. A link's `:dao.stream.remote/resend-after k`\n   254\tmeans: an outstanding `descriptor`, `cursor` or `next` request is re-sent\n   255\twhen the caller has asked `k` further times and it is still unanswered.\n   256\t`k` is unbounded on an ordered reliable channel and small on UDP. Those\n   257\tthree operations are idempotent, so a duplicate request recomputes the same\n   258\tor a later, equally true answer. `append!` is never re-sent: deduplication\n   259\tand correlation are the payload's (OD-2, accepted), and an append whose\n   260\tanswer never arrives has unknown effect, reported on the event writer as\n   261\t`{:dao.stream.remote/event :dao.stream.remote/append-unknown\n   262\t:dao.stream.remote/id n}` when the reflection is closed or the channel ends.\n   263\t\n   264\tThere are no sessions, no acknowledgement vectors, no windows and no\n   265\tresult retention at the answering peer. The cursor is the sequence number for reads,\n   266\tand `gap` remains the source's declaration, never a channel artifact.\n   267\t\n   268\t## 3. Channels\n   269\t\n   270\tA channel declares a portable value domain, a frame budget, and whether it\n   271\tis ordered and reliable. The link reads the last as composition data for\n   272\t`:dao.stream.remote/resend-after`; nothing else in section 2 changes per\n   273\tchannel.\n   274\t\n   275\t### 3.1 WebSocket\n   276\t\n   277\t`dao.stream.ws` as specified in `dao.stream.ws.md`: a channel is one\n   278\tattachment; its deposit medium is the channel reader and its socket handle\n   279\tthe channel writer. Transit-JSON text or the CBOR binary profile\n   280\t(`dao.stream.transit`, `dao.stream.cbor`). Ordered and reliable; frame\n   281\tbudget unbounded.\n   282\t\n   283\tDirection is establishment, not authority. A WebSocket has a dialer and an\n   284\tacceptor because TCP does. Once established the channel is symmetric and\n   285\tnothing in this document remembers who dialed. Hosts: clj, cljs on Node and\n   286\tcljd may dial or accept (`src/clj/dao/stream/ws/jvm.clj`,\n   287\t`src/cljs/dao/stream/ws/node.cljs`, `src/cljd/dao/stream/ws/dart.cljd`);\n   288\tcljs in a browser may only dial (`src/cljs/dao/stream/ws/browser.cljs`).\n   289\t\n   290\t### 3.2 UDP\n   291\t\n   292\t`dao.stream.udp` is a new channel with the WebSocket deposit model: a socket\n   293\tretains nothing, so it has no reader surface, and its adapter deposits every\n   294\tdatagram as an event carrying the datagram's source address and its decoded\n   295\tvalue onto the medium the composition wired.\n   296\t\n   297\t- Descriptor: `{:dao.stream/type :dao.stream/udp :dao.stream/identity\n   298\t  <channel identity> :dao.stream.udp/host h :dao.stream.udp/port p}`.\n   299\t  `attach!` binds or reuses a local socket and returns a handle whose writer\n   300\t  path is datagrams to that address.\n   301\t- One CBOR value per datagram, canonical profile. Datagram budget 1200\n   302\t  bytes, the figure `dao.jing.dht.node` already uses to clear common path\n   303\t  MTUs.\n   304\t- **Fragmentation is channel-internal.** An encoded message over budget is\n   305\t  split into `k` datagrams, each\n   306\t  `{:dao.stream.remote/id n :dao.stream.udp/part i :dao.stream.udp/parts k\n   307\t  :dao.stream.udp/bytes b}`, where `n` is the carried message's own request\n   308\t  id. The receiver reassembles by `[source-address direction id]` and\n   309\t  delivers only a complete message. Loss of any part is loss of the whole\n   310\t  message, recovered by the link's resend rule. Reassembly is bounded by\n   311\t  two composition data, `:dao.stream.udp/max-message-bytes` (default 64\n   312\t  KiB) and `:dao.stream.udp/max-partial-messages`, evicting the oldest\n   313\t  partial message; no clock. Neither the mirror step nor the link sees a\n   314\t  fragment.\n   315\t- Replies go to the datagram's source address, never to an address claimed\n   316\t  in a payload.\n   317\t- A send may name an explicit destination from an already-bound socket. Hole\n   318\t  punching depends on it (section 4).\n   319\t- Unordered and best-effort. Attachment identity is the remote host and port\n   320\t  as the socket saw them; it is the reflexive address a meeting peer\n   321\t  republishes.\n   322\t\n   323\tHosts: clj (`DatagramSocket`), cljs on Node (`dgram`), cljd\n   324\t(`RawDatagramSocket`). Browsers have no UDP.\n   325\t\n   326\t### 3.3 Pair\n   327\t\n   328\tA pair descriptor names two remote descriptors:\n   329\t\n   330\t```clojure\n   331\t{:dao.stream/type      :dao.stream/pair\n   332\t :dao.stream/identity  <channel identity>\n   333\t :dao.stream.remote/in  <remote descriptor>    ; this peer reads here\n   334\t :dao.stream.remote/out <remote descriptor>}   ; this peer writes here\n   335\t```\n   336\t\n   337\t`attach!` on it returns a channel whose reader is a reflection of `in` and\n   338\twhose writer is a reflection of `out`. When a third peer serves both\n   339\tstreams from its table, two peers that cannot reach each other have a\n   340\tchannel, and the third peer runs nothing but mirror steps over two ring\n   340\tchannel, and the third peer runs nothing but mirror steps over two ring\n   341\tbuffers; the requests and answers inside are values in its streams it never\n   342\tinterprets. Streams are values sent through streams. Ordered; best-effort\n   343\tunder the inboxes' retention. A `gap` observed on `in` is channel loss,\n   344\tabsorbed by the resend rule, and is never reported to an inner reader as\n   345\tthe source's `gap`. Double framing consumes budget: an inner value inside an\n   346\touter `append!` must fit the outer channel's budget with both envelopes.\n   347\t\n   348\t## 4. Reachability and NAT\n   349\t\n   350\tA channel needs one end that can receive an unsolicited packet, or a\n   351\tcoordinated simultaneous send. The protocol cannot change that; it makes\n   352\tthe workarounds conventions.\n   353\t\n   354\t+------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   355\t| Situation                                      | Outcome                                                                    |\n   356\t+================================================+============================================================================+\n   357\t| One side reachable (public address, forwarded  | Direct: the other side dials, WebSocket or UDP.                            |\n   358\t| port, or full-cone NAT)                        |                                                                            |\n   359\t+------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   360\t| Restricted or port-restricted NAT on one or    | Hole punch through a meeting peer (below).                                 |\n   361\t| both sides, UDP allowed                        |                                                                            |\n   362\t+------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   363\t| Symmetric NAT on both sides, or carrier-grade  | Punching is unreliable. Relay through a pair served by a reachable peer.   |\n   364\t| NAT                                            |                                                                            |\n   365\t+------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   366\t| UDP blocked                                    | WebSocket only; the browser rule applies.                                  |\n   367\t+------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   368\t| Browser at one end                             | It dials. Two browsers relay through a pair. A WebRTC data channel is a    |\n   369\t|                                                | future channel type whose candidates ride the same meeting stream.        |\n   370\t+------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   371\t\n   372\t**Hole punching, as a convention.** A meeting peer M serves a\n   373\t`meet-requests` stream with surface `#{:writer}` and a `meet-board` stream\n   374\twith surface `#{:reader}`, both ordinary table entries. Over a UDP channel\n   375\ta peer appends `{:meet/here <name>}`; M's interpreter reads the request and\n   376\tthe channel attachment identity it arrived on, which for UDP is the\n   377\treflexive address, and appends `{:meet/seen <name> :meet/reflexive {:host h\n   378\t:port p} :dao.lease/lease L}` to the board. Both parties of a call read the\n   379\tboard, then each sends a `descriptor` request with a fresh id to the other's\n   380\treflexive address from the same socket M observed. A path exists when an\n   381\tanswer, even `not-found`, carrying one of this peer's outstanding ids\n   382\tarrives from the expected address; the first inbound datagram alone confirms\n   383\tnothing. That is the whole of STUN, as facts on a stream; no probe frame\n   384\texists.\n   385\t\n   386\t**Relay, as a convention.** A peer asks M, through `meet-requests`, for a\n   387\tpair; M creates two ring buffers, enters them in its table, and posts their\n   388\tremote descriptors on the board as a lease grant (section 6). The asking\n   389\tpeers attach a pair channel over them. M holds no authority, runs the same\n   390\tcode as every peer, interprets nothing it relays, and may be replaced by any\n   391\treachable peer. That two unreachable peers need M at all is a fact about the\n   392\tnetwork, not a role in this protocol.\n   393\t\n   394\t**Address exposure.** Reflexive addresses on a board are readable by every\n   395\treader of the board. This convention claims no private delivery.\n   396\t\n   397\t## 5. Conventions that are not protocol\n   398\t\n   399\tNone of these has a frame. Each is peers using sections 2 and 3.\n   400\t\n   401\t**The toy.** Peer B holds a handle `s` over the single value `\"hello\"`,\n   402\tidentity `\"str-1\"`, complete history, surface `#{:reader}`. B's table is\n   403\t`{\"str-1\" {:handle s :surface #{:reader}}}`. B accepts WebSocket connections\n   404\tas `dao.stream.ws.md` specifies and drives `mirror-step` over each\n   405\tconnection's deposit medium and socket handle. B publishes\n   406\t`{:dao.stream/type :dao.stream/remote :dao.stream/identity \"str-1\"\n   407\t:dao.stream/channel <B's ws descriptor>}`. Peer A calls `attach!`; the link\n   408\tdials and sends the `descriptor` probe; the answer carries `c0` and `c1`.\n   409\tA mints `cursor :oldest`, receiving `c0`. A calls `next c0`: no answer\n   410\tfiled, the link sends `{... :dao.stream.remote/op :dao.stream/next\n   411\t:dao.stream.remote/args [c0] :dao.stream.remote/budget 64\n   412\t:dao.stream.remote/id 2}` and answers `blocked`. B's mirror applies\n   413\t`(next s c0)`, `ok \"hello\" c1`, then `(next s c1)`, `end`, and answers\n   414\t`{:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok :dao.stream/value \"hello\"\n   415\t:dao.stream/cursor c1 :dao.stream.remote/more [{:dao.stream/outcome\n   416\t:dao.stream/end}] ...}`. A calls `next c0` again: the drain files the\n   417\tanswer and `ok \"hello\" c1` is returned; `next c1` answers `end`. Nothing\n   418\tknew it was a string.\n   419\t\n   420\t**Request and response service.** A peer S enters `requests` with surface\n   421\t`#{:writer}` and `answers` with surface `#{:reader}` in its table and runs\n   422\tan interpreter over the local ends. A peer P attaches reflections to both,\n   423\tmints `:newest` on `answers` first, appends `{:svc/request r ...}` through\n   424\t`requests`, and reads `answers` until it observes `:svc/request r`.\n   425\tRequest ids are self-minted random values so callers sharing one `answers`\n   426\tstream do not collide; a caller that needs private answers asks S for a\n   427\tpair. S is a \"server\" only in that its table has those entries and its\n   428\tinterpreter has that policy. Either peer may serve such a pair to the other\n   429\tover the same channel. This is Linda's request medium, and the shape\n   430\t`yin.repl` and content lookup take (section 8).\n   431\t\n   432\t**Meeting board and relay pair.** Section 4.\n   433\t\n   434\t**Peer names.** A name is a self-minted value of at least 128 random bits,\n   435\tor a self-certifying hash of a public key (`dao.stream.discovery.md`). It\n   436\tappears only inside convention payloads. Nothing authenticates it until\n   437\tauthentication exists.\n   438\t\n   439\t## 6. Lease integration\n   440\t\n   441\tA served stream is a network resource, so its lifetime beyond one call is\n   442\tgoverned by `dao.lease.md`. The judge is composed inside the peer that\n   443\tpossesses the resource, as that contract requires.\n   444\t\n   445\t+--------------------------------------------------------+---------+-------------------------------------------------------------+\n   446\t| Resource                                               | Lease?  | Holder; grantor and judge; subject                          |\n   447\t+========================================================+=========+=============================================================+\n   448\t| Table entry a peer serves by its own policy            | No      | The peer retires it when it likes.                          |\n   449\t+--------------------------------------------------------+---------+-------------------------------------------------------------+\n   450\t| Table entry served for a remote party (a stream a      | Yes     | The remote party; the holding peer; the identity.           |\n   451\t| migrated continuation still reads)                     |         |                                                             |\n   452\t+--------------------------------------------------------+---------+-------------------------------------------------------------+\n   453\t| Relay pair on a meeting peer                           | Yes     | The requesting peer; the meeting peer; the two identities.  |\n   454\t+--------------------------------------------------------+---------+-------------------------------------------------------------+\n   455\t| Link state, UDP reassembly buffers, channel sockets    | No      | Governed by close or by a bounded buffer.                   |\n   456\t+--------------------------------------------------------+---------+-------------------------------------------------------------+\n   457\t| NAT mappings and punch state                           | No      | Possessed by the NAT box, so no judge can exist; keep-alive |\n   458\t|                                                        |         | probes are the peer's own cadence.                          |\n   459\t+--------------------------------------------------------+---------+-------------------------------------------------------------+\n   460\t| Board postings                                         | No      | Records, never reclaimed; each carries the lease id of the  |\n   461\t|                                                        |         | registration or pair it belongs to, and a reader treats a   |\n   462\t|                                                        |         | posting whose lease has a `:lapsed` on the board as stale.  |\n   463\t+--------------------------------------------------------+---------+-------------------------------------------------------------+\n   464\t\n   465\t**Mechanism: no new wire shape.** A grantor enters two conventional streams\n   466\tin its table, `lease-proposals` with surface `#{:writer}` and\n   467\t`lease-grants` with surface `#{:reader}`, published as remote descriptors\n   468\tbeside its meeting descriptors. A holder appends a proposal through a\n   469\treflection on the first and reads its grant through a reflection on the\n   470\tsecond. The grant creates one more table entry, a ring buffer with surface\n   471\t`#{:writer}`, and names its identity as `:dao.lease/holder`: this is the\n   472\tholder's **renewal medium**, and whatever lands on it is attributed to the\n   473\tholder by the medium, which is the attribution resolver `dao.lease.md`\n   474\tnames as per-author media. The judge reads it with a local cursor. The\n   475\trenewal medium lives and dies with its lease; the reclaim procedure removes\n   476\tthe subject entries and the renewal entry together, idempotently, and\n   477\treports success. A table entry under lease carries `:dao.lease/lease L`.\n   478\t\n   479\t**Renewal.** `append!` of `{:dao.lease/event :dao.lease/renewal\n   480\t:dao.lease/lease L}` on the holder's reflection of its renewal medium. The\n   481\treflection's `ok` is outbound acceptance only; the holder advances its\n   482\trenewal bound only on the source's `ok` observed on the link's event\n   483\twriter. A holder with no event writer stops at its bound early, never late.\n   484\t\n   485\t**Reclaim observed.** The mirror answers `not-found` for a reclaimed\n   486\tidentity and the reflection marks itself gone (2.4). The holder may also\n   487\tread the grantor's own `:lapsed` record through its reflection on\n   488\t`lease-grants`: that is observing the grantor's stream, not carriage. And\n   489\tthe holder stops at its own bound regardless.\n   490\t\n   491\t**Expiry versus channel loss.** Channel loss is the absence of answers; the\n   492\ttable entry persists, so a holder that reconnects within duration plus\n   493\ttolerance finds its entry, its cursors and its lease intact. Expiry is a\n   494\tpresent answer, `not-found`, on a healthy channel. Only expiry frees\n   495\tanything. A meeting peer that restarts loses its table and ledger, so every\n   496\tpair is gone, holders observe `not-found` and re-propose; this is\n   497\t`dao.lease.md`'s reclaim-and-regrant with an empty inventory, correct\n   498\tbecause the table is process-scoped.\n   499\t\n   500\tUnits are the composition's; these conventions fix `{:ms 1 :s 1000}`. Each\n   501\tside reads its own tick stream, and cross-host rate skew is what the judge's\n   502\ttolerance covers.\n   503\t\n   504\t## 7. Middleware and the ShiBi seam\n   505\t\n   506\tThe mechanism is `dao.stream.middleware.md`: a middleware is a map of two\n   507\tpure transforms over the operation map and the outcome map; `wrap` turns a\n   508\thandle and a chain into a handle on the same logical stream; a middleware\n   509\tmaps position `p` of the inner to position `p` of the outer and never\n   510\ttouches a cursor, an anchor, an identity, an id or an outcome kind. This\n   511\tdocument adds the network attachment points.\n   512\t\n   513\t+------------------+--------------------------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------+\n   514\t| Attachment point | What is wrapped                                  | Typical use                                          |\n   515\t+==================+==================================================+======================================================+\n   516\t| table entry      | The served handle, before it enters the table.   | Gating, value encryption for serving, redaction,     |\n   517\t|                  | The mirror sees only the wrapped handle.         | metering.                                            |\n   518\t+------------------+--------------------------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------+\n   519\t| reflection       | The reflection, inside the host-composed attach  | Presenting a credential, value decryption,           |\n   520\t|                  | closure.                                         | metering.                                            |\n   520\t|                  | closure.                                         | metering.                                            |\n   521\t+------------------+--------------------------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------+\n   522\t| channel          | The channel's writer and reader at each end.     | Authenticated encryption of whole requests and       |\n   523\t|                  |                                                  | answers, so a relay sees opaque values.              |\n   524\t+------------------+--------------------------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------+\n   525\t\n   526\tKeys and chains are composition data handed to `wrap`, never descriptor\n   527\tfields. Value-level encryption hides values and leaves identity, ops,\n   528\tcursors, sizes and timing visible; channel-level encryption hides all but\n   529\tsizes, timing and which pair talks. Key distribution is a convention and is\n   530\tnot specified here. Ciphers come from libraries.\n   531\t\n   532\t**The seam.** Authentication and authorization belong to ShiBi (`shibi.md`),\n   533\tunspecified. This document offers only:\n   534\t\n   535\t- **The slot.** A reserved open key on the request map,\n   536\t  `:dao.stream.remote/credential`, whose value is opaque here and travels\n   537\t  per request. A per-channel credential is a convention: the reflection's\n   538\t  `present` middleware attaches the same value to every request.\n   539\t- **The context.** The mirror hands `apply-request` the context\n   540\t  `{:dao.stream.remote/channel <attachment identity>}`; local callers hand\n   541\t  `{}`. No other context key exists in this version.\n   542\t- **The gate**, mirror-side: `dao.stream.middleware/gate`, whose `verify`\n   543\t  is a pure function of folded state, context and request, whose `fold` is\n   544\t  a pure function over facts drained from explicit source streams, and\n   545\t  whose refusal is the contract's `:dao.stream/refused` with an opaque\n   546\t  `:dao.stream.middleware/reason`.\n   547\t- **The presenter**, reflection-side: `dao.stream.middleware/present`, a\n   548\t  pure function of context and request that fills the slot.\n   549\t\n   550\tA ShiBi built as index and query interpreters over a tuple space plugs in\n   551\twithout changing this document: its index interpreter is the gate's `fold`\n   552\tover the streams it observes, its query interpreter is the gate's `verify`,\n   553\tits tokens are the slot's values, and its decisions, where it meters, are\n   554\tthe gate's decision emissions onto a stream it also indexes. The seam\n   555\tforecloses none of attenuation, offline verification, revocation, caveats on\n   556\top, identity or time as tick readings, issuance by any peer, or migration\n   557\tcredentials; a budget spent across peers waits on `dao.stream.discovery.md`'s\n   558\tcapability-versus-currency decision, which this document does not depend\n   559\ton. Until ShiBi exists, gating is an allow-list keyed on\n   560\t`:dao.stream.remote/channel` or a pre-shared value in the slot.\n   561\t\n   562\tLease attribution (section 6) rests on per-author media alone and needs no\n   563\tcredential; a gate on a renewal medium is an addition.\n   564\t\n   565\t## 8. Unification\n   566\t\n   567\tEvery existing network path, verified against the tree.\n   568\t\n   569\t+----------------------------------------------------+------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   570\t| Existing                                           | Fate             | Why                                                                  |\n   571\t+====================================================+==================+======================================================================+\n   572\t| `dao.stream.ws` and the jvm, node, browser, dart   | subsumed         | Already a channel: writer plus deposit medium. Its accept and        |\n   573\t| adapters                                           |                  | disclaim frames and served-path table become unnecessary once        |\n   574\t|                                                    |                  | `not-found` is an answer per identity; retiring them is a slice.     |\n   575\t+----------------------------------------------------+------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   576\t| `dao.stream.serving`                               | retired          | Forwards a source into every accepted socket and flattens a source    |\n   577\t|                                                    |                  | `gap` into a detach: the copy model. Its one consumer is             |\n   578\t|                                                    |                  | `yin.repl.serve`.                                                    |\n   579\t+----------------------------------------------------+------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   580\t| `dao.stream.forward`                               | unrelated        | A local copy step; replication by convention, not exposure.          |\n   581\t+----------------------------------------------------+------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   582\t| `dao.stream.rpc`                                   | convention-over  | Correlation by id over two streams is section 5's service; its       |\n   583\t|                                                    |                  | state machine may be kept as one such vocabulary over reflections.   |\n   584\t+----------------------------------------------------+------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   585\t| `dao.stream.rpc.ws`                                | retired          | Its only job is translating `:ws/` envelopes for rpc; a reflection    |\n   586\t|                                                    |                  | is the channel binding now.                                          |\n   587\t+----------------------------------------------------+------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   588\t| `dao.stream.apply` as a wire envelope              | retired          | Its ok-or-error wrapper duplicates the outcome map. The              |\n   589\t|                                                    |                  | `:dao.stream.apply/call` AST node and the VM's FFI bridge keep the    |\n   590\t|                                                    |                  | name and are unrelated to the network.                               |\n   591\t+----------------------------------------------------+------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   592\t| `yin.repl.serve`, `connect`, `driver`, `adapter`,  | convention-over  | An eval service: `requests` and `answers` streams, `:op/eval` as     |\n   593\t| the `daostream:ws://` URL                          |                  | payload; the URL becomes a remote descriptor for that pair. The      |\n   594\t|                                                    |                  | shared-shell privilege is its interpreter's policy.                  |\n   595\t+----------------------------------------------------+------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   596\t| `dao.jing.remote`, `remote.step`, `remote.async`,  | retired          | Deprecated whole; replaced below.                                    |\n   597\t| the `:dao.jing/remote` coordinate in               |                  |                                                                      |\n   598\t| `dao.jing.coordinate`                              |                  |                                                                      |\n   599\t+----------------------------------------------------+------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   600\t| `yin.repl.link`, `yin.vm.linker` M3 and M4         | convention-over  | The content pair is a request and response service over reflections  |\n   601\t|                                                    |                  | or local ring buffers; the linker's stepped core is unchanged.       |\n   602\t+----------------------------------------------------+------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   603\t| `dao.data.btree.storage/hydrate-async`,            | convention-over  | Consume the content client of this section; the callback facade is   |\n   604\t| `store-tree-async`                                 |                  | host policy, per `dao.stream.md` OD-5.                               |\n   605\t+----------------------------------------------------+------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   606\t| `dao.jing.dht.node` UDP DHT                        | unrelated        | Raw JVM datagram RPC with its own Kademlia envelope; its socket       |\n   607\t|                                                    |                  | rules and 1200-byte budget are the UDP channel's template.           |\n   608\t+----------------------------------------------------+------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   609\t| `dao.stream.transit`, `dao.stream.cbor`            | subsumed         | The channel codecs, unchanged.                                       |\n   610\t+----------------------------------------------------+------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   611\t\n   612\t**What replaces `dao.jing.remote`.** Content lookup is a request and response\n   613\tservice over remote streams, in one new module `dao.jing.content`:\n   614\t\n   615\t- **Vocabulary**, a payload convention: `{:jing/request r :jing/get\n   616\t  address}` answered by `{:jing/request r :jing/found? b :jing/bytes b64}`;\n   617\t  `{:jing/request r :jing/put address :jing/bytes b64}` answered by\n   618\t  `{:jing/request r :jing/result :inserted | :present}`. `r` is a\n   619\t  self-minted random value. Bytes travel as padded standard Base64 text, as\n   620\t  today.\n   621\t- **The interpreter** (`dao.jing.content/serve-step`): a pure step over a\n   622\t  local `requests` reader and `answers` writer against a `dao.jing` handle.\n   623\t  It runs the one ingress canonicality check on every put.\n   624\t- **The ingress check**, one shared function `dao.jing/accept-bytes!`,\n   625\t  moved from `dao.jing.remote/accept-bytes!` and used by the interpreter,\n   626\t  by the stepped client on every `get` answer and `:present` verify, and\n   627\t  by `yin.vm.linker`. The three copies become one.\n   628\t- **The stepped client** (`dao.jing.content.step`): the `request-put`,\n   629\t  `request-get`, `request-materialize`, `step`, `abandon` shape of\n   630\t  `dao.jing.remote.step`, over a `requests` writer and an `answers` reader\n   631\t  that are reflections or local handles. This is the portable interface,\n   632\t  per `dao.stream.md` OD-5.\n   633\t- **The blocking driver** (`dao.jing.content.driver`, clj only): host\n   634\t  policy that steps the client and sleeps, returning a synchronous\n   635\t  `dao.jing` handle, for `dao.space.index`, `dao.data.btree.storage` and\n   636\t  `yin.vm.content` on the JVM. It replaces `connect-content!`.\n   637\t- **The async facade** (`dao.jing.content.async`): replaces\n   638\t  `dao.jing.remote.async` over the new stepped client, same consumer API.\n   639\t- **The coordinate**: `{:dao.jing/type :dao.jing/remote :dao.jing/requests\n   640\t  <remote descriptor> :dao.jing/answers <remote descriptor>}` on every\n   641\t  host, resolved by `dao.jing.coordinate/open!` to the driver on clj and to\n   642\t  the stepped client elsewhere. The `:url` form is gone.\n   643\t\n   644\t## 9. Completion criteria and implementation slices\n   645\t\n   646\tOrdered. Each names files, host lanes and its proof. Verification commands\n   647\tare in `docs/agents/build-n-test.md`.\n   648\t\n   649\t+----+------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+---------------+\n   650\t| #  | Slice and files                                            | Proves                                                 | Lanes         |\n   651\t+====+============================================================+========================================================+===============+\n   652\t| 0  | Contract amendments: `docs/design/dao.stream.md` OD-1,     | Existing consumers pass; `dao.stream.observe/step` and | clj cljs cljd |\n   653\t|    | OD-2, OD-3 accepted; `dao.stream.observe`, `yin.vm.engine` | the engine treat an unrecognized outcome as refused,   |               |\n   654\t|    | follow the unrecognized-outcome rule.                      | not a throw.                                           |               |\n   655\t+----+------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+---------------+\n   656\t| 1  | `dao.stream.middleware`: `apply-request`, `wrap`, `gate`,  | Position rule under a value cipher over a ring buffer; | clj cljs cljd |\n   657\t|    | `present`, a metering exemplar. `src/cljc/dao/stream/      | a gate with the channel allow-list refuses with        |               |\n   658\t|    | middleware.cljc`, tests.                                   | `:dao.stream/refused`; a filter cannot be expressed.   |               |\n   659\t+----+------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+---------------+\n   660\t| 2  | `dao.stream.remote` core: `mirror-step`, link, reflection, | The toy over two in-process ring buffers as the        | clj cljs cljd |\n   661\t|    | remote descriptor dispatch. `src/cljc/dao/stream/          | channel; `gap` from an evicting source crosses         |               |\n   662\t|    | remote.cljc`, tests.                                       | verbatim with the source's cursor; `blocked` then      |               |\n   663\t|    |                                                            | `ok` on re-ask; `not-found` marks gone; kept cursor    |               |\n   664\t|    |                                                            | from a reflection is accepted by the source handle.    |               |\n   665\t+----+------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+---------------+\n   666\t| 3  | WebSocket channel composition: mirror steps over accepted  | The toy across a socket on clj to Node, Node to clj,   | clj cljs cljd |\n   667\t|    | and dialed attachments; retire `:ws/accept`, disclaim and  | cljd to clj; both directions serve on one channel;     |               |\n   668\t|    | the served-path table in `dao.stream.ws`.                  | browser dials and reads.                               |               |\n   669\t+----+------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+---------------+\n   670\t| 4  | Content service: `dao.jing.content` with `serve-step`,     | `dao.jing.remote` tests ported and passing over the    | clj cljs cljd |\n   671\t|    | `step`, `driver` (clj), `async`; `dao.jing/accept-bytes!`; | new module; `dao.space.index`, btree hydration, the    |               |\n   672\t|    | `dao.jing.coordinate` new coordinate; delete               | linker M3 and M4 tests unchanged in outcome; no        |               |\n   673\t|    | `src/cljc/dao/jing/remote.cljc`, `remote/step.cljc`,       | require of `dao.jing.remote` remains.                  |               |\n   674\t|    | `remote/async.cljc` and their tests.                       |                                                        |               |\n   675\t+----+------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+---------------+\n   676\t| 5  | REPL as a service: `yin.repl.serve` and `connect` over     | `(connect \"daostream:ws://...\")` resolves to a remote   | clj cljs cljd |\n   677\t|    | `requests` and `answers` reflections; delete               | descriptor pair; the demo REPLs work; `yin.repl.link`  |               |\n   678\t|    | `dao.stream.serving`, `dao.stream.rpc.ws`, the apply wire  | serves content through slice 4.                        |               |\n   679\t|    | envelope from `dao.stream.apply`.                          |                                                        |               |\n   680\t+----+------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+---------------+\n   681\t| 6  | UDP channel: `dao.stream.udp` with fragmentation;          | The toy over UDP with a 100 KiB value; loss injection  | clj cljs cljd |\n   682\t|    | adapters `src/clj/dao/stream/udp/jvm.clj`, `src/cljs/dao/  | recovers by resend; oversize beyond the maximum is     |               |\n   683\t|    | stream/udp/node.cljs`, `src/cljd/dao/stream/udp/dart.cljd`.| `transport-error` with `:dao.stream.remote/oversize`.  |               |\n   684\t+----+------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+---------------+\n   685\t| 7  | Pair channel and the meeting and relay conventions with    | Two peers behind a simulated restricted NAT punch;     | clj           |\n   686\t|    | leases: `dao.stream.remote.pair`, `dao.stream.remote.meet`.| behind a simulated symmetric NAT they relay; a pair    |               |\n   687\t|    |                                                            | whose holder stops renewing answers `not-found` after  |               |\n   688\t|    |                                                            | duration plus tolerance; a reconnect within it finds   |               |\n   689\t|    |                                                            | the pair intact.                                       |               |\n   690\t+----+------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+---------------+\n   691\t| 8  | UCF facade: `yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md`      | The M4 acceptance row: a string-backed stream migrates | clj cljs cljd |\n   692\t|    | 7.4.3 and 7.5.3 lift and lower through remote descriptors  | with a kept cursor and resumes; forced eviction yields |               |\n   693\t|    | with the `:dao.stream.remote/v1` cursor profile.           | `gap` with the source's cursor.                        |               |\n   694\t+----+------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+---------------+\n   695\t\n   696\tDeferred, in order of expected arrival: authentication and authorization\n   697\t(ShiBi), key distribution, a WebRTC data channel, browser inbound. None\n   698\tchanges a shape in section 2.\n   699\t\n   700\t## 10. Contract amendments and companion edits\n   701\t\n   702\tIn `dao.stream.md`: OD-1 accepted with the write fallback \"effect unknown; no\n   703\tautomatic retry\" and the `:dao.stream/retry?` key; OD-2 accepted; OD-3\n   704\tdecision 2 accepted and (a)'s endpoint sentence in Envelopes; one\n   705\t`:dao.stream/refused` row on `cursor`, `next` and `append!`; one sentence\n   706\tin Surfaces on a handle composed over another handle. Nothing network.\n   707\t\n   708\tIn `dao.lease.md`: served entries and relay pairs named as subjects a lease\n   709\tmay govern; the carriage note gains this document's `not-found`.\n   710\t\n   711\tIn `dao.stream.ws.md`: the stream belongs to the peer that holds it;\n   712\tserving copies is the transport's, attaching to the original is this\n   713\tdocument's; dialer-side and acceptor-side; deferred liveness, resumption\n   714\tand RPC items point here.\n   715\t\n   716\tIn `yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md`: 7.4.3 and 7.5.3 cite this\n   717\tdocument for the facade; the put resume rule waits on the event writer's\n   718\tsource outcome; `:yin.k/cursor-profiles` names `:dao.stream.remote/v1`; the\n   719\tacceptance row cites section 9 slice 8.\n   720\t\n   721\tIn `dao.jing.md`, `yin.vm.linker.md`, `dao.data.btree.md`, `dao.jing.dht.md`\n   722\tand `daostream-udp-design.md`: the sentences naming `dao.jing.remote` as\n   723\tthe remote path, the linker's content pair \"over `dao.jing.remote.step`\",\n   724\tthe btree remote row, the DHT's NAT limitation, and the v1 UDP design's\n   725\tstatus now cite this document.\n     1\t# DaoStream Middleware\n     2\t\n     3\tStatus: design target, not implemented. Subordinate to\n     4\t[`dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md), which wins on any disagreement. Every\n     5\tsentence below is a rule. This document names no network; the network\n     6\tattachment points are in [`dao.stream.remote.md`](./dao.stream.remote.md),\n     7\tsection 7.\n     8\t\n     9\t## What a middleware is\n    10\t\n    11\tA middleware is a value: a map of two pure transforms, one over the\n    12\toperation map on the way in and one over the outcome map on the way out. It\n    13\tis the adapter shape of `datom.world.md` (a map of transform functions)\n    14\tapplied to a handle rather than to a host boundary. Ring's request and\n    15\tresponse pair maps onto the operation map and the contract's outcome map.\n    16\t\n    17\t```clojure\n    18\t{:dao.stream.middleware/in  (fn [ctx req] -> req | outcome-map)\n    19\t :dao.stream.middleware/out (fn [ctx req outcome] -> outcome)}\n    20\t```\n    21\t\n    22\t`in` returning an outcome map short-circuits: the inner handle is not\n    23\tconsulted and that map is the operation's outcome, after the `out`\n    24\ttransforms of the middlewares outside it. `ctx` is a plain map the caller\n    25\tsupplies and no middleware may fabricate; a local caller supplies `{}`.\n    26\t\n    27\t## The operation map\n    28\t\n    29\tAn operation as data. Its vocabulary is `dao.stream.remote.md`'s so that the\n    30\tsame chain runs on either side of a channel without translation:\n    31\t\n    32\t```clojure\n    33\t{:dao.stream.remote/op    :dao.stream/cursor | :dao.stream/next | :dao.stream/append!\n    34\t :dao.stream.remote/args  [anchor] | [cursor] | [value]\n    35\t ...}                     ; open: further qualified keys pass through untouched\n    36\t```\n    37\t\n    38\t`descriptor` and `close!` are not operations a middleware sees: `descriptor`\n    39\tis a name and `close!` is a lifecycle transition, and neither has an outcome\n    40\ta policy may decline (`dao.stream.md`, Close; Envelopes).\n    41\t\n    42\t`(apply-request h ctx req) -> outcome` applies one operation map to a\n    43\thandle. It is the one place an operation map becomes a protocol call.\n    44\t\n    45\t## wrap\n    46\t\n    47\t`(wrap h chain) -> handle`, where `chain` is a vector of middlewares\n    48\tapplied outermost first. The returned handle implements the protocols the\n    49\tinner handle implements and no others. On each protocol call it builds the\n    50\toperation map, runs every `in` from the outermost inward, applies the result\n    51\tto `h` with `apply-request`, and runs every `out` from the innermost\n    52\toutward. Its `descriptor` and `close!` delegate to `h` unchanged.\n    53\t\n    54\tThe wrapped handle is a handle on the same logical stream as `h`: it\n    55\tdeclares `h`'s identity, passes cursors through, and may present values\n    56\tunder its own interpretation (`dao.stream.md`, Surfaces).\n    57\t\n    58\t## The position rule\n    59\t\n    60\tA middleware maps position `p` of the inner handle to position `p` of the\n    61\touter handle, for every `p`. It therefore never:\n    62\t\n    63\t- constructs, parses or rewrites a cursor, an anchor or a `gap` recovery\n    64\t  cursor;\n    65\t- changes `:dao.stream/outcome`, `:dao.stream/identity`,\n    66\t  `:dao.stream.remote/op` or `:dao.stream.remote/id`;\n    67\t- skips, merges or reorders elements.\n    68\t\n    69\tIt may replace `(first args)` on `append!` and `:dao.stream/value` on an\n    70\t`ok` from `next` with any value, of any size; add or read open keys; and\n    71\tshort-circuit with an outcome from the operation's exhaustive set. A cipher,\n    72\ta compressor, a redactor and an annotator preserve the rule. A filter that\n    73\tdrops elements does not: it is an interpreter that forwards into a new\n    74\tstream with its own identity (`dao.stream.md`, Composition), never a\n    75\tmiddleware.\n    76\t\n    77\t## The four prohibitions\n    78\t\n    79\tA transform is total and returns at once. It never loops over the inner\n    80\thandle, never retries, never waits, and never invokes any handle operation\n    81\tother than one `append!` to a side stream it was composed with. A transform\n    82\tthat needs facts from a stream drains that stream with its own cursor inside\n    83\tthe call, as `gate` below does, and reads no host clock.\n    84\t\n    85\t## Failure inside a transform\n    86\t\n    87\tA value that cannot be transformed on the way out (undecodable ciphertext,\n    88\ta corrupt compressed value) is presented as\n    89\t`{:dao.stream.middleware/undecodable true :dao.stream.middleware/raw v}`\n    90\tunder `:dao.stream/value`, with the outcome and cursor untouched: the\n    91\tposition is real and the reader decides. A value that cannot be transformed\n    92\ton the way in answers `:dao.stream/invalid-value`.\n    93\t\n    94\t## Two generic middlewares\n    95\t\n    96\t**gate**, the authorize step. Composition data only; the policy is not\n    97\tspecified here.\n    98\t\n    99\t```clojure\n   100\t(gate {:dao.stream.middleware/verify  (fn [state ctx req] -> nil | reason)  ; pure; nil allows\n   101\t       :dao.stream.middleware/fold    (fn [state fact] -> state)             ; pure\n   102\t       :dao.stream.middleware/sources [{:handle h :cursor c} ...]            ; explicit fact streams\n   103\t       :dao.stream.middleware/state   s0})\n   104\t```\n   105\t\n   106\tIts `in` drains every source to `blocked` with the gate's own cursors,\n   107\tfolds each fact into its state, then calls `verify`. `nil` passes the\n   108\trequest through; a reason short-circuits with\n   109\t`{:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/refused :dao.stream.middleware/reason\n   110\treason}`. A source that answers `gap` is folded as the fact\n   111\t`{:dao.stream.middleware/gap true}` so a policy may fail closed. A gate that\n   112\tmust account for its own decisions appends each as\n   113\t`{:dao.stream.middleware/decision :allow | :refuse ...}` to a side stream\n   114\tit was composed with and that is also one of its sources: this is\n   115\t**decision emission**, and it is how metered use keeps its state on a\n   116\tstream rather than in the gate.\n   117\t\n   118\t**present**, the credential step.\n   119\t\n   120\t```clojure\n   121\t(present {:dao.stream.middleware/present (fn [ctx req] -> credential)})\n   122\t```\n   123\t\n   124\tIts `in` associates the result under `:dao.stream.remote/credential`. Its\n   125\t`out` is identity.\n   126\t\n   127\tNeither middleware knows what a credential is. The trivial policy this\n   128\trepository ships with is an allow-list keyed on `:dao.stream.remote/channel`\n   129\tin `ctx`, ignoring the credential. Everything else belongs to\n   130\t[`shibi.md`](./shibi.md).\n   131\t\n   132\t## Exemplars\n   133\t\n   134\tValue encryption: `in` on `:dao.stream/append!` replaces the value with its\n   135\tauthenticated ciphertext; `out` on an `ok` from `:dao.stream/next` replaces\n   136\t`:dao.stream/value` with the plaintext or the undecodable marker. Cursors\n   137\tuntouched. The key is an argument to the constructor that built the\n   138\tmiddleware, exactly as a deposit medium is an argument to a transport\n   139\tconstructor. Ciphers come from libraries.\n   140\t\n   141\tMetering: `out` appends `{:meter/identity id :meter/op op :meter/outcome\n   142\tkind}` to a composed metering stream and returns the outcome unchanged. The\n   143\tside effect is a stream emission (`datom.world.md`, Streams).\n   144\t\n   145\t## Invariant compliance\n   146\t\n   147\tNo callback: a transform is called downward by the caller in the caller's\n   148\tcontrol flow and returns a value; nothing is registered to be invoked\n   149\tlater. No implicit control flow: the four prohibitions. No hidden global\n   150\tstate: keys, policies and sources are arguments to `wrap`. No shared\n   151\tmutable state beyond what any handle holds. Interpretation and execution\n   152\tstay separate: `apply-request` judges no value. No assumed graph: a chain is\n   153\tan explicit vector.\n     1\t# ShiBi\n     2\t\n     3\tStatus: stub. ShiBi is not specified and not implemented\n     4\t(`docs/bootstrap.md`, Capability Engine). This page records only what other\n     5\tdocuments may rely on and what ShiBi must supply. It designs nothing.\n     6\t\n     7\t## What it is\n     8\t\n     9\tShiBi is the capability system of datom.world: authentication and\n    10\tauthorization are its job and no other document's. It is to be built the\n    11\tway `dao.space` is built, as a tuple space that emerges from two\n    12\tinterpreters over ordinary streams, one that indexes and one that queries,\n    13\tneither knowing the other exists (`datom.world.md`, Streams). Its tokens are\n    14\tdescribed elsewhere as Macaroon-style: attenuatable, offline-verifiable,\n    15\trevocable caveats (`dao.space.security.md`, Capabilities Govern\n    16\tInterpreters).\n    17\t\n    18\t## How it plugs in\n    19\t\n    20\tShiBi integrates through the middleware seam of\n    21\t[`dao.stream.middleware.md`](./dao.stream.middleware.md) and the attachment\n    22\tpoints of [`dao.stream.remote.md`](./dao.stream.remote.md) section 7, which\n    23\tare fixed and which ShiBi must not change. To plug in it supplies:\n    24\t\n    25\t- a `verify`, a pure function of folded state, context and request that\n    26\t  answers nil or a reason: its query interpreter;\n    27\t- a `fold` and the source streams it observes: its index interpreter;\n    28\t- a `present`, a pure function of context and request that produces the\n    29\t  value carried in `:dao.stream.remote/credential`;\n    30\t- a reason vocabulary under its own namespace for\n    31\t  `:dao.stream.middleware/reason`;\n    32\t- where use is metered, the decision facts it emits and indexes.\n    33\t\n    34\tThe contract's refusal outcome is `:dao.stream/refused` and is\n    35\tcapability-free (`dao.stream.md`, Result Convention). Lease attribution\n    36\trests on per-author media and does not depend on ShiBi\n    37\t(`dao.stream.remote.md`, section 6; `yin.repl.link-policy.md`).\n    38\t\n    39\t## What blocks it being load-bearing\n    40\t\n    41\t`dao.stream.discovery.md`, The ShiBi precondition: ShiBi is a capability\n    42\ttoken in one place and a fungible currency in another, and a currency needs\n    43\tdouble-spend resolution, which is global consensus. Until that decision is\n    44\tmade, no design may depend on ShiBi for anything beyond the seam above, and\n    45\tgating in this repository is an allow-list or a pre-shared value.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"git diff HEAD -- docs/design/dao.stream.md; rg -n 'dao.stream/refused|retry\\\\?|unknown outcome|SURFACES|middleware|hold|serve|remote|peer|relay|NAT|symmetric|CGNAT|browser|lease|key|credential|gate|no-surface|not-found|reclaim|truth' docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md docs/design/dao.stream.middleware.md docs/design/shibi.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"git diff HEAD -- docs/design/dao.stream.md; rg -n 'dao.stream/refused|retry\\\\?|unknown outcome|SURFACES|middleware|hold|serve|remote|peer|relay|NAT|symmetric|CGNAT|browser|lease|key|credential|gate|no-surface|not-found|reclaim|truth' docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md docs/design/dao.stream.middleware.md docs/design/shibi.md\"","aggregated_output":"diff --git a/docs/design/dao.stream.md b/docs/design/dao.stream.md\nindex a5e26986..4daa2637 100644\n--- a/docs/design/dao.stream.md\n+++ b/docs/design/dao.stream.md\n@@ -110,6 +110,20 @@ Every DaoStream operation returns data.\n   outcome, and a consumer must ignore keys it does not understand. This is\n   what allows future extensions (for example readiness notification) to be\n   added without breaking existing consumers.\n+- Each outcome set is exhaustive **for this version of the contract**. A\n+  consumer that receives an outcome it does not recognize treats it as: not\n+  `ok`; nothing observed; for a write, effect unknown and no automatic\n+  retry; and not retryable unless the result map carries\n+  `:dao.stream/retry? true`. It does not throw, because an unrecognized\n+  outcome is a newer contract, not a defect in this host's assembly. A\n+  transport may carry `:dao.stream/retry? true` on any non-`ok` outcome to\n+  say that asking again is the right response.\n+- `:dao.stream/refused` is an outcome `cursor`, `next` and `append!` may\n+  produce: a policy composed on this handle declined the operation; nothing\n+  was observed and nothing was appended; not retryable unless the map\n+  carries `:dao.stream/retry? true`. `descriptor` and `close!` never produce\n+  it: a name is not gated and a lifecycle transition is not declined. What\n+  the policy is, and how it is composed, is outside this contract.\n - Exceptions are reserved for defects in the host's own assembly of\n   DaoStream, detected before any operation runs. Everything observable at an\n   operation returns data: closed, full, blocked, end, gap, not found,\n@@ -262,9 +276,13 @@ Its properties are settled:\n   key.\n - It carries the logical-stream identity under the contract-owned\n   `:dao.stream/identity` key. Different descriptors may reach\n-  the same logical stream through different endpoints; their reachability data\n+  the same logical stream through different endpoints of the same owning\n+  transport; their reachability data\n   may differ, but their logical-stream identity is structurally equal. The same\n-  descriptor attaches to the same stream every time.\n+  descriptor attaches to the same stream every time. A logical stream is the\n+  sequence one transport instance owns: a copy on another host or medium is a\n+  different logical stream with its own identity, and that it mirrors the\n+  first is a fact for an interpreter to record.\n - It is self-contained. If the stream is remote, it contains whatever is\n   needed to connect — perhaps an IP address and port. The entry data is\n   transport-owned; a handler that cannot make sense of it returns\n@@ -418,6 +436,12 @@ than on a transport it detected. Such an interpreter is composed for a\n particular medium and says so; it is not contract-generic code, and nothing\n contract-generic may be built on it.\n \n+A handle may be composed over another handle on the same logical stream: it\n+declares that stream's identity, implements no surface the inner handle\n+lacks, passes cursors through unchanged, and may present values under its\n+own interpretation. Such a handle is host-composed, like a transport\n+constructor's closures, and is otherwise an ordinary handle.\n+\n The reader surface is a promise: the handle presents its elements as\n one positioned, append-only, retained sequence that cursors can observe and\n re-observe. A transport whose medium retains nothing cannot make that\n@@ -492,7 +516,8 @@ A cursor is an immutable value owned by the interpreter that holds it.\n   cannot fail this way excludes the outcome like any other (see Surfaces).\n   `cursor`'s outcome set is therefore `:dao.stream/ok` (with\n   `:dao.stream/cursor`), `:dao.stream/invalid-anchor`,\n-  `:dao.stream/closed`, and `:dao.stream/transport-error`.\n+  `:dao.stream/closed`, `:dao.stream/refused`, and\n+  `:dao.stream/transport-error`.\n - A composition that intends to observe events caused by an operation mints\n   its `:dao.stream/newest` cursor **before** invoking that operation. Minting\n   after `attach!`, endpoint bind, handoff installation, or any other operation\n@@ -520,9 +545,11 @@ A cursor is an immutable value owned by the interpreter that holds it.\n   cursor's representation belongs to the transport.\n - A cursor binds to the logical stream, not to a handle: any handle on the\n   same logical stream accepts it, so a cursor handed to another interpreter\n-  works with that interpreter's own handle. Whether a cursor survives\n-  serialization to another host is transport-owned and TBD, like the\n-  descriptor envelope.\n+  works with that interpreter's own handle. A cursor is plain data and\n+  survives the host serialization codec structurally unchanged, under the\n+  same rule as the envelopes. A cursor that outlives the stream it names\n+  yields `not-found` at `attach!` or `cursor-mismatch` at `next`, never a\n+  defect.\n - A cursor carries the logical-stream identity, not a reachability descriptor.\n   `cursor-mismatch` compares that identity, so handles reached through distinct\n   descriptors for the same logical stream accept the same cursor.\n@@ -547,6 +574,7 @@ answer.\n | `:dao.stream/gap`             | This position was evicted.                                                    | `:dao.stream/cursor` (earliest retained position, or the tail when nothing is retained) |\n | `:dao.stream/cursor-mismatch` | The cursor was minted by a different logical stream.                          | —                                                                                       |\n | `:dao.stream/invalid-cursor`  | The value is not a cursor.                                                    | —                                                                                       |\n+| `:dao.stream/refused`         | A policy composed on this handle declined the read; nothing was observed.     | —                                                                                       |\n | `:dao.stream/transport-error` | The transport failed to perform the read; nothing was observed.               | —                                                                                       |\n \n Reading never mutates the stream. Two readers with cursors at the same\n@@ -565,7 +593,8 @@ reader's progress every other reader's data loss.\n | `:dao.stream/full`            | The transport does not accept the value; nothing was appended. Whether this is transient or permanent is the transport's declared nature (see Retention and Gaps). | —             |\n | `:dao.stream/invalid-value`   | The transport cannot carry this value (for example, it cannot be encoded); nothing was appended.                                                     | —             |\n | `:dao.stream/closed`          | What this handle is on is closed to new appends; the value was not appended.                                                                         | —             |\n-| `:dao.stream/transport-error` | The transport failed to perform the append; nothing was appended.                                                                                    | —             |\n+| `:dao.stream/refused`         | A policy composed on this handle declined the append; nothing was appended.                                                                          | —             |\n+| `:dao.stream/transport-error` | The transport failed to perform the append. Whether the value was appended is unknown unless the transport declares that its failures are clean.     | —             |\n \n Acceptance is handle-relative, as close outcomes are. **Which sequence** a\n handle's writer surface is on is part of the transport's declared nature: for a\n@@ -576,7 +605,18 @@ not here. No remote transport can say more — under *no operation waits*, an\n answer from the far end cannot arrive before `append!` returns.\n \n The writer decides what to do about any non-`ok` outcome; DaoStream does not\n-retry, buffer, or notify on its behalf.\n+retry, buffer, or notify on its behalf. The three in-memory transports\n+(`ringbuffer`, `memory-log`, `ws`) declare clean failure: their\n+`transport-error` means nothing was appended.\n+\n+**Deduplication and correlation are the payload's.** A writer that must\n+retry safely puts an identity in the value; a reader that must not act twice\n+deduplicates on it. The stream never promises exactly-once, and a\n+`transport-error` whose effect is unknown is never retried automatically.\n+\n+A transport whose acceptance is not its final answer (durability reached,\n+far-end refusal) declares the channel on which that answer is deposited, as\n+*Close* already requires for a failing flush.\n \n ## Close\n \n@@ -802,149 +842,12 @@ replaced by one sentence in *Explicitly Absent* recording why. Drafted\n 2026-09-19 from a review of this document against `dao.stream`, its three\n transports (`ringbuffer`, `memory-log`, `ws`), and their consumers.\n \n-OD-1 to OD-4 are ordered by cost of delay. Each is a paragraph today, while\n-three transports and a handful of consumers exist, and a migration once a\n-durable or replicated transport ships. OD-5 was added afterwards and is\n-numbered by arrival; it concerns the layers above this contract rather than the\n-contract's own operations.\n-\n-### OD-1. An unrecognized outcome has no defined meaning\n-\n-**The assumption.** The Result Convention makes each operation's outcome set\n-exhaustive and makes result *maps* open. The two rules are asymmetric: a future\n-extension may add a key, never an outcome. The contract assumes the seven\n-outcome sets are complete for every transport that will ever exist.\n-\n-**Evidence.** Consumers are already total over the closed sets and treat\n-anything else as a fault: `dao.stream.observe/step` classifies an unrecognized\n-read or effect outcome as `transport-error` and reports `:defect` or `:failed`;\n-`yin.vm.engine/handle-put` and `handle-next` throw. *Envelopes* declines to\n-reserve an outcome for gating and says gating \"arrives as an addition\", but\n-under the exhaustive rule a new outcome is not an addition.\n-\n-**What breaks.** Any condition the present outcomes cannot express honestly:\n-attachment or append refused for lack of authority; a descriptor that is valid\n-but served elsewhere now; a transport that is throttling rather than full; an\n-append whose effect is unknown (OD-2). Each must either be squeezed into an\n-existing outcome that misdescribes it (`not-found` for unauthorized, `full` for\n-throttled) or break every consumer.\n-\n-**Proposed resolution.** Keep each outcome set exhaustive *for this version of\n-the contract* and add one rule to the Result Convention that makes later\n-outcomes safe to introduce:\n-\n-> A consumer that receives an outcome it does not recognize treats it as: not\n-> `ok`; nothing observed and nothing appended; and not retryable, unless the\n-> result map carries `:dao.stream/retry? true`. It does not throw, because an\n-> unrecognized outcome is a newer contract, not a defect in this host's\n-> assembly.\n-\n-With that rule a newer transport degrades to \"refused, do not retry\" on an\n-older consumer instead of to an exception, and `:dao.stream/retry?` lets it\n-say when waiting is the right response. The existing `full` and `blocked` keep\n-their meanings and need not carry the key.\n-\n-**Cost if deferred.** Every consumer written in the meantime hard-codes\n-\"unknown means defect\", and the first new outcome is a coordinated change\n-across all of them.\n-\n-### OD-2. `append!` assumes its own effect is always knowable\n-\n-**The assumption.** *Writing* defines `transport-error` as \"the transport\n-failed to perform the append; nothing was appended\", and `ok` as acceptance at\n-a definite position. Both assume the transport knows, at return time, whether\n-the value is in the sequence.\n-\n-**Evidence.** True of all three transports today: `ringbuffer` and `memory-log`\n-are single in-memory state transitions, and `ws` sidesteps the question because\n-its writer surface is the outbound path, so `ok` claims only enqueueing. No\n-transport yet has a medium that can fail *after* accepting bytes.\n-\n-**What breaks.** A durable file whose write succeeds and whose sync fails; a\n-log replicated to a quorum; an append forwarded to a remote sequencer. Each has\n-a third state, *unknown*, that the table cannot express. A consumer written\n-against the present text retries on `transport-error`, which is correct only if\n-nothing was appended, and duplicates the value when something was.\n-\n-Two related gaps compound it. `append!`'s `ok` carries no correlation value, so\n-where the real answer is displaced to another channel (*Writing*: \"what becomes\n-of the value at the far end is reported there, not here\") nothing in the\n-contract ties that answer to the append that caused it. `attach!` has\n-`:dao.stream/attachment` for exactly this; `append!` has no counterpart. And\n-`ok` promises neither readability nor delivery nor durability, with no named\n-way to learn any of the three later.\n-\n-**Proposed resolution.** Three sentences, no new operation:\n-\n-1. Reword `transport-error` on `append!`: \"The transport failed to perform the\n-   append. Whether the value was appended is unknown unless the transport\n-   declares that its failures are clean.\" The three existing transports declare\n-   clean failure, so nothing changes for them.\n-2. State the rule the RPC layer already follows: **deduplication and\n-   correlation are the payload's.** A writer that must retry safely puts an\n-   identity in the value; a reader that must not act twice deduplicates on it.\n-   The stream never promises exactly-once.\n-3. Name where later answers land: a transport whose acceptance is not its\n-   final answer (durability reached, far-end refusal) declares the channel on\n-   which that answer is deposited, as *Close* already requires for a failing\n-   flush.\n-\n-**Cost if deferred.** The first durable transport either lies (reports clean\n-failure it cannot guarantee) or changes the meaning of an outcome that every\n-retry loop already depends on.\n-\n-### OD-3. A cursor binds to the logical stream but is shaped by one transport\n-\n-**The assumption.** *Cursors* promises that \"any handle on the same logical\n-stream accepts it\", and *Envelopes* allows that \"different descriptors may\n-reach the same logical stream through different endpoints\". *Cursors* also says\n-\"a cursor's representation belongs to the transport\". Together these hold only\n-if a logical stream lives on exactly one transport for its whole life.\n-\n-**Evidence.** Cursor maps are keyed in the minting transport's namespace\n-(`:dao.stream.ringbuffer/identity` and `/position`,\n-`:dao.stream.memory-log/identity` and `/position`), so a cursor from one\n-transport is `invalid-cursor` on any other, whatever identity it names. Across\n-hosts the promise has never been exercised: `ws` has no reader surface, so\n-inbound values are deposited on a local stream with its own identity and its\n-own positions. What a remote host reads is a copy, not the stream. Finally,\n-\"whether a cursor survives serialization to another host is transport-owned and\n-TBD\", while *Explicitly Absent* removes `seek`: a kept cursor is therefore the\n-only checkpoint a consumer has, and whether it can be written down is\n-undecided.\n-\n-**What breaks.** Replication and DHT replicas (which replica's positions are\n-the stream's?); re-homing a stream from memory to a durable medium without\n-invalidating every kept cursor; a durable consumer resuming after restart;\n-comparing two replicas to detect a forked history.\n-\n-**Proposed resolution.** Two decisions, the first a choice:\n-\n-1. **Choose what a logical identity ranges over.** Either\n-   (a) *one origin, one transport*: a logical stream is the sequence one\n-   transport instance owns. A copy on another host or medium is a different\n-   logical stream with its own identity; that it mirrors the first is a fact for an\n-   interpreter to record. This is how the code already behaves:\n-   `dao.stream.forward` copies into a destination with its own identity. The\n-   sentence\n-   about different descriptors reaching one stream narrows to \"different\n-   endpoints of the same owning transport\". Or\n-   (b) *contract-level position*: the contract defines a position within an\n-   identity (an ordinal), cursors carry it under a `:dao.stream/…` key, and any\n-   transport serving that identity honors it.\n-   Recommended: (a). It matches Axiom 2 (sameness between sequences is an\n-   interpretation), costs a paragraph, and leaves (b) available later as an\n-   additive key. (b) commits every future transport, including ones with no\n-   natural ordinal, now.\n-2. **Settle serialization.** Replace \"TBD\" with: \"A cursor is plain data and\n-   survives the host serialization codec structurally unchanged, under the same\n-   rule as the envelopes. A cursor that outlives the stream it names yields\n-   `not-found` at `attach!` or `cursor-mismatch` at `next`, never a defect.\"\n-   Both existing transports already satisfy this.\n-\n-**Cost if deferred.** The first durable or replicated transport decides both\n-questions implicitly, by whatever its cursor happens to look like, and every\n-consumer that persisted a cursor inherits that accident.\n+OD-1, OD-2 and OD-3 were accepted on 2026-09-27 and their text moved into\n+*Result Convention*, *Writing*, *Envelopes* and *Cursors*; the numbers are\n+not reused. OD-4 is a paragraph today, while three transports and a handful\n+of consumers exist, and a migration once a durable or replicated transport\n+ships. OD-5 was added afterwards and is numbered by arrival; it concerns the\n+layers above this contract rather than the contract's own operations.\n \n ### OD-4. A logical stream can close but cannot end\n \ndocs/design/shibi.md:20:ShiBi integrates through the middleware seam of\ndocs/design/shibi.md:21:[`dao.stream.middleware.md`](./dao.stream.middleware.md) and the attachment\ndocs/design/shibi.md:22:points of [`dao.stream.remote.md`](./dao.stream.remote.md) section 7, which\ndocs/design/shibi.md:27:- a `fold` and the source streams it observes: its index interpreter;\ndocs/design/shibi.md:29:  value carried in `:dao.stream.remote/credential`;\ndocs/design/shibi.md:31:  `:dao.stream.middleware/reason`;\ndocs/design/shibi.md:34:The contract's refusal outcome is `:dao.stream/refused` and is\ndocs/design/shibi.md:37:(`dao.stream.remote.md`, section 6; `yin.repl.link-policy.md`).\ndocs/design/dao.stream.middleware.md:6:attachment points are in [`dao.stream.remote.md`](./dao.stream.remote.md),\ndocs/design/dao.stream.middleware.md:9:## What a middleware is\ndocs/design/dao.stream.middleware.md:11:A middleware is a value: a map of two pure transforms, one over the\ndocs/design/dao.stream.middleware.md:18:{:dao.stream.middleware/in  (fn [ctx req] -> req | outcome-map)\ndocs/design/dao.stream.middleware.md:19: :dao.stream.middleware/out (fn [ctx req outcome] -> outcome)}\ndocs/design/dao.stream.middleware.md:24:transforms of the middlewares outside it. `ctx` is a plain map the caller\ndocs/design/dao.stream.middleware.md:25:supplies and no middleware may fabricate; a local caller supplies `{}`.\ndocs/design/dao.stream.middleware.md:29:An operation as data. Its vocabulary is `dao.stream.remote.md`'s so that the\ndoc