{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md | sed -n '280,565p;565,645p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.middleware.md | sed -n '9,153p'; nl -ba docs/design/shibi.md\"","aggregated_output":"   280\t(`dao.stream.transit`, `dao.stream.cbor`). Ordered and reliable; frame\n   281\tbudget unbounded.\n   282\t\n   283\tDirection is establishment, not authority. A WebSocket has a dialer and an\n   284\tacceptor because TCP does. Once established the channel is symmetric and\n   285\tnothing in this document remembers who dialed. Hosts: clj, cljs on Node and\n   286\tcljd may dial or accept (`src/clj/dao/stream/ws/jvm.clj`,\n   287\t`src/cljs/dao/stream/ws/node.cljs`, `src/cljd/dao/stream/ws/dart.cljd`);\n   288\tcljs in a browser may only dial (`src/cljs/dao/stream/ws/browser.cljs`).\n   289\t\n   290\t### 3.2 UDP\n   291\t\n   292\t`dao.stream.udp` is a new channel with the WebSocket deposit model: a socket\n   293\tretains nothing, so it has no reader surface, and its adapter deposits every\n   294\tdatagram as an event carrying the datagram's source address and its decoded\n   295\tvalue onto the medium the composition wired.\n   296\t\n   297\t- Descriptor: `{:dao.stream/type :dao.stream/udp :dao.stream/identity\n   298\t  <channel identity> :dao.stream.udp/host h :dao.stream.udp/port p}`.\n   299\t  `attach!` binds or reuses a local socket and returns a handle whose writer\n   300\t  path is datagrams to that address.\n   301\t- One CBOR value per datagram, canonical profile. Datagram budget 1200\n   302\t  bytes, the figure `dao.jing.dht.node` already uses to clear common path\n   303\t  MTUs.\n   304\t- **Fragmentation is channel-internal.** An encoded message over budget is\n   305\t  split into `k` datagrams, each\n   306\t  `{:dao.stream.remote/id n :dao.stream.udp/part i :dao.stream.udp/parts k\n   307\t  :dao.stream.udp/bytes b}`, where `n` is the carried message's own request\n   308\t  id. The receiver reassembles by `[source-address direction id]` and\n   309\t  delivers only a complete message. Loss of any part is loss of the whole\n   310\t  message, recovered by the link's resend rule. Reassembly is bounded by\n   311\t  two composition data, `:dao.stream.udp/max-message-bytes` (default 64\n   312\t  KiB) and `:dao.stream.udp/max-partial-messages`, evicting the oldest\n   313\t  partial message; no clock. Neither the mirror step nor the link sees a\n   314\t  fragment.\n   315\t- Replies go to the datagram's source address, never to an address claimed\n   316\t  in a payload.\n   317\t- A send may name an explicit destination from an already-bound socket. Hole\n   318\t  punching depends on it (section 4).\n   319\t- Unordered and best-effort. Attachment identity is the remote host and port\n   320\t  as the socket saw them; it is the reflexive address a meeting peer\n   321\t  republishes.\n   322\t\n   323\tHosts: clj (`DatagramSocket`), cljs on Node (`dgram`), cljd\n   324\t(`RawDatagramSocket`). Browsers have no UDP.\n   325\t\n   326\t### 3.3 Pair\n   327\t\n   328\tA pair descriptor names two remote descriptors:\n   329\t\n   330\t```clojure\n   331\t{:dao.stream/type      :dao.stream/pair\n   332\t :dao.stream/identity  <channel identity>\n   333\t :dao.stream.remote/in  <remote descriptor>    ; this peer reads here\n   334\t :dao.stream.remote/out <remote descriptor>}   ; this peer writes here\n   335\t```\n   336\t\n   337\t`attach!` on it returns a channel whose reader is a reflection of `in` and\n   338\twhose writer is a reflection of `out`. When a third peer serves both\n   339\tstreams from its table, two peers that cannot reach each other have a\n   340\tchannel, and the third peer runs nothing but mirror steps over two ring\n   341\tbuffers; the requests and answers inside are values in its streams it never\n   342\tinterprets. Streams are values sent through streams. Ordered; best-effort\n   343\tunder the inboxes' retention. A `gap` observed on `in` is channel loss,\n   344\tabsorbed by the resend rule, and is never reported to an inner reader as\n   345\tthe source's `gap`. Double framing consumes budget: an inner value inside an\n   346\touter `append!` must fit the outer channel's budget with both envelopes.\n   347\t\n   348\t## 4. Reachability and NAT\n   349\t\n   350\tA channel needs one end that can receive an unsolicited packet, or a\n   351\tcoordinated simultaneous send. The protocol cannot change that; it makes\n   352\tthe workarounds conventions.\n   353\t\n   354\t+------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   355\t| Situation                                      | Outcome                                                                    |\n   356\t+================================================+============================================================================+\n   357\t| One side reachable (public address, forwarded  | Direct: the other side dials, WebSocket or UDP.                            |\n   358\t| port, or full-cone NAT)                        |                                                                            |\n   359\t+------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   360\t| Restricted or port-restricted NAT on one or    | Hole punch through a meeting peer (below).                                 |\n   361\t| both sides, UDP allowed                        |                                                                            |\n   362\t+------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   363\t| Symmetric NAT on both sides, or carrier-grade  | Punching is unreliable. Relay through a pair served by a reachable peer.   |\n   364\t| NAT                                            |                                                                            |\n   365\t+------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   366\t| UDP blocked                                    | WebSocket only; the browser rule applies.                                  |\n   367\t+------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   368\t| Browser at one end                             | It dials. Two browsers relay through a pair. A WebRTC data channel is a    |\n   369\t|                                                | future channel type whose candidates ride the same meeting stream.        |\n   370\t+------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   371\t\n   372\t**Hole punching, as a convention.** A meeting peer M serves a\n   373\t`meet-requests` stream with surface `#{:writer}` and a `meet-board` stream\n   374\twith surface `#{:reader}`, both ordinary table entries. Over a UDP channel\n   375\ta peer appends `{:meet/here <name>}`; M's interpreter reads the request and\n   376\tthe channel attachment identity it arrived on, which for UDP is the\n   377\treflexive address, and appends `{:meet/seen <name> :meet/reflexive {:host h\n   378\t:port p} :dao.lease/lease L}` to the board. Both parties of a call read the\n   379\tboard, then each sends a `descriptor` request with a fresh id to the other's\n   380\treflexive address from the same socket M observed. A path exists when an\n   381\tanswer, even `not-found`, carrying one of this peer's outstanding ids\n   382\tarrives from the expected address; the first inbound datagram alone confirms\n   383\tnothing. That is the whole of STUN, as facts on a stream; no probe frame\n   384\texists.\n   385\t\n   386\t**Relay, as a convention.** A peer asks M, through `meet-requests`, for a\n   387\tpair; M creates two ring buffers, enters them in its table, and posts their\n   388\tremote descriptors on the board as a lease grant (section 6). The asking\n   389\tpeers attach a pair channel over them. M holds no authority, runs the same\n   390\tcode as every peer, interprets nothing it relays, and may be replaced by any\n   391\treachable peer. That two unreachable peers need M at all is a fact about the\n   392\tnetwork, not a role in this protocol.\n   393\t\n   394\t**Address exposure.** Reflexive addresses on a board are readable by every\n   395\treader of the board. This convention claims no private delivery.\n   396\t\n   397\t## 5. Conventions that are not protocol\n   398\t\n   399\tNone of these has a frame. Each is peers using sections 2 and 3.\n   400\t\n   401\t**The toy.** Peer B holds a handle `s` over the single value `\"hello\"`,\n   402\tidentity `\"str-1\"`, complete history, surface `#{:reader}`. B's table is\n   403\t`{\"str-1\" {:handle s :surface #{:reader}}}`. B accepts WebSocket connections\n   404\tas `dao.stream.ws.md` specifies and drives `mirror-step` over each\n   405\tconnection's deposit medium and socket handle. B publishes\n   406\t`{:dao.stream/type :dao.stream/remote :dao.stream/identity \"str-1\"\n   407\t:dao.stream/channel <B's ws descriptor>}`. Peer A calls `attach!`; the link\n   408\tdials and sends the `descriptor` probe; the answer carries `c0` and `c1`.\n   409\tA mints `cursor :oldest`, receiving `c0`. A calls `next c0`: no answer\n   410\tfiled, the link sends `{... :dao.stream.remote/op :dao.stream/next\n   411\t:dao.stream.remote/args [c0] :dao.stream.remote/budget 64\n   412\t:dao.stream.remote/id 2}` and answers `blocked`. B's mirror applies\n   413\t`(next s c0)`, `ok \"hello\" c1`, then `(next s c1)`, `end`, and answers\n   414\t`{:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok :dao.stream/value \"hello\"\n   415\t:dao.stream/cursor c1 :dao.stream.remote/more [{:dao.stream/outcome\n   416\t:dao.stream/end}] ...}`. A calls `next c0` again: the drain files the\n   417\tanswer and `ok \"hello\" c1` is returned; `next c1` answers `end`. Nothing\n   418\tknew it was a string.\n   419\t\n   420\t**Request and response service.** A peer S enters `requests` with surface\n   421\t`#{:writer}` and `answers` with surface `#{:reader}` in its table and runs\n   422\tan interpreter over the local ends. A peer P attaches reflections to both,\n   423\tmints `:newest` on `answers` first, appends `{:svc/request r ...}` through\n   424\t`requests`, and reads `answers` until it observes `:svc/request r`.\n   425\tRequest ids are self-minted random values so callers sharing one `answers`\n   426\tstream do not collide; a caller that needs private answers asks S for a\n   427\tpair. S is a \"server\" only in that its table has those entries and its\n   428\tinterpreter has that policy. Either peer may serve such a pair to the other\n   429\tover the same channel. This is Linda's request medium, and the shape\n   430\t`yin.repl` and content lookup take (section 8).\n   431\t\n   432\t**Meeting board and relay pair.** Section 4.\n   433\t\n   434\t**Peer names.** A name is a self-minted value of at least 128 random bits,\n   435\tor a self-certifying hash of a public key (`dao.stream.discovery.md`). It\n   436\tappears only inside convention payloads. Nothing authenticates it until\n   437\tauthentication exists.\n   438\t\n   439\t## 6. Lease integration\n   440\t\n   441\tA served stream is a network resource, so its lifetime beyond one call is\n   442\tgoverned by `dao.lease.md`. The judge is composed inside the peer that\n   443\tpossesses the resource, as that contract requires.\n   444\t\n   445\t+--------------------------------------------------------+---------+-------------------------------------------------------------+\n   446\t| Resource                                               | Lease?  | Holder; grantor and judge; subject                          |\n   447\t+========================================================+=========+=============================================================+\n   448\t| Table entry a peer serves by its own policy            | No      | The peer retires it when it likes.                          |\n   449\t+--------------------------------------------------------+---------+-------------------------------------------------------------+\n   450\t| Table entry served for a remote party (a stream a      | Yes     | The remote party; the holding peer; the identity.           |\n   451\t| migrated continuation still reads)                     |         |                                                             |\n   452\t+--------------------------------------------------------+---------+-------------------------------------------------------------+\n   453\t| Relay pair on a meeting peer                           | Yes     | The requesting peer; the meeting peer; the two identities.  |\n   454\t+--------------------------------------------------------+---------+-------------------------------------------------------------+\n   455\t| Link state, UDP reassembly buffers, channel sockets    | No      | Governed by close or by a bounded buffer.                   |\n   456\t+--------------------------------------------------------+---------+-------------------------------------------------------------+\n   457\t| NAT mappings and punch state                           | No      | Possessed by the NAT box, so no judge can exist; keep-alive |\n   458\t|                                                        |         | probes are the peer's own cadence.                          |\n   459\t+--------------------------------------------------------+---------+-------------------------------------------------------------+\n   460\t| Board postings                                         | No      | Records, never reclaimed; each carries the lease id of the  |\n   461\t|                                                        |         | registration or pair it belongs to, and a reader treats a   |\n   462\t|                                                        |         | posting whose lease has a `:lapsed` on the board as stale.  |\n   463\t+--------------------------------------------------------+---------+-------------------------------------------------------------+\n   464\t\n   465\t**Mechanism: no new wire shape.** A grantor enters two conventional streams\n   466\tin its table, `lease-proposals` with surface `#{:writer}` and\n   467\t`lease-grants` with surface `#{:reader}`, published as remote descriptors\n   468\tbeside its meeting descriptors. A holder appends a proposal through a\n   469\treflection on the first and reads its grant through a reflection on the\n   470\tsecond. The grant creates one more table entry, a ring buffer with surface\n   471\t`#{:writer}`, and names its identity as `:dao.lease/holder`: this is the\n   472\tholder's **renewal medium**, and whatever lands on it is attributed to the\n   473\tholder by the medium, which is the attribution resolver `dao.lease.md`\n   474\tnames as per-author media. The judge reads it with a local cursor. The\n   475\trenewal medium lives and dies with its lease; the reclaim procedure removes\n   476\tthe subject entries and the renewal entry together, idempotently, and\n   477\treports success. A table entry under lease carries `:dao.lease/lease L`.\n   478\t\n   479\t**Renewal.** `append!` of `{:dao.lease/event :dao.lease/renewal\n   480\t:dao.lease/lease L}` on the holder's reflection of its renewal medium. The\n   481\treflection's `ok` is outbound acceptance only; the holder advances its\n   482\trenewal bound only on the source's `ok` observed on the link's event\n   483\twriter. A holder with no event writer stops at its bound early, never late.\n   484\t\n   485\t**Reclaim observed.** The mirror answers `not-found` for a reclaimed\n   486\tidentity and the reflection marks itself gone (2.4). The holder may also\n   487\tread the grantor's own `:lapsed` record through its reflection on\n   488\t`lease-grants`: that is observing the grantor's stream, not carriage. And\n   489\tthe holder stops at its own bound regardless.\n   490\t\n   491\t**Expiry versus channel loss.** Channel loss is the absence of answers; the\n   492\ttable entry persists, so a holder that reconnects within duration plus\n   493\ttolerance finds its entry, its cursors and its lease intact. Expiry is a\n   494\tpresent answer, `not-found`, on a healthy channel. Only expiry frees\n   495\tanything. A meeting peer that restarts loses its table and ledger, so every\n   496\tpair is gone, holders observe `not-found` and re-propose; this is\n   497\t`dao.lease.md`'s reclaim-and-regrant with an empty inventory, correct\n   498\tbecause the table is process-scoped.\n   499\t\n   500\tUnits are the composition's; these conventions fix `{:ms 1 :s 1000}`. Each\n   501\tside reads its own tick stream, and cross-host rate skew is what the judge's\n   502\ttolerance covers.\n   503\t\n   504\t## 7. Middleware and the ShiBi seam\n   505\t\n   506\tThe mechanism is `dao.stream.middleware.md`: a middleware is a map of two\n   507\tpure transforms over the operation map and the outcome map; `wrap` turns a\n   508\thandle and a chain into a handle on the same logical stream; a middleware\n   509\tmaps position `p` of the inner to position `p` of the outer and never\n   510\ttouches a cursor, an anchor, an identity, an id or an outcome kind. This\n   511\tdocument adds the network attachment points.\n   512\t\n   513\t+------------------+--------------------------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------+\n   514\t| Attachment point | What is wrapped                                  | Typical use                                          |\n   515\t+==================+==================================================+======================================================+\n   516\t| table entry      | The served handle, before it enters the table.   | Gating, value encryption for serving, redaction,     |\n   517\t|                  | The mirror sees only the wrapped handle.         | metering.                                            |\n   518\t+------------------+--------------------------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------+\n   519\t| reflection       | The reflection, inside the host-composed attach  | Presenting a credential, value decryption,           |\n   520\t|                  | closure.                                         | metering.                                            |\n   521\t+------------------+--------------------------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------+\n   522\t| channel          | The channel's writer and reader at each end.     | Authenticated encryption of whole requests and       |\n   523\t|                  |                                                  | answers, so a relay sees opaque values.              |\n   524\t+------------------+--------------------------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------+\n   525\t\n   526\tKeys and chains are composition data handed to `wrap`, never descriptor\n   527\tfields. Value-level encryption hides values and leaves identity, ops,\n   528\tcursors, sizes and timing visible; channel-level encryption hides all but\n   529\tsizes, timing and which pair talks. Key distribution is a convention and is\n   530\tnot specified here. Ciphers come from libraries.\n   531\t\n   532\t**The seam.** Authentication and authorization belong to ShiBi (`shibi.md`),\n   533\tunspecified. This document offers only:\n   534\t\n   535\t- **The slot.** A reserved open key on the request map,\n   536\t  `:dao.stream.remote/credential`, whose value is opaque here and travels\n   537\t  per request. A per-channel credential is a convention: the reflection's\n   538\t  `present` middleware attaches the same value to every request.\n   539\t- **The context.** The mirror hands `apply-request` the context\n   540\t  `{:dao.stream.remote/channel <attachment identity>}`; local callers hand\n   541\t  `{}`. No other context key exists in this version.\n   542\t- **The gate**, mirror-side: `dao.stream.middleware/gate`, whose `verify`\n   543\t  is a pure function of folded state, context and request, whose `fold` is\n   544\t  a pure function over facts drained from explicit source streams, and\n   545\t  whose refusal is the contract's `:dao.stream/refused` with an opaque\n   546\t  `:dao.stream.middleware/reason`.\n   547\t- **The presenter**, reflection-side: `dao.stream.middleware/present`, a\n   548\t  pure function of context and request that fills the slot.\n   549\t\n   550\tA ShiBi built as index and query interpreters over a tuple space plugs in\n   551\twithout changing this document: its index interpreter is the gate's `fold`\n   552\tover the streams it observes, its query interpreter is the gate's `verify`,\n   553\tits tokens are the slot's values, and its decisions, where it meters, are\n   554\tthe gate's decision emissions onto a stream it also indexes. The seam\n   555\tforecloses none of attenuation, offline verification, revocation, caveats on\n   556\top, identity or time as tick readings, issuance by any peer, or migration\n   557\tcredentials; a budget spent across peers waits on `dao.stream.discovery.md`'s\n   558\tcapability-versus-currency decision, which this document does not depend\n   559\ton. Until ShiBi exists, gating is an allow-list keyed on\n   560\t`:dao.stream.remote/channel` or a pre-shared value in the slot.\n   561\t\n   562\tLease attribution (section 6) rests on per-author media alone and needs no\n   563\tcredential; a gate on a renewal medium is an addition.\n   564\t\n   565\t## 8. Unification\n   565\t## 8. Unification\n   566\t\n   567\tEvery existing network path, verified against the tree.\n   568\t\n   569\t+----------------------------------------------------+------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   570\t| Existing                                           | Fate             | Why                                                                  |\n   571\t+====================================================+==================+======================================================================+\n   572\t| `dao.stream.ws` and the jvm, node, browser, dart   | subsumed         | Already a channel: writer plus deposit medium. Its accept and        |\n   573\t| adapters                                           |                  | disclaim frames and served-path table become unnecessary once        |\n   574\t|                                                    |                  | `not-found` is an answer per identity; retiring them is a slice.     |\n   575\t+----------------------------------------------------+------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   576\t| `dao.stream.serving`                               | retired          | Forwards a source into every accepted socket and flattens a source    |\n   577\t|                                                    |                  | `gap` into a detach: the copy model. Its one consumer is             |\n   578\t|                                                    |                  | `yin.repl.serve`.                                                    |\n   579\t+----------------------------------------------------+------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   580\t| `dao.stream.forward`                               | unrelated        | A local copy step; replication by convention, not exposure.          |\n   581\t+----------------------------------------------------+------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   582\t| `dao.stream.rpc`                                   | convention-over  | Correlation by id over two streams is section 5's service; its       |\n   583\t|                                                    |                  | state machine may be kept as one such vocabulary over reflections.   |\n   584\t+----------------------------------------------------+------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   585\t| `dao.stream.rpc.ws`                                | retired          | Its only job is translating `:ws/` envelopes for rpc; a reflection    |\n   586\t|                                                    |                  | is the channel binding now.                                          |\n   587\t+----------------------------------------------------+------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   588\t| `dao.stream.apply` as a wire envelope              | retired          | Its ok-or-error wrapper duplicates the outcome map. The              |\n   589\t|                                                    |                  | `:dao.stream.apply/call` AST node and the VM's FFI bridge keep the    |\n   590\t|                                                    |                  | name and are unrelated to the network.                               |\n   591\t+----------------------------------------------------+------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   592\t| `yin.repl.serve`, `connect`, `driver`, `adapter`,  | convention-over  | An eval service: `requests` and `answers` streams, `:op/eval` as     |\n   593\t| the `daostream:ws://` URL                          |                  | payload; the URL becomes a remote descriptor for that pair. The      |\n   594\t|                                                    |                  | shared-shell privilege is its interpreter's policy.                  |\n   595\t+----------------------------------------------------+------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   596\t| `dao.jing.remote`, `remote.step`, `remote.async`,  | retired          | Deprecated whole; replaced below.                                    |\n   597\t| the `:dao.jing/remote` coordinate in               |                  |                                                                      |\n   598\t| `dao.jing.coordinate`                              |                  |                                                                      |\n   599\t+----------------------------------------------------+------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   600\t| `yin.repl.link`, `yin.vm.linker` M3 and M4         | convention-over  | The content pair is a request and response service over reflections  |\n   601\t|                                                    |                  | or local ring buffers; the linker's stepped core is unchanged.       |\n   602\t+----------------------------------------------------+------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   603\t| `dao.data.btree.storage/hydrate-async`,            | convention-over  | Consume the content client of this section; the callback facade is   |\n   604\t| `store-tree-async`                                 |                  | host policy, per `dao.stream.md` OD-5.                               |\n   605\t+----------------------------------------------------+------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   606\t| `dao.jing.dht.node` UDP DHT                        | unrelated        | Raw JVM datagram RPC with its own Kademlia envelope; its socket       |\n   607\t|                                                    |                  | rules and 1200-byte budget are the UDP channel's template.           |\n   608\t+----------------------------------------------------+------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   609\t| `dao.stream.transit`, `dao.stream.cbor`            | subsumed         | The channel codecs, unchanged.                                       |\n   610\t+----------------------------------------------------+------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   611\t\n   612\t**What replaces `dao.jing.remote`.** Content lookup is a request and response\n   613\tservice over remote streams, in one new module `dao.jing.content`:\n   614\t\n   615\t- **Vocabulary**, a payload convention: `{:jing/request r :jing/get\n   616\t  address}` answered by `{:jing/request r :jing/found? b :jing/bytes b64}`;\n   617\t  `{:jing/request r :jing/put address :jing/bytes b64}` answered by\n   618\t  `{:jing/request r :jing/result :inserted | :present}`. `r` is a\n   619\t  self-minted random value. Bytes travel as padded standard Base64 text, as\n   620\t  today.\n   621\t- **The interpreter** (`dao.jing.content/serve-step`): a pure step over a\n   622\t  local `requests` reader and `answers` writer against a `dao.jing` handle.\n   623\t  It runs the one ingress canonicality check on every put.\n   624\t- **The ingress check**, one shared function `dao.jing/accept-bytes!`,\n   625\t  moved from `dao.jing.remote/accept-bytes!` and used by the interpreter,\n   626\t  by the stepped client on every `get` answer and `:present` verify, and\n   627\t  by `yin.vm.linker`. The three copies become one.\n   628\t- **The stepped client** (`dao.jing.content.step`): the `request-put`,\n   629\t  `request-get`, `request-materialize`, `step`, `abandon` shape of\n   630\t  `dao.jing.remote.step`, over a `requests` writer and an `answers` reader\n   631\t  that are reflections or local handles. This is the portable interface,\n   632\t  per `dao.stream.md` OD-5.\n   633\t- **The blocking driver** (`dao.jing.content.driver`, clj only): host\n   634\t  policy that steps the client and sleeps, returning a synchronous\n   635\t  `dao.jing` handle, for `dao.space.index`, `dao.data.btree.storage` and\n   636\t  `yin.vm.content` on the JVM. It replaces `connect-content!`.\n   637\t- **The async facade** (`dao.jing.content.async`): replaces\n   638\t  `dao.jing.remote.async` over the new stepped client, same consumer API.\n   639\t- **The coordinate**: `{:dao.jing/type :dao.jing/remote :dao.jing/requests\n   640\t  <remote descriptor> :dao.jing/answers <remote descriptor>}` on every\n   641\t  host, resolved by `dao.jing.coordinate/open!` to the driver on clj and to\n   642\t  the stepped client elsewhere. The `:url` form is gone.\n   643\t\n   644\t## 9. Completion criteria and implementation slices\n   645\t\n     9\t## What a middleware is\n    10\t\n    11\tA middleware is a value: a map of two pure transforms, one over the\n    12\toperation map on the way in and one over the outcome map on the way out. It\n    13\tis the adapter shape of `datom.world.md` (a map of transform functions)\n    14\tapplied to a handle rather than to a host boundary. Ring's request and\n    15\tresponse pair maps onto the operation map and the contract's outcome map.\n    16\t\n    17\t```clojure\n    18\t{:dao.stream.middleware/in  (fn [ctx req] -> req | outcome-map)\n    19\t :dao.stream.middleware/out (fn [ctx req outcome] -> outcome)}\n    20\t```\n    21\t\n    22\t`in` returning an outcome map short-circuits: the inner handle is not\n    23\tconsulted and that map is the operation's outcome, after the `out`\n    24\ttransforms of the middlewares outside it. `ctx` is a plain map the caller\n    25\tsupplies and no middleware may fabricate; a local caller supplies `{}`.\n    26\t\n    27\t## The operation map\n    28\t\n    29\tAn operation as data. Its vocabulary is `dao.stream.remote.md`'s so that the\n    30\tsame chain runs on either side of a channel without translation:\n    31\t\n    32\t```clojure\n    33\t{:dao.stream.remote/op    :dao.stream/cursor | :dao.stream/next | :dao.stream/append!\n    34\t :dao.stream.remote/args  [anchor] | [cursor] | [value]\n    35\t ...}                     ; open: further qualified keys pass through untouched\n    36\t```\n    37\t\n    38\t`descriptor` and `close!` are not operations a middleware sees: `descriptor`\n    39\tis a name and `close!` is a lifecycle transition, and neither has an outcome\n    40\ta policy may decline (`dao.stream.md`, Close; Envelopes).\n    41\t\n    42\t`(apply-request h ctx req) -> outcome` applies one operation map to a\n    43\thandle. It is the one place an operation map becomes a protocol call.\n    44\t\n    45\t## wrap\n    46\t\n    47\t`(wrap h chain) -> handle`, where `chain` is a vector of middlewares\n    48\tapplied outermost first. The returned handle implements the protocols the\n    49\tinner handle implements and no others. On each protocol call it builds the\n    50\toperation map, runs every `in` from the outermost inward, applies the result\n    51\tto `h` with `apply-request`, and runs every `out` from the innermost\n    52\toutward. Its `descriptor` and `close!` delegate to `h` unchanged.\n    53\t\n    54\tThe wrapped handle is a handle on the same logical stream as `h`: it\n    55\tdeclares `h`'s identity, passes cursors through, and may present values\n    56\tunder its own interpretation (`dao.stream.md`, Surfaces).\n    57\t\n    58\t## The position rule\n    59\t\n    60\tA middleware maps position `p` of the inner handle to position `p` of the\n    61\touter handle, for every `p`. It therefore never:\n    62\t\n    63\t- constructs, parses or rewrites a cursor, an anchor or a `gap` recovery\n    64\t  cursor;\n    65\t- changes `:dao.stream/outcome`, `:dao.stream/identity`,\n    66\t  `:dao.stream.remote/op` or `:dao.stream.remote/id`;\n    67\t- skips, merges or reorders elements.\n    68\t\n    69\tIt may replace `(first args)` on `append!` and `:dao.stream/value` on an\n    70\t`ok` from `next` with any value, of any size; add or read open keys; and\n    71\tshort-circuit with an outcome from the operation's exhaustive set. A cipher,\n    72\ta compressor, a redactor and an annotator preserve the rule. A filter that\n    73\tdrops elements does not: it is an interpreter that forwards into a new\n    74\tstream with its own identity (`dao.stream.md`, Composition), never a\n    75\tmiddleware.\n    76\t\n    77\t## The four prohibitions\n    78\t\n    79\tA transform is total and returns at once. It never loops over the inner\n    80\thandle, never retries, never waits, and never invokes any handle operation\n    81\tother than one `append!` to a side stream it was composed with. A transform\n    82\tthat needs facts from a stream drains that stream with its own cursor inside\n    83\tthe call, as `gate` below does, and reads no host clock.\n    84\t\n    85\t## Failure inside a transform\n    86\t\n    87\tA value that cannot be transformed on the way out (undecodable ciphertext,\n    88\ta corrupt compressed value) is presented as\n    89\t`{:dao.stream.middleware/undecodable true :dao.stream.middleware/raw v}`\n    90\tunder `:dao.stream/value`, with the outcome and cursor untouched: the\n    91\tposition is real and the reader decides. A value that cannot be transformed\n    92\ton the way in answers `:dao.stream/invalid-value`.\n    93\t\n    94\t## Two generic middlewares\n    95\t\n    96\t**gate**, the authorize step. Composition data only; the policy is not\n    97\tspecified here.\n    98\t\n    99\t```clojure\n   100\t(gate {:dao.stream.middleware/verify  (fn [state ctx req] -> nil | reason)  ; pure; nil allows\n   101\t       :dao.stream.middleware/fold    (fn [state fact] -> state)             ; pure\n   102\t       :dao.stream.middleware/sources [{:handle h :cursor c} ...]            ; explicit fact streams\n   103\t       :dao.stream.middleware/state   s0})\n   104\t```\n   105\t\n   106\tIts `in` drains every source to `blocked` with the gate's own cursors,\n   107\tfolds each fact into its state, then calls `verify`. `nil` passes the\n   108\trequest through; a reason short-circuits with\n   109\t`{:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/refused :dao.stream.middleware/reason\n   110\treason}`. A source that answers `gap` is folded as the fact\n   111\t`{:dao.stream.middleware/gap true}` so a policy may fail closed. A gate that\n   112\tmust account for its own decisions appends each as\n   113\t`{:dao.stream.middleware/decision :allow | :refuse ...}` to a side stream\n   114\tit was composed with and that is also one of its sources: this is\n   115\t**decision emission**, and it is how metered use keeps its state on a\n   116\tstream rather than in the gate.\n   117\t\n   118\t**present**, the credential step.\n   119\t\n   120\t```clojure\n   121\t(present {:dao.stream.middleware/present (fn [ctx req] -> credential)})\n   122\t```\n   123\t\n   124\tIts `in` associates the result under `:dao.stream.remote/credential`. Its\n   125\t`out` is identity.\n   126\t\n   127\tNeither middleware knows what a credential is. The trivial policy this\n   128\trepository ships with is an allow-list keyed on `:dao.stream.remote/channel`\n   129\tin `ctx`, ignoring the credential. Everything else belongs to\n   130\t[`shibi.md`](./shibi.md).\n   131\t\n   132\t## Exemplars\n   133\t\n   134\tValue encryption: `in` on `:dao.stream/append!` replaces the value with its\n   135\tauthenticated ciphertext; `out` on an `ok` from `:dao.stream/next` replaces\n   136\t`:dao.stream/value` with the plaintext or the undecodable marker. Cursors\n   137\tuntouched. The key is an argument to the constructor that built the\n   138\tmiddleware, exactly as a deposit medium is an argument to a transport\n   139\tconstructor. Ciphers come from libraries.\n   140\t\n   141\tMetering: `out` appends `{:meter/identity id :meter/op op :meter/outcome\n   142\tkind}` to a composed metering stream and returns the outcome unchanged. The\n   143\tside effect is a stream emission (`datom.world.md`, Streams).\n   144\t\n   145\t## Invariant compliance\n   146\t\n   147\tNo callback: a transform is called downward by the caller in the caller's\n   148\tcontrol flow and returns a value; nothing is registered to be invoked\n   149\tlater. No implicit control flow: the four prohibitions. No hidden global\n   150\tstate: keys, policies and sources are arguments to `wrap`. No shared\n   151\tmutable state beyond what any handle holds. Interpretation and execution\n   152\tstay separate: `apply-request` judges no value. No assumed graph: a chain is\n   153\tan explicit vector.\n     1\t# ShiBi\n     2\t\n     3\tStatus: stub. ShiBi is not specified and not implemented\n     4\t(`docs/bootstrap.md`, Capability Engine). This page records only what other\n     5\tdocuments may rely on and what ShiBi must supply. It designs nothing.\n     6\t\n     7\t## What it is\n     8\t\n     9\tShiBi is the capability system of datom.world: authentication and\n    10\tauthorization are its job and no other document's. It is to be built the\n    11\tway `dao.space` is built, as a tuple space that emerges from two\n    12\tinterpreters over ordinary streams, one that indexes and one that queries,\n    13\tneither knowing the other exists (`datom.world.md`, Streams). Its tokens are\n    14\tdescribed elsewhere as Macaroon-style: attenuatable, offline-verifiable,\n    15\trevocable caveats (`dao.space.security.md`, Capabilities Govern\n    16\tInterpreters).\n    17\t\n    18\t## How it plugs in\n    19\t\n    20\tShiBi integrates through the middleware seam of\n    21\t[`dao.stream.middleware.md`](./dao.stream.middleware.md) and the attachment\n    22\tpoints of [`dao.stream.remote.md`](./dao.stream.remote.md) section 7, which\n    23\tare fixed and which ShiBi must not change. To plug in it supplies:\n    24\t\n    25\t- a `verify`, a pure function of folded state, context and request that\n    26\t  answers nil or a reason: its query interpreter;\n    27\t- a `fold` and the source streams it observes: its index interpreter;\n    28\t- a `present`, a pure function of context and request that produces the\n    29\t  value carried in `:dao.stream.remote/credential`;\n    30\t- a reason vocabulary under its own namespace for\n    31\t  `:dao.stream.middleware/reason`;\n    32\t- where use is metered, the decision facts it emits and indexes.\n    33\t\n    34\tThe contract's refusal outcome is `:dao.stream/refused` and is\n    35\tcapability-free (`dao.stream.md`, Result Convention). Lease attribution\n    36\trests on per-author media and does not depend on ShiBi\n    37\t(`dao.stream.remote.md`, section 6; `yin.repl.link-policy.md`).\n    38\t\n    39\t## What blocks it being load-bearing\n    40\t\n    41\t`dao.stream.discovery.md`, The ShiBi precondition: ShiBi is a capability\n    42\ttoken in one place and a fungible currency in another, and a currency needs\n    43\tdouble-spend resolution, which is global consensus. Until that decision is\n    44\tmade, no design may depend on ShiBi for anything beyond the seam above, and\n    45\tgating in this repository is an allow-list or a pre-shared value.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
