/design/dao.jing.remote.implementation-plan.md:77:   `dao.stream.ws.jvm` is a `.clj` file with none, so cljd owes this plan\ndocs/design/dao.jing.remote.implementation-plan.md:79:   `#?@(:cljd [] :clj [[dao.stream.ws.jvm :as jvm]])` (`yin.repl.host:16`),\ndocs/design/dao.jing.remote.implementation-plan.md:95:   still requires `dao.stream.rpc.client` and `.ws` (`repl.cljc:12-13`) and is\ndocs/design/dao.jing.remote.implementation-plan.md:97:   refusing the twin shape for `dao.jing`. This plan migrates `dao.jing.remote`\ndocs/design/dao.jing.remote.implementation-plan.md:102:   (`:4-6`) name `dao.stream.rpc`; `docs/dao.space.stigmergy.md:9` and\ndocs/design/dao.jing.remote.implementation-plan.md:111:7. **`public/demo.html` does not reach `dao.jing.remote`.** Checked rather\ndocs/design/dao.jing.remote.implementation-plan.md:113:   `dao.space.query` → `dao.jing.coordinate`, whose `dao.jing.remote` require\ndocs/design/dao.jing.remote.implementation-plan.md:144:`dao.jing.remote` is a content-store handle that lives on the other side of a\ndocs/design/dao.jing.remote.implementation-plan.md:157:constructor** over `dao.stream.rpc`, plus a small portable step the JVM\ndocs/design/dao.jing.remote.implementation-plan.md:158:driver turns, with `content-client`, `default-handlers`, `dao.jing.coordinate`\ndocs/design/dao.jing.remote.implementation-plan.md:169:### 2.0 The JVM send seam — `dao.stream.ws.jvm`, Phase 0 (done)\ndocs/design/dao.jing.remote.implementation-plan.md:178:| J3 | A send that fails after acceptance is reported as data on the boundary's stream — `:ws/error` through the adapter's `:error!`, then the socket aborted, then the terminal `:ws/closed` through `:closed!` — never as a return value and never swallowed | `[D]` — `dao.stream.ws.md` *Deposited events*, `dao.stream.md` *Surfaces* (\"observably gone\") |\ndocs/design/dao.jing.remote.implementation-plan.md:199:| H5 | A handler that throws never escapes the server step: it becomes a `:dao.stream.apply/handler-error` response, and an unknown op an `/unknown-operation` response | `[D]` **new** — v2 `apply/dispatch-request`'s rule replaces v1 `rpc.server`'s |\ndocs/design/dao.jing.remote.implementation-plan.md:228:| N2 | `connect-content!` returns only after the attachment reports `/established`; a terminal lifecycle before that throws with the reason, and `:connect-timeout-ms` without either throws `{:url :timeout-ms}`. In **all three** failure cases — the terminal lifecycle, the deadline, and an interruption of the establishment loop's sleep — **before the throw**, `stream/close!` has run on the handle, which flips its phase to `:closed` so no handle, medium or state escapes to the caller and no later call could be made through it. The third was found in Phase 2's review: an interrupt propagating raw left an attached handle and its socket live while the caller held nothing, which is a failure of this clause, not of N11 (no id is allocated yet). The traffic cursor is minted **before** `attach!`, so `/established` cannot land ahead of the position that will observe it. **The limit, in this plan's own words**: on the JVM, `stream/close!` before the socket exists records a `:close-request` and does nothing else (`jvm.clj:168-173`); nothing touches the pending `buildAsync` future, and the `HttpClient` that `connect!` builds carries no connect or request timeout. So against a peer that accepts TCP and never completes the upgrade, the client-side JDK connection is **not torn down**, the peer **need not observe EOF at any time**, and the connection persists for the **process lifetime**. Each such failed open leaks one JDK connection, and `dao.jing.remote` has no handle through which to prevent it. This is the shipped transport's behaviour and this plan does not change it (§8) | `[T→D]` — `network-invalid-url-test` pins only validation (§5.2 #5); §5.2 #5 and #6 pin the throw and that nothing escapes; nothing pins teardown, because there is none |\ndocs/design/dao.jing.remote.implementation-plan.md:232:| N6 | A call with no completion within `:request-timeout-ms` throws `{:request-id id :timeout-ms ms}` and **retires its bookkeeping**: the id leaves `:outstanding`, and a still-`:unsent` envelope is abandoned with reason `:dao.jing.remote/timeout`. The allocator is untouched, so a late response for that id is classified `unsolicited-response` by `rpc/handle-event` (`rpc.cljc:363-364`) and dropped with the diagnostics; it can never be delivered to a later call | `[D]` **new** — r2 |\ndocs/design/dao.jing.remote.implementation-plan.md:233:| N7 | A payload outside the v2 portable domain (`transit/portable-value?`) is refused at the writer as `invalid-value`; the call throws with reason `:dao.stream/invalid-value` and nothing was sent | `[D]` **new** — v1 Transit custom handlers are gone; `dao.jing.file` already stores `pr-str` EDN, so every payload the tests carry is portable |\ndocs/design/dao.jing.remote.implementation-plan.md:250:| S5 | A handler result outside the portable domain is never dropped silently: it is replaced by a correlated `:dao.jing.remote/non-portable-result` error response, so the client throws N9's error rather than timing out. Any non-`ok` outcome from the response append retires the attachment (`stream/close!` on its socket handle), so the client observes N8's terminal loss rather than silence | `[D]` **new** — r2 |\ndocs/design/dao.jing.remote.implementation-plan.md:269:up through `dao.data.btree.storage`, `index/read-datoms`, `query/\ndocs/design/dao.jing.remote.implementation-plan.md:297:timeout could not bound that wait. This was a defect in `dao.stream.ws.jvm`\ndocs/design/dao.jing.remote.implementation-plan.md:298:against its own docstring and against `dao.stream.ws.md`'s `ok`, live for\ndocs/design/dao.jing.remote.implementation-plan.md:370:Repairing it is establishment-lifecycle work in `dao.stream.ws.jvm`, not\ndocs/design/dao.jing.remote.implementation-plan.md:397:                (:dao.stream.rpc/state (rpc/reque