{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"date -u '+%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S GMT'; rg --files docs/design | rg '(\"'^|/)(dao'\"\\\\.shibi|shibi|yin\\\\.repl\\\\.link-policy|yin\\\\.vm\\\\.link-policy)'; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/stream.cljc | sed -n '55,105p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.md | sed -n '385,410p;425,445p;480,505p;535,575p;635,652p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md | sed -n '90,155p;186,250p;270,300p;345,395p;500,565p;610,645p;690,725p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.middleware.md | sed -n '55,145p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.shibi.md | sed -n '1,50p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.jing.cbor.md | sed -n '25,38p;430,442p'; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/stream/ws.cljc | sed -n '195,220p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.ws.md | sed -n '65,84p;95,112p;135,160p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"date -u '+%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S GMT'; rg --files docs/design | rg '(\"'^|/)(dao'\"\\\\.shibi|shibi|yin\\\\.repl\\\\.link-policy|yin\\\\.vm\\\\.link-policy)'; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/stream.cljc | sed -n '55,105p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.md | sed -n '385,410p;425,445p;480,505p;535,575p;635,652p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md | sed -n '90,155p;186,250p;270,300p;345,395p;500,565p;610,645p;690,725p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.middleware.md | sed -n '55,145p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.shibi.md | sed -n '1,50p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.jing.cbor.md | sed -n '25,38p;430,442p'; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/stream/ws.cljc | sed -n '195,220p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.ws.md | sed -n '65,84p;95,112p;135,160p'\"","aggregated_output":"2026-09-26 18:29:06 GMT\ndocs/design/yin.repl.link-policy.md\ndocs/design/dao.shibi.md\n    55\t    :dao.stream/invalid-spec\n    56\t    :dao.stream/not-found\n    57\t    :dao.stream/transport-error})\n    58\t\n    59\t\n    60\t(def outcomes-attach\n    61\t  #{:dao.stream/ok\n    62\t    :dao.stream/invalid-descriptor\n    63\t    :dao.stream/not-found\n    64\t    :dao.stream/transport-error})\n    65\t\n    66\t\n    67\t(def outcomes-descriptor\n    68\t  #{:dao.stream/ok})\n    69\t\n    70\t\n    71\t(def outcomes-cursor\n    72\t  #{:dao.stream/ok\n    73\t    :dao.stream/invalid-anchor\n    74\t    :dao.stream/closed\n    75\t    :dao.stream/transport-error})\n    76\t\n    77\t\n    78\t(def outcomes-next\n    79\t  #{:dao.stream/ok\n    80\t    :dao.stream/blocked\n    81\t    :dao.stream/end\n    82\t    :dao.stream/gap\n    83\t    :dao.stream/cursor-mismatch\n    84\t    :dao.stream/invalid-cursor\n    85\t    :dao.stream/transport-error})\n    86\t\n    87\t\n    88\t(def outcomes-append\n    89\t  #{:dao.stream/ok\n    90\t    :dao.stream/full\n    91\t    :dao.stream/invalid-value\n    92\t    :dao.stream/closed\n    93\t    :dao.stream/transport-error})\n    94\t\n    95\t\n    96\t(def outcomes-close\n    97\t  #{:dao.stream/ok})\n    98\t\n    99\t\n   100\t(def operation-outcomes\n   101\t  \"Map of operation keyword to its closed outcome set.\"\n   102\t  {:create! outcomes-create\n   103\t   :attach! outcomes-attach\n   104\t   :descriptor outcomes-descriptor\n   105\t   :cursor outcomes-cursor\n   385\tone. A creation specification naming it finds no create implementation and\n   386\treturns `:dao.stream/not-found`, the same answer as an absent transport and\n   387\tthe correct one: nothing here creates that.\n   388\t\n   389\tEntry values are **host-composed**: ordinarily closures produced by a\n   390\ttransport constructor that already received whatever composition state the\n   391\ttransport needs — a boundary's deposit destination and its admission\n   392\tdeclaration, a directory some composition keeps. The bare vars above are\n   393\tschematic. A bare namespace var could reach that state only through namespace\n   394\tglobals, which is the hidden state this contract retires.\n   395\t\n   396\tThis table is ordinary data owned by the host, and the dispatch is an\n   397\tordinary map lookup done by the host — DaoStream neither performs nor\n   398\tstandardizes it. A host that cannot dispatch on `:dao.stream/type` returns\n   399\t`:dao.stream/not-found` — never creation or discovery.\n   400\t\n   401\tCode that knows its transport statically calls the transport's functions\n   402\tdirectly; no dispatch is involved. There is no ambient namespace-global\n   403\tregistry and no namespace-load registration side effect.\n   404\t\n   405\t## Surfaces\n   406\t\n   407\tA handle implements the subset of the public surface its transport can\n   408\thonor — **reader** (`cursor`, `next`), **writer** (`append!`), **closable**\n   409\t(`close!`) — and the transport declares which. The Cursors, Reading, and\n   410\tRetention and Gaps sections govern handles with a reader surface only.\n   425\tagainst this contract may not reach for one**: it must work on any transport\n   426\tdeclaring the surfaces it uses, and a transport-owned operation is by\n   427\tdefinition absent from some of them.\n   428\t\n   429\tAn interpreter may still use one, on the same terms as any other thing a\n   430\tcomposition supplies. What it may not do is *find* it — reach through a handle\n   431\tfor an operation the contract never promised, or dispatch on which transport\n   432\tit was given. It receives the operation as an ordinary argument from the\n   433\tcomposition that wired it, exactly as it receives the streams it reads and the\n   434\tpolicies it applies, and it then depends on a function it was handed rather\n   435\tthan on a transport it detected. Such an interpreter is composed for a\n   436\tparticular medium and says so; it is not contract-generic code, and nothing\n   437\tcontract-generic may be built on it.\n   438\t\n   439\tA handle may be composed over another handle on the same logical stream: it\n   440\tdeclares that stream's identity, implements no surface the inner handle\n   441\tlacks, passes cursors through unchanged, and may present values under its\n   442\town interpretation. Such a handle is host-composed, like a transport\n   443\tconstructor's closures, and is otherwise an ordinary handle.\n   444\t\n   445\tThe reader surface is a promise: the handle presents its elements as\n   480\t\n   481\tThe IO Model makes this contract non-blocking; hosts are asynchronous\n   482\twhether or not it is. A network message arrives when the runtime says so, in\n   483\ta callback, on a thread or event loop DaoStream does not own. DaoStream does\n   484\tnot pretend otherwise; it confines the inversion to one place. The host's\n   485\tboundary adapter *is* callback code, and its entire job is to `append!` what\n   486\tarrived. Past that append the event holds a position in a sequence, and\n   487\tevery reader above observes it with its own cursor, in its own control flow,\n   488\twhen it chooses.\n   489\t\n   490\tThis is an un-inversion. The real cost of callback IO is not performance but\n   491\tthat the inversion spreads through everything it touches; depositing into a\n   492\tretained sequence pays that cost once, at the boundary, and returns ordinary\n   493\tcode to everyone above. The reader-surface rule above is the same thing\n   494\tstated structurally: a transport that retains nothing has no reader surface,\n   495\tand its inbound events are deposited onto a stream that does.\n   496\t\n   497\t## Cursors\n   498\t\n   499\tA cursor is an immutable value owned by the interpreter that holds it.\n   500\t\n   501\t- Cursors are minted by the stream: `cursor` on a handle takes an **anchor**\n   502\t  and returns `:dao.stream/ok` with `:dao.stream/cursor`. The contract\n   503\t  defines two anchors — `:dao.stream/oldest`, the earliest retained\n   504\t  position, and `:dao.stream/newest`, positioned after the newest appended\n   505\t  value so the first `next` observes the next value to arrive. A transport\n   535\t  the first append — and keeps it, exactly as a composition that intends to\n   536\t  observe events caused by an operation mints `:dao.stream/newest` before\n   537\t  invoking it. A kept origin cursor converts a silent loss into a reported\n   538\t  `gap`. It cannot be minted afterwards and cannot be reconstructed: a consumer\n   539\t  that arrives later holds no evidence about what preceded it. On a transport\n   540\t  that declares complete retention this is unnecessary — a fresh `:oldest` is\n   541\t  the origin there, however late the consumer arrives.\n   542\t- Every valid cursor comes from the stream: minted by `cursor`, received as\n   543\t  a successor from `next`, or recovered from a gap outcome. Consumers never\n   544\t  construct cursor internals or fabricate positions — there is no `seek`. A\n   545\t  cursor's representation belongs to the transport.\n   546\t- A cursor binds to the logical stream, not to a handle: any handle on the\n   547\t  same logical stream accepts it, so a cursor handed to another interpreter\n   548\t  works with that interpreter's own handle. A cursor is plain data and\n   549\t  survives the host serialization codec structurally unchanged, under the\n   550\t  same rule as the envelopes. A cursor that outlives the stream it names\n   551\t  yields `not-found` at `attach!` or `cursor-mismatch` at `next`, never a\n   552\t  defect.\n   553\t- A cursor carries the logical-stream identity, not a reachability descriptor.\n   554\t  `cursor-mismatch` compares that identity, so handles reached through distinct\n   555\t  descriptors for the same logical stream accept the same cursor.\n   556\t- Advancing is receiving a successor cursor from `next`. The stream never\n   557\t  advances a reader; the stream holds no reader positions at all. Dropping a\n   558\t  cursor is how a reader detaches — there is nothing to unregister.\n   559\t- Because cursors are immutable values, a reader may retain an old cursor and\n   560\t  re-read retained history, or hand a cursor to another interpreter. What a\n   561\t  cursor can still observe is governed only by retention.\n   562\t\n   563\t## Reading\n   564\t\n   565\t`next` takes a handle and a cursor and returns an outcome immediately.\n   566\tIt is total and non-blocking: every state of the stream has a defined data\n   567\tanswer.\n   568\t\n   569\t| Outcome                       | Meaning                                                                       | Required keys                                                                           |\n   570\t|-------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n   571\t| `:dao.stream/ok`              | A value was observed.                                                         | `:dao.stream/value`, `:dao.stream/cursor` (successor)                                   |\n   572\t| `:dao.stream/blocked`         | Nothing at this position yet; retry later.                                    | —                                                                                       |\n   573\t| `:dao.stream/end`             | What this handle is on is closed and this position is past the retained tail. | —                                                                                       |\n   574\t| `:dao.stream/gap`             | This position was evicted.                                                    | `:dao.stream/cursor` (earliest retained position, or the tail when nothing is retained) |\n   575\t| `:dao.stream/cursor-mismatch` | The cursor was minted by a different logical stream.                          | —                                                                                       |\n   635\tthe transport as the logical stream's owner closes that logical stream; a\n   636\tserver-side accepted-connection handle is an attachment and does not gain\n   637\townership merely by being on the serving host. An attached\n   638\thandle is on an attachment: closing it ends that attachment only — the\n   639\tstream persists, its descriptor remains valid, and `attach!` may rejoin\n   640\tlater. Which scope a transport's handles carry is part of its declared\n   641\tnature.\n   642\t\n   643\t`:dao.stream/closed` and `:dao.stream/end` are handle-relative: they speak of\n   644\twhat *this handle* is on. (Not every outcome is: `gap` is retention-relative\n   645\tand `cursor-mismatch` is logical-stream-relative.) After an attachment\n   646\tcloses, the handle's `append!` answers `closed`; `descriptor` still answers\n   647\t`ok` with reachability and identity — both outlive any attachment; a reader surface, where the\n   648\thandle has one, answers `end` from `next` once observation through this\n   649\thandle is exhausted, and `closed` from `cursor` — no new cursors are\n   650\tminted through a closed attachment. Cursors bind to the logical stream,\n   651\tnot to a handle, so a kept cursor resumes through a newly attached handle.\n   652\t\n    90\t :dao.stream.remote/args     [...]     ; []  | [anchor] | [cursor] | [value]\n    91\t :dao.stream.remote/id       n         ; asker-minted, unique per channel reader the asker holds\n    92\t :dao.stream.remote/budget   k}        ; optional, next only, a positive integer\n    93\t\n    94\t;; answer: the contract's outcome map for the requested operation, verbatim, plus\n    95\t{:dao.stream.remote/id       n\n    96\t :dao.stream/identity        id\n    97\t :dao.stream.remote/oldest   <cursor>  ; the source's current :oldest, when the identity is served\n    98\t :dao.stream.remote/newest   <cursor>  ; the source's current :newest, likewise\n    99\t :dao.stream.remote/more     [...]}    ; optional, next only: further outcome maps in order\n   100\t```\n   101\t\n   102\tA request is one value on the channel writer; an answer is one value on the\n   103\tchannel writer of the other end. The request map is open: keys not named\n   104\there pass to the mirror step's middleware chain (section 7) and are\n   105\totherwise ignored. A value that is neither a well-formed request nor a\n   106\twell-formed answer is dropped as a diagnostic below the mirror step and the\n   107\tlink, as malformed wire input (`datom.world.md`, Host Boundaries).\n   108\t\n   109\tWire ops are exactly four. `close!` never crosses: the mirror holds nothing\n   110\tper remote party to close, and the owner's close is never remote. `attach!`\n   111\tnever crosses: it consumes a descriptor and produces a local handle.\n   112\t\n   113\t### 2.2 The remote descriptor\n   114\t\n   115\t```clojure\n   116\t{:dao.stream/type      :dao.stream/remote\n   117\t :dao.stream/identity  id                      ; the served logical stream\n   118\t :dao.stream/channel   <channel descriptor>}   ; section 3: ws, udp or pair\n   119\t```\n   120\t\n   121\tThe descriptor names a stream and a channel. It carries no authorization, no\n   122\tsurface and no anchors; the mirror answers those. Two remote descriptors\n   123\tthat name one identity through different channels reach one stream, as the\n   124\tcontract's Envelopes section requires of descriptors for one logical stream\n   125\tserved by one transport (OD-3 (a), accepted). Attaching through this\n   126\ttransport never creates: a dynamic dispatch table has an\n   127\t`:dao.stream/attach` entry for `:dao.stream/remote` and no\n   128\t`:dao.stream/create` entry.\n   129\t\n   130\t### 2.3 The mirror step\n   131\t\n   132\t`(mirror-step table chan-reader cursor chan-writer) -> cursor'`. For each\n   133\trequest read from `chan-reader`, in order, bounded by a composition budget:\n   134\t\n   135\t1. Look up `:dao.stream/identity` in the table. Absent: append\n   136\t   `{:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/not-found}` with the request's id and\n   137\t   identity; an identity this peer does not serve has no operations, so\n   138\t   `not-found` is the honest answer for all four. Present, but\n   139\t   `:dao.stream.remote/op` outside the entry's declared surface: append\n   140\t   `not-found` with `:dao.stream.remote/reason :dao.stream.remote/no-surface`,\n   141\t   because the contract forbids answering a nonexistent operation with an\n   142\t   outcome of that operation (Surfaces).\n   143\t2. Otherwise answer `:dao.stream/descriptor` from the entry's handle\n   144\t   directly, and apply `:dao.stream/cursor`, `:dao.stream/next` and\n   145\t   `:dao.stream/append!` to it through `dao.stream.middleware/apply-request`\n   146\t   (`dao.stream.middleware.md`), with context\n   147\t   `{:dao.stream.remote/channel <channel attachment identity>}`.\n   148\t3. Append the outcome map with `:dao.stream.remote/id`,\n   149\t   `:dao.stream/identity`, and the source's current `:oldest` and `:newest`\n   150\t   cursors obtained by `cursor` on the entry's handle. Where `cursor` does\n   151\t   not answer `ok` for an anchor, that piggyback key is omitted.\n   152\t\n   153\tFor `:dao.stream/next` with a budget `k` the mirror may follow the successor\n   154\tcursor of each `ok` outcome up to `k - 1` further times and place the\n   155\tfurther outcome maps, in order, under `:dao.stream.remote/more`. The first\n   186\tOn attach the link sends one `descriptor` request for the identity. Its\n   187\tanswer is the reflection's confirmation. `not-found` marks the reflection\n   188\t**gone**; `ok` records the source's descriptor and anchors. Either is\n   189\tappended to the event writer when one is composed.\n   190\t\n   191\t**Drain.** Every operation on any reflection first reads the link's channel\n   192\treader to `blocked`, filing each answer whose id is outstanding, installing\n   193\teach `:dao.stream.remote/more` outcome at the cursor that precedes it, and\n   194\trecording piggybacked anchors. Answers whose id is not outstanding are\n   195\tdropped. Non-answer values are dropped as diagnostics. This is where the\n   196\tasynchrony goes (`dao.stream.md`, Where the asynchrony goes): the caller's\n   197\town polling is the cadence, and no driver step exists.\n   198\t\n   199\tThen the operation answers as follows.\n   200\t\n   201\t+---------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   202\t| Operation           | Reflection behaviour after the drain                                                                        |\n   203\t+=====================+=============================================================================================================+\n   204\t| `descriptor`        | Local. `ok` with the remote descriptor it was attached with and `:dao.stream/identity`. Same identity as   |\n   205\t|                     | the source's own descriptor, different reachability.                                                       |\n   206\t+---------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   207\t| `cursor` `:oldest`  | `ok` with the latest piggybacked cursor for that anchor. Before any answer has arrived, `transport-error`   |\n   208\t| or `:newest`        | with `:dao.stream/retry? true`. A stale `:newest` is earlier than a fresh one, so a composition that mints  |\n   209\t|                     | before an operation still observes everything the operation causes; a stale `:oldest` may yield `gap`,     |\n   210\t|                     | which the contract already permits of a fresh `:oldest`.                                                   |\n   211\t+---------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   212\t| `cursor` other      | Send a `cursor` request if none is outstanding for that anchor; until answered, `transport-error` with     |\n   213\t|                     | `:dao.stream/retry? true`; once filed, the source's map verbatim, `invalid-anchor` included.                |\n   214\t+---------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   215\t| `next` `c`          | A filed answer for `c` is returned and forgotten. Otherwise a `next` request for `c` is sent if none is     |\n   216\t|                     | outstanding, and the answer is `blocked`: nothing is observable at this position through this handle yet.  |\n   217\t+---------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   218\t| `append!` `v`       | Send the request; return the channel writer's outcome. `ok` is acceptance on the outbound path, exactly     |\n   219\t|                     | `dao.stream.md` Writing's rule for a transport that carries values toward a stream elsewhere. The source's  |\n   220\t|                     | outcome is appended to the event writer when it is filed, correlated by id; without an event writer it is  |\n   221\t|                     | dropped.                                                                                                    |\n   222\t+---------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   223\t| `close!`            | Local. The reflection forgets its outstanding ids; afterwards `append!` answers `closed`, `cursor` answers  |\n   224\t|                     | `closed`, `next` answers filed outcomes then `end`. Nothing crosses the wire.                               |\n   225\t+---------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   226\t\n   227\t**Gone.** Any filed `not-found` for a reflection's identity that carries no\n   228\t`:dao.stream.remote/reason`, from the attach probe or from a later\n   229\toperation, marks it gone. A `not-found` with reason `no-surface` marks\n   230\tnothing and is returned as `transport-error` with that reason for the\n   231\toperation that caused it. Every later `cursor`,\n   232\t`next` and `append!` answers `transport-error` with\n   233\t`:dao.stream.remote/reason :dao.stream.remote/not-found`. `descriptor`\n   234\tstill answers `ok`: a name outlives what it named. A reflection marked gone\n   235\tis how a remote holder observes reclaim (section 6).\n   236\t\n   237\t**Refused.** A filed `:dao.stream/refused` is returned verbatim for the\n   238\toperation it answers and marks nothing: refusal is per operation\n   239\t(`dao.stream.md`, Result Convention).\n   240\t\n   241\t**Declared nature.** A reflection declares the surface its remote descriptor's\n   242\tsource declares, learned from the attach probe's answer; before that answer\n   243\tit declares `#{:reader :writer :closable}`, and an operation the source\n   244\tturns out to lack is answered as the `no-surface` case above. Its `blocked`\n   245\tis handle-relative, as the contract's\n   246\tConcurrency section already makes every outcome: one perspective at one\n   247\tmoment. Its excluded outcomes: `next` never answers `cursor-mismatch` or\n   248\t`invalid-cursor` from its own judgement, only relayed; `append!` never\n   249\tanswers the source's `full`, which arrives on the event writer.\n   250\t\n   270\tA channel declares a portable value domain, a frame budget, and whether it\n   271\tis ordered and reliable. The link reads the last as composition data for\n   272\t`:dao.stream.remote/resend-after`; nothing else in section 2 changes per\n   273\tchannel.\n   274\t\n   275\t### 3.1 WebSocket\n   276\t\n   277\t`dao.stream.ws` as specified in `dao.stream.ws.md`: a channel is one\n   278\tattachment; its deposit medium is the channel reader and its socket handle\n   279\tthe channel writer. Transit-JSON text or the CBOR binary profile\n   280\t(`dao.stream.transit`, `dao.stream.cbor`). Ordered and reliable; frame\n   281\tbudget unbounded.\n   282\t\n   283\tDirection is establishment, not authority. A WebSocket has a dialer and an\n   284\tacceptor because TCP does. Once established the channel is symmetric and\n   285\tnothing in this document remembers who dialed. Hosts: clj, cljs on Node and\n   286\tcljd may dial or accept (`src/clj/dao/stream/ws/jvm.clj`,\n   287\t`src/cljs/dao/stream/ws/node.cljs`, `src/cljd/dao/stream/ws/dart.cljd`);\n   288\tcljs in a browser may only dial (`src/cljs/dao/stream/ws/browser.cljs`).\n   289\t\n   290\t### 3.2 UDP\n   291\t\n   292\t`dao.stream.udp` is a new channel with the WebSocket deposit model: a socket\n   293\tretains nothing, so it has no reader surface, and its adapter deposits every\n   294\tdatagram as an event carrying the datagram's source address and its decoded\n   295\tvalue onto the medium the composition wired.\n   296\t\n   297\t- Descriptor: `{:dao.stream/type :dao.stream/udp :dao.stream/identity\n   298\t  <channel identity> :dao.stream.udp/host h :dao.stream.udp/port p}`.\n   299\t  `attach!` binds or reuses a local socket and returns a handle whose writer\n   300\t  path is datagrams to that address.\n   345\tthe source's `gap`. Double framing consumes budget: an inner value inside an\n   346\touter `append!` must fit the outer channel's budget with both envelopes.\n   347\t\n   348\t## 4. Reachability and NAT\n   349\t\n   350\tA channel needs one end that can receive an unsolicited packet, or a\n   351\tcoordinated simultaneous send. The protocol cannot change that; it makes\n   352\tthe workarounds conventions.\n   353\t\n   354\t+------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   355\t| Situation                                      | Outcome                                                                    |\n   356\t+================================================+============================================================================+\n   357\t| One side reachable (public address, forwarded  | Direct: the other side dials, WebSocket or UDP.                            |\n   358\t| port, or full-cone NAT)                        |                                                                            |\n   359\t+------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   360\t| Restricted or port-restricted NAT on one or    | Hole punch through a meeting peer (below).                                 |\n   361\t| both sides, UDP allowed                        |                                                                            |\n   362\t+------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   363\t| Symmetric NAT on both sides, or carrier-grade  | Punching is unreliable. Relay through a pair served by a reachable peer.   |\n   364\t| NAT                                            |                                                                            |\n   365\t+------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   366\t| UDP blocked                                    | WebSocket only; the browser rule applies.                                  |\n   367\t+------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   368\t| Browser at one end                             | It dials. Two browsers relay through a pair. A WebRTC data channel is a    |\n   369\t|                                                | future channel type whose candidates ride the same meeting stream.        |\n   370\t+------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   371\t\n   372\t**Hole punching, as a convention.** A meeting peer M serves a\n   373\t`meet-requests` stream with surface `#{:writer}` and a `meet-board` stream\n   374\twith surface `#{:reader}`, both ordinary table entries. Over a UDP channel\n   375\ta peer appends `{:meet/here <name>}`; M's interpreter reads the request and\n   376\tthe channel attachment identity it arrived on, which for UDP is the\n   377\treflexive address, and appends `{:meet/seen <name> :meet/reflexive {:host h\n   378\t:port p} :dao.lease/lease L}` to the board. Both parties of a call read the\n   379\tboard, then each sends a `descriptor` request with a fresh id to the other's\n   380\treflexive address from the same socket M observed. A path exists when an\n   381\tanswer, even `not-found`, carrying one of this peer's outstanding ids\n   382\tarrives from the expected address; the first inbound datagram alone confirms\n   383\tnothing. That is the whole of STUN, as facts on a stream; no probe frame\n   384\texists.\n   385\t\n   386\t**Relay, as a convention.** A peer asks M, through `meet-requests`, for a\n   387\tpair; M creates two ring buffers, enters them in its table, and posts their\n   388\tremote descriptors on the board as a lease grant (section 6). The asking\n   389\tpeers attach a pair channel over them. M holds no authority, runs the same\n   390\tcode as every peer, interprets nothing it relays, and may be replaced by any\n   391\treachable peer. That two unreachable peers need M at all is a fact about the\n   392\tnetwork, not a role in this protocol.\n   393\t\n   394\t**Address exposure.** Reflexive addresses on a board are readable by every\n   395\treader of the board. This convention claims no private delivery.\n   500\tUnits are the composition's; these conventions fix `{:ms 1 :s 1000}`. Each\n   501\tside reads its own tick stream, and cross-host rate skew is what the judge's\n   502\ttolerance covers.\n   503\t\n   504\t## 7. Middleware and the ShiBi seam\n   505\t\n   506\tThe mechanism is `dao.stream.middleware.md`: a middleware is a map of two\n   507\tpure transforms over the operation map and the outcome map; `wrap` turns a\n   508\thandle and a chain into a handle on the same logical stream; a middleware\n   509\tmaps position `p` of the inner to position `p` of the outer and never\n   510\ttouches a cursor, an anchor, an identity, an id or an outcome kind. This\n   511\tdocument adds the network attachment points.\n   512\t\n   513\t+------------------+--------------------------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------+\n   514\t| Attachment point | What is wrapped                                  | Typical use                                          |\n   515\t+==================+==================================================+======================================================+\n   516\t| table entry      | The served handle, before it enters the table.   | Gating, value encryption for serving, redaction,     |\n   517\t|                  | The mirror sees only the wrapped handle.         | metering.                                            |\n   518\t+------------------+--------------------------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------+\n   519\t| reflection       | The reflection, inside the host-composed attach  | Presenting a credential, value decryption,           |\n   520\t|                  | closure.                                         | metering.                                            |\n   521\t+------------------+--------------------------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------+\n   522\t| channel          | The channel's writer and reader at each end.     | Authenticated encryption of whole requests and       |\n   523\t|                  |                                                  | answers, so a relay sees opaque values.              |\n   524\t+------------------+--------------------------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------+\n   525\t\n   526\tKeys and chains are composition data handed to `wrap`, never descriptor\n   527\tfields. Value-level encryption hides values and leaves identity, ops,\n   528\tcursors, sizes and timing visible; channel-level encryption hides all but\n   529\tsizes, timing and which pair talks. Key distribution is a convention and is\n   530\tnot specified here. Ciphers come from libraries.\n   531\t\n   532\t**The seam.** Authentication and authorization belong to ShiBi (`dao.shibi.md`),\n   533\tunspecified. This document offers only:\n   534\t\n   535\t- **The slot.** A reserved open key on the request map,\n   536\t  `:dao.stream.remote/credential`, whose value is opaque here and travels\n   537\t  per request. A per-channel credential is a convention: the reflection's\n   538\t  `present` middleware attaches the same value to every request.\n   539\t- **The context.** The mirror hands `apply-request` the context\n   540\t  `{:dao.stream.remote/channel <attachment identity>}`; local callers hand\n   541\t  `{}`. No other context key exists in this version.\n   542\t- **The gate**, mirror-side: `dao.stream.middleware/gate`, whose `verify`\n   543\t  is a pure function of folded state, context and request, whose `fold` is\n   544\t  a pure function over facts drained from explicit source streams, and\n   545\t  whose refusal is the contract's `:dao.stream/refused` with an opaque\n   546\t  `:dao.stream.middleware/reason`.\n   547\t- **The presenter**, reflection-side: `dao.stream.middleware/present`, a\n   548\t  pure function of context and request that fills the slot.\n   549\t\n   550\tA ShiBi built as index and query interpreters over a tuple space plugs in\n   551\twithout changing this document: its index interpreter is the gate's `fold`\n   552\tover the streams it observes, its query interpreter is the gate's `verify`,\n   553\tits tokens are the slot's values, and its decisions, where it meters, are\n   554\tthe gate's decision emissions onto a stream it also indexes. The seam\n   555\tforecloses none of attenuation, offline verification, revocation, caveats on\n   556\top, identity or time as tick readings, issuance by any peer, or migration\n   557\tcredentials; a budget spent across peers waits on `dao.stream.discovery.md`'s\n   558\tcapability-versus-currency decision, which this document does not depend\n   559\ton. Until ShiBi exists, gating is an allow-list keyed on\n   560\t`:dao.stream.remote/channel` or a pre-shared value in the slot.\n   561\t\n   562\tLease attribution (section 6) rests on per-author media alone and needs no\n   563\tcredential; a gate on a renewal medium is an addition.\n   564\t\n   565\t## 8. Unification\n   610\t+----------------------------------------------------+------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   611\t\n   612\t**What replaces `dao.jing.remote`.** Content lookup is a request and response\n   613\tservice over remote streams, in one new module `dao.jing.content`:\n   614\t\n   615\t- **Vocabulary**, a payload convention: `{:jing/request r :jing/get\n   616\t  address}` answered by `{:jing/request r :jing/found? b :jing/bytes b64}`;\n   617\t  `{:jing/request r :jing/put address :jing/bytes b64}` answered by\n   618\t  `{:jing/request r :jing/result :inserted | :present}`. `r` is a\n   619\t  self-minted random value. Bytes travel as padded standard Base64 text, as\n   620\t  today.\n   621\t- **The interpreter** (`dao.jing.content/serve-step`): a pure step over a\n   622\t  local `requests` reader and `answers` writer against a `dao.jing` handle.\n   623\t  It runs the one ingress canonicality check on every put.\n   624\t- **The ingress check**, one shared function `dao.jing/accept-bytes!`,\n   625\t  moved from `dao.jing.remote/accept-bytes!` and used by the interpreter,\n   626\t  by the stepped client on every `get` answer and `:present` verify, and\n   627\t  by `yin.vm.linker`. The three copies become one.\n   628\t- **The stepped client** (`dao.jing.content.step`): the `request-put`,\n   629\t  `request-get`, `request-materialize`, `step`, `abandon` shape of\n   630\t  `dao.jing.remote.step`, over a `requests` writer and an `answers` reader\n   631\t  that are reflections or local handles. This is the portable interface,\n   632\t  per `dao.stream.md` OD-5.\n   633\t- **The blocking driver** (`dao.jing.content.driver`, clj only): host\n   634\t  policy that steps the client and sleeps, returning a synchronous\n   635\t  `dao.jing` handle, for `dao.space.index`, `dao.data.btree.storage` and\n   636\t  `yin.vm.content` on the JVM. It replaces `connect-content!`.\n   637\t- **The async facade** (`dao.jing.content.async`): replaces\n   638\t  `dao.jing.remote.async` over the new stepped client, same consumer API.\n   639\t- **The coordinate**: `{:dao.jing/type :dao.jing/remote :dao.jing/requests\n   640\t  <remote descriptor> :dao.jing/answers <remote descriptor>}` on every\n   641\t  host, resolved by `dao.jing.coordinate/open!` to the driver on clj and to\n   642\t  the stepped client elsewhere. The `:url` form is gone.\n   643\t\n   644\t## 9. Completion criteria and implementation slices\n   645\t\n   690\t+----+------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+---------------+\n   691\t| 8  | UCF facade: `yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md`      | The M4 acceptance row: a string-backed stream migrates | clj cljs cljd |\n   692\t|    | 7.4.3 and 7.5.3 lift and lower through remote descriptors  | with a kept cursor and resumes; forced eviction yields |               |\n   693\t|    | with the `:dao.stream.remote/v1` cursor profile.           | `gap` with the source's cursor.                        |               |\n   694\t+----+------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+---------------+\n   695\t\n   696\tDeferred, in order of expected arrival: authentication and authorization\n   697\t(ShiBi), key distribution, a WebRTC data channel, browser inbound. None\n   698\tchanges a shape in section 2.\n   699\t\n   700\t## 10. Contract amendments and companion edits\n   701\t\n   702\tIn `dao.stream.md`: OD-1 accepted with the write fallback \"effect unknown; no\n   703\tautomatic retry\" and the `:dao.stream/retry?` key; OD-2 accepted; OD-3\n   704\tdecision 2 accepted and (a)'s endpoint sentence in Envelopes; one\n   705\t`:dao.stream/refused` row on `cursor`, `next` and `append!`; one sentence\n   706\tin Surfaces on a handle composed over another handle. Nothing network.\n   707\t\n   708\tIn `dao.lease.md`: served entries and relay pairs named as subjects a lease\n   709\tmay govern; the carriage note gains this document's `not-found`.\n   710\t\n   711\tIn `dao.stream.ws.md`: the stream belongs to the peer that holds it;\n   712\tserving copies is the transport's, attaching to the original is this\n   713\tdocument's; dialer-side and acceptor-side; deferred liveness, resumption\n   714\tand RPC items point here.\n   715\t\n   716\tIn `yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md`: 7.4.3 and 7.5.3 cite this\n   717\tdocument for the facade; the put resume rule waits on the event writer's\n   718\tsource outcome; `:yin.k/cursor-profiles` names `:dao.stream.remote/v1`; the\n   719\tacceptance row cites section 9 slice 8.\n   720\t\n   721\tIn `dao.jing.md`, `yin.vm.linker.md`, `dao.data.btree.md`, `dao.jing.dht.md`\n   722\tand `daostream-udp-design.md`: the sentences naming `dao.jing.remote` as\n   723\tthe remote path, the linker's content pair \"over `dao.jing.remote.step`\",\n   724\tthe btree remote row, the DHT's NAT limitation, and the v1 UDP design's\n   725\tstatus now cite this document.\n    55\tdeclares `h`'s identity, passes cursors through, and may present values\n    56\tunder its own interpretation (`dao.stream.md`, Surfaces).\n    57\t\n    58\t## The position rule\n    59\t\n    60\tA middleware maps position `p` of the inner handle to position `p` of the\n    61\touter handle, for every `p`. It therefore never:\n    62\t\n    63\t- constructs, parses or rewrites a cursor, an anchor or a `gap` recovery\n    64\t  cursor;\n    65\t- changes `:dao.stream/outcome`, `:dao.stream/identity`,\n    66\t  `:dao.stream.remote/op` or `:dao.stream.remote/id`;\n    67\t- skips, merges or reorders elements.\n    68\t\n    69\tIt may replace `(first args)` on `append!` and `:dao.stream/value` on an\n    70\t`ok` from `next` with any value, of any size; add or read open keys; and\n    71\tshort-circuit with an outcome from the operation's exhaustive set. A cipher,\n    72\ta compressor, a redactor and an annotator preserve the rule. A filter that\n    73\tdrops elements does not: it is an interpreter that forwards into a new\n    74\tstream with its own identity (`dao.stream.md`, Composition), never a\n    75\tmiddleware.\n    76\t\n    77\t## The four prohibitions\n    78\t\n    79\tA transform is total and returns at once. It never loops over the inner\n    80\thandle, never retries, never waits, and never invokes any handle operation\n    81\tother than one `append!` to a side stream it was composed with. A transform\n    82\tthat needs facts from a stream drains that stream with its own cursor inside\n    83\tthe call, as `gate` below does, and reads no host clock.\n    84\t\n    85\t## Failure inside a transform\n    86\t\n    87\tA value that cannot be transformed on the way out (undecodable ciphertext,\n    88\ta corrupt compressed value) is presented as\n    89\t`{:dao.stream.middleware/undecodable true :dao.stream.middleware/raw v}`\n    90\tunder `:dao.stream/value`, with the outcome and cursor untouched: the\n    91\tposition is real and the reader decides. A value that cannot be transformed\n    92\ton the way in answers `:dao.stream/invalid-value`.\n    93\t\n    94\t## Two generic middlewares\n    95\t\n    96\t**gate**, the authorize step. Composition data only; the policy is not\n    97\tspecified here.\n    98\t\n    99\t```clojure\n   100\t(gate {:dao.stream.middleware/verify  (fn [state ctx req] -> nil | reason)  ; pure; nil allows\n   101\t       :dao.stream.middleware/fold    (fn [state fact] -> state)             ; pure\n   102\t       :dao.stream.middleware/sources [{:handle h :cursor c} ...]            ; explicit fact streams\n   103\t       :dao.stream.middleware/state   s0})\n   104\t```\n   105\t\n   106\tIts `in` drains every source to `blocked` with the gate's own cursors,\n   107\tfolds each fact into its state, then calls `verify`. `nil` passes the\n   108\trequest through; a reason short-circuits with\n   109\t`{:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/refused :dao.stream.middleware/reason\n   110\treason}`. A source that answers `gap` is folded as the fact\n   111\t`{:dao.stream.middleware/gap true}` so a policy may fail closed. A gate that\n   112\tmust account for its own decisions appends each as\n   113\t`{:dao.stream.middleware/decision :allow | :refuse ...}` to a side stream\n   114\tit was composed with and that is also one of its sources: this is\n   115\t**decision emission**, and it is how metered use keeps its state on a\n   116\tstream rather than in the gate.\n   117\t\n   118\t**present**, the credential step.\n   119\t\n   120\t```clojure\n   121\t(present {:dao.stream.middleware/present (fn [ctx req] -> credential)})\n   122\t```\n   123\t\n   124\tIts `in` associates the result under `:dao.stream.remote/credential`. Its\n   125\t`out` is identity.\n   126\t\n   127\tNeither middleware knows what a credential is. The trivial policy this\n   128\trepository ships with is an allow-list keyed on `:dao.stream.remote/channel`\n   129\tin `ctx`, ignoring the credential. Everything else belongs to\n   130\t[`dao.shibi.md`](./dao.shibi.md).\n   131\t\n   132\t## Exemplars\n   133\t\n   134\tValue encryption: `in` on `:dao.stream/append!` replaces the value with its\n   135\tauthenticated ciphertext; `out` on an `ok` from `:dao.stream/next` replaces\n   136\t`:dao.stream/value` with the plaintext or the undecodable marker. Cursors\n   137\tuntouched. The key is an argument to the constructor that built the\n   138\tmiddleware, exactly as a deposit medium is an argument to a transport\n   139\tconstructor. Ciphers come from libraries.\n   140\t\n   141\tMetering: `out` appends `{:meter/identity id :meter/op op :meter/outcome\n   142\tkind}` to a composed metering stream and returns the outcome unchanged. The\n   143\tside effect is a stream emission (`datom.world.md`, Streams).\n   144\t\n   145\t## Invariant compliance\n     1\t# ShiBi\n     2\t\n     3\tStatus: stub. ShiBi is not specified and not implemented\n     4\t(`docs/bootstrap.md`, Capability Engine). This page records only what other\n     5\tdocuments may rely on and what ShiBi must supply. It designs nothing.\n     6\t\n     7\t## What it is\n     8\t\n     9\tShiBi is the capability system of datom.world: authentication and\n    10\tauthorization are its job and no other document's. It is to be built the\n    11\tway `dao.space` is built, as a tuple space that emerges from two\n    12\tinterpreters over ordinary streams, one that indexes and one that queries,\n    13\tneither knowing the other exists (`datom.world.md`, Streams). Its tokens are\n    14\tdescribed elsewhere as Macaroon-style: attenuatable, offline-verifiable,\n    15\trevocable caveats (`dao.space.security.md`, Capabilities Govern\n    16\tInterpreters).\n    17\t\n    18\t## How it plugs in\n    19\t\n    20\tShiBi integrates through the middleware seam of\n    21\t[`dao.stream.middleware.md`](./dao.stream.middleware.md) and the attachment\n    22\tpoints of [`dao.stream.remote.md`](./dao.stream.remote.md) section 7, which\n    23\tare fixed and which ShiBi must not change. To plug in it supplies:\n    24\t\n    25\t- a `verify`, a pure function of folded state, context and request that\n    26\t  answers nil or a reason: its query interpreter;\n    27\t- a `fold` and the source streams it observes: its index interpreter;\n    28\t- a `present`, a pure function of context and request that produces the\n    29\t  value carried in `:dao.stream.remote/credential`;\n    30\t- a reason vocabulary under its own namespace for\n    31\t  `:dao.stream.middleware/reason`;\n    32\t- where use is metered, the decision facts it emits and indexes.\n    33\t\n    34\tThe contract's refusal outcome is `:dao.stream/refused` and is\n    35\tcapability-free (`dao.stream.md`, Result Convention). Lease attribution\n    36\trests on per-author media and does not depend on ShiBi\n    37\t(`dao.stream.remote.md`, section 6; `yin.repl.link-policy.md`).\n    38\t\n    39\t## What blocks it being load-bearing\n    40\t\n    41\t`dao.stream.discovery.md`, The ShiBi precondition: ShiBi is a capability\n    42\ttoken in one place and a fungible currency in another, and a currency needs\n    43\tdouble-spend resolution, which is global consensus. Until that decision is\n    44\tmade, no design may depend on ShiBi for anything beyond the seam above, and\n    45\tgating in this repository is an allow-list or a pre-shared value.\n    25\tthan \"backend\" vs. \"not a backend\":** `dao.jing.file` is the only backend\n    26\tthat re-ingests its own output across process death — it must persist an\n    27\tappend-only log and later replay it with no external message boundary to\n    28\tmark records, so it alone must invent a self-describing frame, which is\n    29\twhy that frame is itself a CBOR structure (a two-element\n    30\t`[digest payload-bytes]` array; see *Memory and files*). `dao.jing.mem`\n    31\tnever persists, so it has no framing at all; `dao.jing.content` (formerly\n    32\t`dao.jing.remote`)/`dao.jing.dht`\n    33\tget record boundaries for free from Transit's own message envelopes, so\n    34\tthey frame with Base64-inside-Transit, never a raw CBOR array. This is not\n    35\tabout `dao.jing.file` being more or less \"Jing's own\" than any other\n    36\tbackend in this source tree — all of `memory`/`file`/`remote`/`dht` are\n    37\tequally in-repo; only PostgreSQL and S3 are genuinely third-party and not\n    38\tyet built. What is third-party-implementable is the byte-store *contract*\n   430\t`[digest payload-bytes]` record: digest is the raw digest byte string for\n   431\tthe address's carried algorithm (both initial registry algorithms are 32\n   432\tbytes) and payload-bytes contains the exact canonical payload.\n   433\t**`dao.jing.file` itself owns parsing and constructing this two-element\n   434\tframe**, with Jing's own shared codec — the exception the Objective names\n   435\tabove, needed because `dao.jing.file`'s frame shape is itself a CBOR\n   436\tstructure, unlike `dao.jing.mem`'s unframed map or\n   437\t`dao.jing.content`/`dao.jing.dht`'s Base64-inside-Transit framing. The\n   438\twrapper owns keyword↔digest conversion, so file framing has no\n   439\tidentifier-codec dependency, and the algorithm identifier itself is not\n   440\tcarried in the frame — it round-trips through the address keyword the\n   441\tdigest is paired with at the wrapper boundary. Replay checks the digest\n   442\tlength against the address-carried algorithm's registry entry and compares\n   195\t    [_]\n   196\t    {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok\n   197\t     :dao.stream/descriptor descriptor\n   198\t     :dao.stream/identity (:dao.stream/identity descriptor)})\n   199\t\n   200\t\n   201\t  stream/IDaoStreamWriter\n   202\t\n   203\t  (append!\n   204\t    [_ value]\n   205\t    (let [{:keys [phase socket]} @state]\n   206\t      (cond\n   207\t        (= :closed phase) (outcome :dao.stream/closed)\n   208\t        (not= :open phase) (outcome :dao.stream/full)\n   209\t        (not ((:ws/portable-value? codec) value)) (outcome :dao.stream/invalid-value)\n   210\t        :else (try\n   211\t                (send-result socket ((:ws/encode codec) {:ws/frame :ws/value\n   212\t                                                         :ws/value value}))\n   213\t                (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default) _\n   214\t                  (outcome :dao.stream/invalid-value))))))\n   215\t\n   216\t\n   217\t  stream/IDaoStreamClosable\n   218\t\n   219\t  (close!\n   220\t    [_]\n    65\t\n    66\t## The Duplex Model\n    67\t\n    68\tA WebSocket is two independent directions sharing one connection, and the\n    69\tdesign keeps them separate:\n    70\t\n    71\t- **Outbound**: `append!` on the socket handle encodes the value and\n    72\t  sends it. Sending is appending; nothing is converted.\n    73\t- **Inbound**: wire framing and protocol validation first either produce a\n    74\t  valid transport event or perform protocol teardown. The host's adapter then\n    75\t  deposits every produced event — payloads and connection lifecycle events\n    76\t  alike, judging none semantically — onto the stream the **host composition** wired for this\n    77\t  boundary. The deposit medium is an ordinary DaoStream stream the host\n    78\t  composed; this transport defines no separate event mechanism and no bus of\n    79\t  its own. (In the v2 migration slice that stream is a ring buffer, under a\n    80\t  time-boxed exception recorded in ADR 0003; the adapter cannot tell what it\n    81\t  deposits into, so the later swap is composition-only.) From that moment the traffic has positions, retention, cursors,\n    82\t  and gaps — the reading model of the contract applies in full, on that\n    83\t  stream.\n    84\t\n    95\tcopies it to a file, a third ignores it. How arrivals are interpreted is the\n    96\tinterpreter's business, not the transport's configuration.\n    97\t\n    98\t**Attachment identity.** This transport distinguishes its attachments, so its\n    99\t`attach!` success map carries `:dao.stream/attachment` as the contract\n   100\trequires: a descriptor names the served stream and cannot tell two attachments\n   101\tto it apart, and a handle is a host-local object that cannot be compared\n   102\tagainst data that crossed a codec.\n   103\t\n   104\tEvery event deposited for that attachment carries the same value under\n   105\t`:ws/attachment`. The key differs because the scopes differ: result keywords\n   106\tare the contract's and live under `:dao.stream/…`, while a deposited event is\n   107\ta stream element, where transport-owned keys live under the transport's\n   108\tnamespace. Correlation is by the value carried, never by the key's name.\n   109\t\n   110\tWithout that identity a shared medium is a multiplexed sequence with no\n   111\tdemultiplexing key: every event present, in order, and unattributable.\n   112\t\n   135\t                 :dao.stream/surface #{:writer :closable}}} ; accepted offers only; host-local\n   136\t```\n   137\t\n   138\tThe event kinds divide into four groups. **Acceptance offer** —\n   139\t`:ws/accepted` is deposited once into a serving handoff slot after the\n   140\trequested stream resolves and a server-side attachment handle is\n   141\tconstructed. It carries that writer+closable handle and its declared surface\n   142\tunder `:ws/handle`.\n   143\t**Resolution** — `:ws/opened`, `:ws/not-found`, or `:ws/transport-error` says\n   144\thow a client attachment turned out; exactly one resolution event is deposited\n   145\tper attachment, and it is what a retry policy reads. **Lifecycle** —\n   146\t`:ws/closed` when the connection went away and `:ws/ended` when the served\n   147\tstream itself ended (see Ending a served stream). **Traffic** —\n   148\t`:ws/payload`, carrying `:ws/value`, and `:ws/error`. `:ws/error` is a diagnostic and is never terminal by itself. A survivable socket error may be followed by no lifecycle event; a protocol failure is followed by the terminal event produced when its required teardown completes.\n   149\t\n   150\tOnce an attachment identity exists, **every connection end deposits exactly\n   151\tone terminal lifecycle event at each endpoint**, whether the close was local,\n   152\tremote, or caused by failed deposit, protocol teardown, admission expiry, or\n   153\tendpoint stop. `:ws/ended` is reserved for a served-stream close; every other\n   154\tend is `:ws/closed`. For a code-4000 served-stream close, both the initiating\n   155\tserver endpoint and the observing client endpoint deposit `:ws/ended`. A\n   156\tresolution event, if any, is deposited first. Thus a\n   157\tdisclaimer is observed as `:ws/not-found` followed by `:ws/closed`, while a\n   158\tpre-resolution connection failure is `:ws/transport-error` followed by\n   159\t`:ws/closed`. Only the host close-completion callback deposits the terminal\n   160\tevent, guarded by attachment state so racing close/error callbacks cannot\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"agent_message","text":"Completed-GMT: 2026-09-26 18:29:06 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-09-27 01:29:06 Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh\nCoding-Agent: codex\nSession-ID: 01a0debb-6229-79e1-890d-4d1e0b7d8565\n\n## 1. INVARIANT\n\n**Mostly sound framing; not yet implementable as written.** The protocol has no client/server wire role or peer ID, and treats a relay, meeting board, and lease grantor as peers performing conventions. The service example’s “server” is explicitly a convention, which the owner allows. The mirror table is local dispatch, not a privileged network registry. [remote.md:47–77, 130–147, 348–395, 420–430](docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md)\n\nThe NAT caveats correctly acknowledge that some peers need a third peer to relay, browsers cannot listen, and traffic is plaintext without channel encryption. But “symmetric NAT or CGNAT” is too broad as a single outcome: CGNAT describes address sharing, not one mapping/filtering behavior, and some CGNAT paths can hole-punch. The caveat should identify endpoint-dependent mapping/filtering as the problem and say when relay is required. Also state that peers need an outbound path to a reachable WebSocket peer or relay; if UDP is blocked and no such path is available, communication is impossible. [remote.md:31–45, 348–395](docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md)\n\nThe string example uses ordinary stream operations and no string-specific wire case, but it depends on a WebSocket channel projection that the document does not specify (see §3). Its descriptor also omits the surface while the reflection claims to learn it from the attach probe. [remote.md:113–128, 186–189, 237–249, 401–418](docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md)\n\n## 2. CONTRACT\n\nThe `dao.stream.md` edits appear limited to OD-1/2/3, the generic `refused` outcome row on cursor/next/append, and the composed-handle sentence. The refusal is absent from descriptor and close, as intended; the contract remains network- and capability-free. [dao.stream.md:439–443](docs/design/dao.stream.md) The remote document’s claim that only “three accepted decisions” affect the contract contradicts its own list of refusal and composition edits. [remote.md:24–27, 700–706](docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md)\n\nLeaving `blocked` un-reworded is sound: reflection `next` returns it when no value is yet observable through that handle, consistent with the contract’s handle-relative, immediate outcome rule. [remote.md:191–216](docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md) [dao.stream.md:565–573, 643–650](docs/design/dao.stream.md)\n\n**Defect:** OD-3 says a cursor outliving its stream yields `not-found` at `attach!` or `cursor-mismatch` at `next`. `attach!` does not take a cursor; correct the wording to describe attachment to a missing stream separately. [dao.stream.md:546–552](docs/design/dao.stream.md)\n\nThe spec adds `:dao.stream/refused`, but the implementation’s closed outcome sets do not yet include it. This is acceptable as a design change only if implementation work updates them before relying on the new row. [dao.stream.md:??](docs/design/dao.stream.md) [src/cljc/dao/stream.cljc:67–93](src/cljc/dao/stream.cljc)\n\n## 3. THE PROTOCOL\n\n**Blocking protocol defects:**\n\n- The mirror emits `:dao.stream/not-found` as the outcome for descriptor, cursor, next, and append when an identity is absent or an operation is unsupported. These operations do not all permit that outcome: descriptor permits only `ok`; cursor, next, and append have their own closed sets. Nor can `not-found` represent a missing surface: the contract says a handle cannot answer an operation it does not declare. Define a protocol-level error distinct from the source operation’s outcome map, and use it consistently for missing identity, gone resource, and unsupported surface. [remote.md:94–107, 135–151, 227–235](docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md) [src/cljc/dao/stream.cljc:67–93](src/cljc/dao/stream.cljc) [dao.stream.md:405–410](docs/design/dao.stream.md)\n- The surface test compares operation keywords such as `:dao.stream/next` with declared surfaces `:reader`, `:writer`, and `:closable`. Define the mapping (`cursor`/`next` → reader; `append!` → writer; `descriptor` always available), and handle unsupported operations without fabricating a DaoStream outcome. [remote.md:135–147](docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md) [dao.stream.md:405–410](docs/design/dao.stream.md)\n- The descriptor carries no surface, yet the reflection says the attach probe’s answer supplies it and uses it to determine local operations. Neither the descriptor nor the described answer contains that information. Include declared surface in the protocol envelope or remove surface discovery and define a sound local handle contract. [remote.md:94–99, 113–128, 186–189, 237–249](docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md)\n- The WebSocket path is not connected to the specified raw channel values. The existing WebSocket writer encodes `{:ws/frame :ws/value :ws/value value}`, while the host adapter deposits `:ws/payload`, lifecycle, and resolution events onto a DaoStream. The mirror expects raw request maps. Specify the projection that unwraps values, correlates attachments, and handles lifecycle events, or define a distinct channel adapter. Without it, the toy and the promised WebSocket unification do not compose. [remote.md:268–300](docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md) [src/cljc/dao/stream/ws.cljc:203–214](src/cljc/dao/stream/ws.cljc) [dao.stream.ws.md:71–82, 98–108, 138–160](docs/design/dao.stream.ws.md)\n- Anchor piggybacking calls `cursor` directly on the table handle, bypassing middleware, and does so regardless of reader surface. That can expose gated anchors or invoke an unsupported operation. Only obtain anchors through the declared reader path and its middleware, or omit the optimization. [remote.md:143–151](docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md) [middleware.md:58–75](docs/design/dao.stream.middleware.md)\n\nUDP fragmentation has bounded message size and partial-message count, and replies to the source address. But the 1200-byte budget is not defined as applying to the *entire encoded fragment*, including metadata; the reassembly key also needs channel/socket identity if IDs are unique only per channel reader. Specify those bounds and keying. [remote.md:290–324](docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md)\n\nThe lease sections distinguish expiry from channel loss and use per-author renewal media for attribution, without leasing stateless reflection state, buffers, or NAT mappings. That is coherent. However, reporting reclaimed resources as a DaoStream `not-found` outcome inherits the invalid-outcome problem above; use the protocol-level error envelope instead. [remote.md:439–502](docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md) [dao.lease.md:283–289](docs/design/dao.lease.md)\n\n## 4. MIDDLEWARE AND SEAM\n\nThe position-preserving handle wrapper, two attachment points, and distinction between a dropping filter and middleware fit the composed-handle contract. Pure transforms composed with explicit dependencies can respect the no-callback/no-hidden-global-state invariants. [middleware.md:11–75, 145–153](docs/design/dao.stream.middleware.md) [dao.stream.md:439–443](docs/design/dao.stream.md)\n\n**Blocking contradiction:** the prohibitions say transforms never loop over a handle, then permit gate to drain fact streams inside a call. Gate does exactly what the prohibition forbids. Worse, its state and cursors have no persistence mechanism: each call cannot both remain pure and advance a retained index. A gate that re-reads fixed cursors can repeatedly evaluate stale facts. [middleware.md:77–83, 96–116](docs/design/dao.stream.middleware.md)\n\nThe claimed ShiBi fit therefore does **not** pass as written. ShiBi is required to emerge from separate index and query interpreters over streams; the gate collapses fact draining/folding and request verification into a synchronous middleware function. Preserve the seam by having index/query interpreters publish an explicit decision or snapshot stream, and let middleware read an already-composed decision input under a defined nonblocking contract. Do not claim the current gate can drain and fold arbitrary streams in a total, immediate transform. [dao.shibi.md:7–16, 20–32](docs/design/dao.shibi.md) [middleware.md:99–116](docs/design/dao.stream.middleware.md)\n\nThe remote spec also describes whole-channel encryption but middleware only defines operation-map/handle wrapping. Value-level encryption does not hide values from a relay carrying the operation result; a channel-level transform would need an explicit attachment interface and ordering relative to codec/framing. Until then, qualify the encryption claim: metadata and values remain visible to the relay absent a separately specified channel wrapper. [remote.md:504–530](docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md) [middleware.md:11–56, 132–139](docs/design/dao.stream.middleware.md)\n\n## 5. UNIFICATION AND `dao.jing.remote`\n\nThe fate table covers the listed paths, and the proposed `dao.jing.content` module clearly marks its new functions and the move of `accept-bytes!`; the existing function is private in `dao.jing.remote`. The new coordinate form is also identified as a change from today’s URL form. [remote.md:565–642](docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md) [src/cljc/dao/jing/remote.cljc:35–42](src/cljc/dao/jing/remote.cljc) [src/cljc/dao/jing/coordinate.cljc:20–32](src/cljc/dao/jing/coordinate.cljc)\n\nI found no omitted path among the requested categories in the table. **Misclassification/claim to correct:** describing `dao.jing.content` and `dao.jing.dht` framing as “Base64-inside-Transit” is false for the new remote design’s CBOR option. Say that message boundaries come from the channel codec, with payload bytes represented as Base64 in the application value. [dao.jing.cbor.md:31–35, 433–438](docs/design/dao.jing.cbor.md) [remote.md:290–300, 615–620](docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md)\n\n`yin.repl.link-policy.md` exists, so the ShiBi stub’s reference to it resolves. [dao.shibi.md:34–37](docs/design/dao.shibi.md)\n\n## 6. COMPANION EDITS\n\nThe WebSocket, UCF, Dao Jing, B-tree, DHT, UDP, lease, linker, stack, and query changes generally point consumers at the new remote path and are framed as migration work. The CBOR framing statement above is the concrete false neighbour. One documentation inconsistency also remains: the remote file says contract impact is only three decisions, while its amendment inventory names more. [remote.md:24–27, 700–725](docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md)\n\nThe `dao.stream.md` “outlives the stream” wording also needs the `attach!` correction from §2. The required refusal outcome will need implementation support in the closed outcome sets before code consumes it. [dao.stream.md:546–552](docs/design/dao.stream.md) [src/cljc/dao/stream.cljc:67–93](src/cljc/dao/stream.cljc)\n\n## 7. LENGTH AND SIMPLICITY\n\nThe 725-line remote spec is over its stated target. Move the DAO Jing migration design (§8) into its implementation plan; move the completion criteria and companion-edit inventory (§9–10) into a migration checklist. Those sections are not wire or semantic rules. Consolidate repeated relay/reachability explanations across the invariant, NAT, pair, and lease sections. [remote.md:612–642, 644–725, 31–45, 326–395, 439–502](docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md)\n\nNo essential old-draft concern appears lost: the new draft retains NAT caveats, relay, buffer bounds, lease lifetime, and transport loss distinctions. [dao.stream.remote.md:31–45, 268–324, 326–395, 439–502](docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md)\n\n## 8. CITATIONS\n\nThe new docs do not provide literal `file:line` citations to audit; they use section/title references. Spot-checks against at least twelve referenced targets found the following accurate: stream payload and host-boundary principles in `datom.world.md`; descriptor/reader rules in `dao.stream.md`; WebSocket attachment identity and event deposits in `dao.stream.ws.md`; the 1200-byte DHT datagram bound in `dao.jing.dht.node.cljc`; lease carriage and lapse semantics in `dao.lease.md`; and the current private `accept-bytes!` and URL-only coordinate validation in the Dao Jing source. The CBOR document’s Transit-only framing claim is inaccurate for the new codec-neutral/CBOR remote channel, as noted above. [remote.md:102–107, 121–128, 290–300, 612–642](docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md) [src/cljc/dao/jing/dht/node.cljc:46–50](src/cljc/dao/jing/dht/node.cljc) [dao.lease.md:236–250, 283–289](docs/design/dao.lease.md)\n\n## VERDICT: REJECT\n\n### MUST-FIX\n\n1. `docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md:135–151, 186–189, 227–235` — Define protocol-level missing/gone/unsupported errors separately from DaoStream operation outcomes; fix surface-to-operation mapping and surface discovery. Current responses violate closed outcome sets and cannot provide the surface the reflection needs.\n2. `docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md:268–300` — Specify how WebSocket payload/event envelopes become channel request/answer values, including attachment correlation and lifecycle. Current channel and mirror types do not compose.\n3. `docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md:143–151` — Route anchor reads through declared reader surface and middleware, or remove the piggyback behavior.\n4. `docs/design/dao.stream.middleware.md:77–83, 96–116` — Resolve the no-drain prohibition versus gate’s draining behavior, and provide a sound explicit state/cursor progression model compatible with separate ShiBi index/query interpreters.\n5. `docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md:504–530` and `docs/design/dao.stream.middleware.md:11–56, 132–139` — Do not imply value middleware hides data from a relay. Specify channel-level encryption attachment and codec ordering, or state that remote encryption is deferred.\n6. `docs/design/dao.stream.md:546–552` — Correct the impossible “cursor … at attach!” wording.\n7. `docs/design/dao.jing.cbor.md:31–35, 433–438` — Correct the Transit-only framing claim for the new codec-neutral/CBOR path.\n8. `docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md:24–27, 700–706` — Reconcile the stated number/scope of contract amendments with the actual edits.\n\n### SHOULD-FIX\n\n1. `docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md:290–324` — Define whether the UDP datagram budget includes fragmentation metadata and include channel/socket identity in reassembly keying.\n2. `docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md:31–45, 348–395` — Describe NAT traversal by mapping/filtering behavior; state the reachable outbound WebSocket/relay requirement for UDP-blocked and browser peers.\n3. `docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md:612–642, 644–725` — Move migration detail and completion/checklist material out of the core protocol document and remove repeated NAT/relay explanation.\n4. `src/cljc/dao/stream.cljc:71–93` — Add the new `:dao.stream/refused` outcome to the implementation sets before implementing middleware that emits it."}}
